\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Sharply Higher As Manufacturing Data Allay Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 2,542 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 September 2010\par 00:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Vietnamese markets are closed Thursday for the National Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10269.47  +254.75  +2.54% \par Nasdaq     2176.84   +62.81  +2.97% \par S&P 500    1080.29   +30.96  +2.95% \par FTSE 100   5366.41  +141.19  +2.70% \par Xetra DAX  6083.90  +158.68  +2.68% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 23/32  -2/32 0.509%  +3.2BP \par 5Y Tsy    99 7/32 -12/32 1.411%  +8.0BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 13/32 -30/32 2.577% +10.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY       84.46-48     +0.44% \par Range         84.66-83.69 \par EUR/USD       1.2806-08    +1.04% \par Range         1.2855-1.2664 \par AUD/USD       0.9105-08    +2.19% \par Range         0.9113-0.8913 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   11.90/12.20% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   11.55/11.90% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   12.90/13.20% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,248.10 -$2.20 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,244.10-$1,256.60 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $73.91   +$1.99 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks rallied Wednesday, with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , Caterpillar and \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  leading broad gains in a strong start to September after manufacturing data topped expectations.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average advanced 254.75 points, or 2.54%, to 10269.47, marking its biggest one-day gain since July 7 and its fifth-largest one-day gain this year. All 30 of the measure's components rose, led by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , which jumped 76 cents, or 6.1%, to 13.21. Caterpillar was also strong, up 3, or 4.6%, to 68.16, and \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  added 1.38, or 3.8%, to 37.74.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite gained 62.81, or 2.97%, to 2176.84. The Standard & Poor's 500 index climbed 30.96, or 2.95%, to 1080.29, with all of its sectors ending the session higher. The industrial and financial sectors posted the biggest percentage increases.\par \par \uc2 The gains followed a report from the Institute for Supply Management showing the U.S. manufacturing sector unexpectedly rebounded in August, with employment rising strongly. The data came on the back of encouraging manufacturing numbers from China. The reports stood in great contrast to more downbeat numbers on the economy recently, and helped feed investors' thirst for a ray of hope following the market's worst August since 2001.\par \par \uc2 There were some weak spots in Wednesday's data. The Commerce Department reported U.S. construction spending declined in July to the lowest level in 10 years, and payroll giant \uc2 Automatic Data Processing\uc2  said private-sector jobs in the U.S. fell 10,000 during August. However, investors overlooked those reports as the manufacturing data seemingly put the market into a euphoria.\par \par \uc2 "Investors were looking for optimism, and they found it," said Jack Ablin, chief investment officer at Harris Private Bank. "They shrugged off ADP, but like it or not, investors are going to come face to face with jobs data at the end of the week. I think that's going to have a much bigger impact on the future direction of the market than what we're trifling with today."\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended lower Wednesday as a decline in property companies, after comments by the country's banking regulator on speculation in the sector, and profit-taking in small- and medium-sized firms outweighed gains posted by banks following encouraging data on manufacturing activity.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely test the psychologically important support level of 2600 in coming sessions as investors remain concerned about rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 "The higher PMI is boosting confidence in the pace of economic growth and driving gains in heavyweights," said Zhang Xiang, an analyst at \uc2 Guodu Securities\uc2 . However, further upside may be limited as moderating exports and fixed-asset investment point to an overall slowdown, Zhang said.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Wednesday as bellwether exporters rebounded on brisk economic data from China and a pledge by Democratic Party of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  presidential candidate Ichiro Ozawa to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to curb the yen's steep rise if elected party leader.\par \par \uc2 "There have been widespread doubts over whether the current government can really carry out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities. "The specific mention of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (by Ozawa) had an impact," he added, though noting market reaction will be short-lived.\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures settled higher in U.S. trading Wednesday, helped by improving data on U.S. manufacturing and rising equities markets that trumped fears about increasing oil supplies.\par \par \uc2 The manufacturing data dulled some of the safe-haven shine enjoyed by gold, with gold futures finishing slightly lower after earlier reaching fresh two-month highs.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed higher on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday on the back of stronger equities and the U.S. manufacturing data.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar slumped against most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Wednesday after positive global economic data encouraged investors to dip into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closely tied to global growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Better-than-expected U.S. manufacturing figures added fuel to the rally in higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , helping the Australian dollar push more than 2% higher against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , while the euro racked up a gain of nearly 1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The brightening economic picture painted by the data diminishes the attraction of the dollar as a safe harbor and encourages investors to seek riskier assets that promise better yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After a recent stream of weakening U.S. data, including a worse-than-expected U.S. jobs report released Wednesday, the positive surprise in U.S. manufacturing could mean the U.S. economic picture is "not as gloomy as had been feared," said Jane Foley, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London, helping the dollar to rebound against the yen and giving riskier assets room to climb even higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, investors aren't likely to plow too much further into higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Foley said, ahead of Friday's U.S. non-farm payrolls report, considered a key gauge of an economy many have feared is slipping.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given the gloomy backdrop that we still have, it's still quite possible to argue that the rally we've seen [Wednesday] in the risky assets will need to see something more significant," such as an upside surprise to Friday's payrolls numbers, Foley said, to keep going.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday in New York, the euro was at $1.2800 from $1.2673. The dollar was at Y84.47 from Y83.83, while the euro was at Y108.11 from Y106.25. The U.K. pound was at $1.5445 from $1.5346. The dollar was at CHF1.0158 from CHF1.0147.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 82.510 from 83.104.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. manufacturing sector unexpectedly rebounded in August, with employment rising strongly, according to data released Wednesday by the Institute for Supply Management.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's not in isolation in and of itself enough to change one's view of the world," said Ronald Leven, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, of the U.S. manufacturing report. "It's still part of a mixed bunch of data, but it definitely helps," bring investors to the higher-yielding party.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. manufacturing report added to momentum triggered overnight by figures showing China's manufacturing activity expanded in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia also reported economic growth was at its fastest quarterly pace in three years in the second quarter of 2010 as a recovery in household spending and a mining boom spurred activity, boosting expectations the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will need to raise interest rates again, potentially before the end of the year, sending the Australian dollar soaring to $0.9087 from $0.8904 late Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro also gained sharply against the dollar, jumping about 1% after euro-zone manufacturing PMI remained in expansionary territory.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar had ticked down against the yen after a U.S. jobs report earlier Wednesday missed expectations, but reversed its losses against the yen after the positive U.S. manufacturing report.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to remain in a tight range against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after Ichiro Ozawa, a power-broker in the ruling Democratic Party of Japan who will challenge Prime Minister Naoto Kan for party leadership, said he advocated market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem yen strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We will boldly take all measures, including market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , to counter the yen's steep gains down the track," Ozawa said in a statement on his policy goals, Kyodo News reported. The remarks contrasted with Kan's less direct rhetoric against yen strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys suffered broad-based losses Wednesday as the latest batch of global data eased worries about the economic outlook, sapping demand for safe assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1954 GMT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was 30/32 lower and its yield was up about 11 basis points to 2.577%. The 30-year bond was 2 13/32 lower and its yield was up about 13 basis points to 3.647%. The two-year note was down 2/32 to yield 0.509%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Selling was driven mainly by better-than-forecast increases in the manufacturing sector for both China and the U.S., the two main engines of the global economy. The data came as a relief for many investors after recent data, especially from the U.S., had raised worries that the economic recovery could lose traction in the second half.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Treasury market is eating up strong Chinese and American growth indicators. Suddenly a double-dip recession and deflation scenarios don't seem quite so likely," said Eric Lascelles, chief economics and rates strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 TD Securities\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year and 30-year Treasurys were the worst performers as bond investors brace for new government debt supply next week. The Treasury Department will release the size of next week's supply in Treasurys maturing in three, 10 and 30 years Thursday. [ 01-09-10 2206GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Bond prices had reached elevated levels on heavy buying for month-end and there was certainly plenty of room for a correction," said Richard Bryant, head of Treasury trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York. "It would not be surprising to see Treasurys remain under pressure in the near-term, particularly into long-end supply."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The data also cut speculation that the Federal Reserve may need to conduct larger-scale Treasury purchases as a way to support the economy, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Wednesday after firm demand at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 20-year bond sale.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The issue result consolidated investors' confidence in holding long-term debt despite data showing the manufacturing sector rebounded last month," a Guotai Junan Securities trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expectations that the PBOC will continue to keep bill yields flat in regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are also helping support bond prices, the trader noted.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is expected to move in a 126.60-126.70 range Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds fell Wednesday on the possibility that a powerful lawmaker who advocates a sharp increase in government spending may win a vote to head the nation's ruling party, and therefore, be poised to become prime minister.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Known as an advocate of fiscal expansion to fight the weak economy, Ichiro Ozawa's candidacy is fueling predictions he would push through increased social spending that would require a sharp rise in government bond the issuance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Anxiety over fiscal health may intensify due to the DPJ's election, especially because Japan's budget conditions are already difficult to handle," said Tetsuya Miura, chief market analyst at Mizuho Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead, yields are likely to keep rising, at least for another two weeks, said Deutsche Securities strategist Makoto Yamashita.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I am personally bullish on JGBs ahead, but even so, I have to say yields will likely keep rising until the DPJ race ends," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS Securities Japan rates strategist RuiXue Xu said investors shouldn't expect JGB yields to keep rising even after the political event. "If you look around, economic fundamentals around the world haven't changed so much and the yen is still strong while stocks are falling," she said. "I expect longer-term demand for the assets to remain."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Thursday, Malaysian exports are expected to have risen 12.1% on the year in July compared with a 17.2% rise in June, according to the median forecast of 13 economists in a Dow Jones poll.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The continued moderation in growth rate should be quite marked in July to reinforce our view that Malaysia's export growth peaked in the first quarter and we are heading for a less rosy second half," Standard Chartered economist Alvin Liew said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par Apple Unveils Revamped AppleTV, Ipod Lineup \par US Construction Down In July; ISM Up In August \par Ex-fund Manager Faces Insider Charges \par US August Auto Sales Plunge \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Burger King\f28 \uc2 \f2  Is In Talks Over Possible Sale \par Defense Secretary Gates Says US War In Iraq Is Over \par Panel Members Spar Over Lehman's Collapse \par US Mortgage Application Volumes Up In Week \par China's 'Indigenous Innovation' Policy Rankles US \par WSJ: China Auto Sales Grow 56% On Yr In Aug - Report \par =WSJ: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sinochem\f28 \uc2 \f2  Hires \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  To Advise On Choices Regarding \f28 \uc2 \f2 Potash Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2  - Source \par =