\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Defends BOJ Step; Threatens  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 533 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2010\par 06:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from finance minister on the BOJ; background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister defended the Bank of Japan Tuesday from criticism that its latest policy step was too modest to halt the yen's rise, suggesting the minister may refrain from demanding more monetary easing for some time.\par \par \uc2 But in a sign the government still sees the strong yen as a major problem, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda voiced displeasure about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's persistent rise, again hinting at the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if necessary.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "acted properly and quickly in response to recent changes in the financial and economic conditions, and I value that highly," Noda said at a news conference. "I expect that the BOJ's monetary policy step, combined with the government's new countermeasures, will begin to show an impact from now on."\par \par \uc2 At a separate news conference, Economy Minister Satoshi Arai also noted that he thinks "very highly" of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's quickness.\par \par \uc2 Their assessment comes despite the failure of the Bank of Japan's fresh easing measure, announced Monday, aimed at dissuading investors from buying the yen.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Naoto Kan also seemed satisfied with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's action. The administration's tone suggests it may suspend its campaign of pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for a while to do more to revitalize the export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2 The economy has suffered as overseas growth has moderated and the effects of domestic stimulus measures have waned.\par \par \uc2 At an emergency meeting Monday, the BOJ's policy board voted to offer domestic financial institutions Y10 trillion of six-month loans at an ultralow rate of 0.1%. This was in addition to the Y20 trillion in three-month loans it had been offering.\par \par \uc2 The same day, the government outlined a relatively small economic stimulus package worth Y920 billion, partly to showcase its unity with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its efforts to keep Japan's economic recovery from faltering.\par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen about Y1.5, or 1.7%, to Y85.35 since the BOJ made public its latest approach.\par \par \uc2 The government, meanwhile, continued its efforts to slow the yen's rise through public statements.\par \par \uc2 "Current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are one-sided," said Noda, who has the authority to order the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell or buy the yen. "As I've been saying, my fundamental stance is that excessive fluctuations or disorderly movements in exchange rates have a negative effect on the stability of economies and financial markets. Based on this stance, we will monitor the market with great interest, and we will take decisive steps when that becomes necessary."\par \par \uc2 But the yen moved little in reaction to Noda's remarks, even though the phrase "decisive steps" has been used often by past governments to signal imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The absence of market reaction could mean that, after weeks without actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite Noda's escalating warnings, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders are beginning to doubt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's readiness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com [ 31-08-10 0432GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100831e68v0008y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
