\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Lead JGB Futures Could Fall To 142.50 - Analyst\b0\par\par\uc2 1,679 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2010\par 01:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead September JGB futures tipped down in 142.50-142.90 range vs Tuesday's close at 142.99, says Jun Ishii, chief fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. With attention on MOF's Y2.2 trillion offering of 10-year cash bonds later, says "caution toward the 10-year auction will unavoidably be higher with some nervousness given the reaction to the sudden fall" recently in yields. Tips 10-year yield in 0.970%-1.020% range vs Tuesday's 0.975%. Says another factor prompting unease over auction, possibly pushing yields higher is concern that DPJ power-broker and spending-advocate Ozawa's bid to oust more fiscally-conservative PM Kan could fuel supply-side worries.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] South Korea's consumer prices rise for second straight month in August from July, but slower than market consensus, possibly giving leeway to BOK not to immediately resume monetary tightening next week amid increased risk of slowdown in global economic recovery. August CPI +0.3% on month, same pace as July, but falling short of +0.4% forecast in Dow Jones poll; on year, CPI +2.6%, also same as July, tad lower than +2.7% forecast. Weaker-than-expected inflation readings likely to lead more market participants to look to October for BOK's next rate hike, though many still believe BOK will move preemptively next week to rein in rising inflation expectations as economy comes back to robust growth this year. (kanga.kong@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 3.2 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 2nd day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 16.3 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 13.1 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold early in Sydney $1,247.30/oz, down 50 cents this morning, a tidy overnight gain from intraday low of $1,232 attributed to safe haven buying. \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says September often a bullish month for gold with price rising in 21 of past 30 years. More recently, during current "great" bull run, gold up 9 in 11 years, with September often when price rallied out of long-standing ranges, signaling resumption of secular trend, it says. Technically, \uc2 Barclays\uc2  says that after close above previous $1,242/$1,245 barrier, "we are inclined to expect further near-term gains for gold in September, given the strong seasonal bias associated with the month during the past 13 years." While nothing guaranteed, strong close overnight suggests further gains "very likely." Consolidation this week above $1,229 bullish with high at $1,265 the primary target ahead of $1,280, then $1,300.(ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to trade narrowly between 8750-8900 vs 8824.06 yesterday close(down 3.6%). While distinct possibility index could test year-low of 8807.41, analysts say further selling likely to be limited by better-than-expected U.S. consumer confidence and housing data, and buying by Japanese pension funds. "The Nikkei may test below 8800 (if USD/JPY falls below 84), but expectations for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely provide support at that level," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Real estate shares likely to outperform market following positive housing starts data in Japan and BOJ's easing measures, while chipmakers such as \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  (8035.TO) expected to continue falling on overnight decline in Intel (INTC) shares. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 8830 vs Osaka close at 8800.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2315 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may drop to 83.70 as investor sentiment remains risk-averse after FOMC minutes overnight highlighted slowdown of U.S. economic recovery, ways Fed can bolster growth, says Minoru Shioiri, senior dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Says EUR/JPY may fall in tandem to 106.00. Markets focusing on U.S. ISM manufacturing PMI for August due 1400 GMT -- reading likely 52.5 vs 55.5 in July, shows Dow Jones poll of economists -- if worse than expected, market sentiment would shift further toward risk-off, sending investors into safe-heaven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as JPY. EUR/USD may trade in 1.2620-1.2720 range vs last 1.2688. USD/JPY may trade in 83.70-84.60 band vs last 84.15. EUR/JPY may trade in 106.00-107.20 range vs last 106.63.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] BIS's triennial \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey shows USD/JPY's average daily turnover rises to $568 billion in April 2010; that compares with $328 billion in April 2004, year when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Nearly-doubled market volumes suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF may find it very difficult stopping JPY's rise via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What's more, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF official recently told Dow Jones that if it steps into market, amount of JPY-selling would be much less than in past campaigns. BIS survey shows activity by other financial institutions including hedge funds up by 42% in 2010 survey from 2007 report, suggesting if Japan steps into market, they could have to contend with many more short-term investors taking on fresh JPY-long positions on view U.S. economic growth momentum to weaken, JPY to rise. Pair last 84.17, Tokyo dealers says pair may fall below 84.00 again in Asian trade.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia likely to post trade surplus of $850 million in July, Dow Jones Newswires' poll of 10 economists shows; not much bigger than $580 million surplus in June as imports growth continues to outpace exports growth on back of higher domestic demand and stronger IDR. Historically, country's trade surplus usually come between $1.5-$2.0 billion, but trend has changed in recent months. "Compared to export growth, import growth should still be higher, mainly driven by domestic demand and the relative strength of the rupiah," says Bank Danamon economist Anton Gunawan. Statistics agency will release trade data around 0430 GMT Wednesday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's inflation likely to accelerate to its highest level in 16 months in August, but Bank Indonesia not expected to start tightening yet; median forecast of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones tip CPI to rise 6.7% on-year vs 6.22% in July due to rising food prices during Ramadan fasting month and impact of 10% electricity tariff introduced in July; official data due around 0400 GMT Wednesday; all of economists polled tip \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its overnight policy rate unchanged at 6.50% when it meets Friday. But inflation, especially if it turns out to be higher than consensus, will raise urgency for rate hike later this year despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inclination to keep rates unchanged for as long as it can. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Thai inflation likely relatively stable in August, though rising food prices to continue applying upward pressure. CPI likely +3.2% on-year vs +3.4% in July, according to median forecast of 9 economists in Dow Jones poll. On-month, CPI expected +0.25% vs +0.16% in July; core CPI likely +1.3% on-year, +0.1% on-month, according to median projection of 8 economists. "Price pressures will remain on the upward bias, paving the way for the BOT to hike another 25 bps in the next Monetary Policy Committee meeting in October," says Standard Chartered bank senior economist Usara Wilaipich. Most economists tip rate hike in October, though view more mixed on whether it will move again at this year's final meeting in December. August CPI data due later. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures slip as market awaits weekly U.S. inventory data from DOE Wednesday, and after API releases own data showing much larger-than-expected build in oil inventories; API says crude stockpiles grew by 4.8 million bbls, more than 5 times analysts' expectations; API says gasoline inventories fell by 600,000 bbls, 3 times expected level; API says distillate inventories fell by 1.9 million bbls vs expectations for 1.1 million-bbl build. "Bearish for crude, but bullish for heating oil," says Citi analyst Tim Evans. October crude recently 16 cents down at $71.76/bbl. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2248 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional markets likely to drift with mostly flat Wall Street performance; investors will look to stock-specific cues, also cautious ahead of China PMIs today, U.S. data, particularly jobs numbers Friday. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, look to equities for cues; EUR/USD at 1.2685 vs 1.2673 late NY, EUR/JPY at 106.74 vs 106.25, USD/JPY at 84.15 vs 84.83. On data slate: Korea CPI at 2300 GMT, Australia PMI at 2330 GMT, Korea manufacturing PMI at 0000 GMT, CFLP China PMI at 0100 GMT, Korea trade at 0100 GMT, Australia GDP at 0130 GMT, Taiwan manufacturing PMI at 0200 GMT, China \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at 0230 GMT, Indonesia CPI, trade balance at 0400 GMT, India manufacturing PMI at 0500 GMT, Thailand CPI at 0700 GMT, Japan's ruling party begins campaigning for leadership election. Later there's EU PMI, UK CIPS manufacturing PMI, U.S. MBA mortgage applications, ADP employment report, ISM manufacturing PMI, construction spending, EIA oil report, Fed governor Duke speaks, Dallas Fed president Fisher speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com) [ 08-31-10 1923ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100831e68v000da\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
