nes.com)\par \par \uc2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures slip as market awaits weekly U.S. inventory data from DOE Wednesday, and after API releases own data showing much larger-than-expected build in oil inventories; API says crude stockpiles grew by 4.8 million bbls, more than 5 times analysts' expectations; API says gasoline inventories fell by 600,000 bbls, 3 times expected level; API says distillate inventories fell by 1.9 million bbls vs expectations for 1.1 million-bbl build. "Bearish for crude, but bullish for heating oil," says Citi analyst Tim Evans. October crude recently 16 cents down at $71.76/bbl. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2248 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional markets likely to drift with mostly flat Wall Street performance; investors will look to stock-specific cues, also cautious ahead of China PMIs today, U.S. data, particularly jobs numbers Friday. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, look to equities for cues; EUR/USD at 1.2685 vs 1.2673 late NY, EUR/JPY at 106.74 vs 106.25, USD/JPY at 84.15 vs 84.83. On data slate: Korea CPI at 2300 GMT, Australia PMI at 2330 GMT, Korea manufacturing PMI at 0000 GMT, CFLP China PMI at 0100 GMT, Korea trade at 0100 GMT, Australia GDP at 0130 GMT, Taiwan manufacturing PMI at 0200 GMT, China \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at 0230 GMT, Indonesia CPI, trade balance at 0400 GMT, India manufacturing PMI at 0500 GMT, Thailand CPI at 0700 GMT, Japan's ruling party begins campaigning for leadership election. Later there's EU PMI, UK CIPS manufacturing PMI, U.S. MBA mortgage applications, ADP employment report, ISM manufacturing PMI, construction spending, EIA oil report, Fed governor Duke speaks, Dallas Fed president Fisher speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2243 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD is vulnerable to soft reading of Aussie 2Q GDP, says RBC, with market experts expecting a strong reading of 0.9% quarter-on-quarter growth. But if data match or even beat, AUD/USD likely won't surge as wave of global data--including China PMI, U.S. jobs--later in week weigh on risk. Another AUD cross, however, could get a lift on a robust 2Q Aussie GDP: "If data surprise to the upside, keep an eye on AUD/NZD, which broke through a key medium-term resistance level at 1.2726, highlighting resistance at 1.2801," says Matthew Strauss, senior fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with RBC. AUD/NZD recently at 1.2759, up from morning low of 1.2733. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com) [ 08-31-10 1923ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100831e68v000d9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
