\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei Tipped Dn; May Test Yr-Low, Yen Key Focus\b0\par\par\uc2 1,841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2010\par 01:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2319 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to trade narrowly between 8750-8900 vs 8824.06 yesterday close(down 3.6%). While distinct possibility index could test year-low of 8807.41, analysts say further selling likely to be limited by better-than-expected U.S. consumer confidence and housing data, and buying by Japanese pension funds. "The Nikkei may test below 8800 (if USD/JPY falls below 84), but expectations for a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would likely provide support at that level," says Kazuhiro Takahashi, general manager at Daiwa Securities Capital Markets. Real estate shares likely to outperform market following positive housing starts data in Japan and BOJ's easing measures, while chipmakers such as \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tokyo Electron\f28 \uc2 \f2  (8035.TO) expected to continue falling on overnight decline in Intel (INTC) shares. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 8830 vs Osaka close at 8800.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2315 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may drop to 83.70 as investor sentiment remains risk-averse after FOMC minutes overnight highlighted slowdown of U.S. economic recovery, ways Fed can bolster growth, says Minoru Shioiri, senior dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Says EUR/JPY may fall in tandem to 106.00. Markets focusing on U.S. ISM manufacturing PMI for August due 1400 GMT -- reading likely 52.5 vs 55.5 in July, shows Dow Jones poll of economists -- if worse than expected, market sentiment would shift further toward risk-off, sending investors into safe-heaven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as JPY. EUR/USD may trade in 1.2620-1.2720 range vs last 1.2688. USD/JPY may trade in 83.70-84.60 band vs last 84.15. EUR/JPY may trade in 106.00-107.20 range vs last 106.63.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] BIS's triennial \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey shows USD/JPY's average daily turnover rises to $568 billion in April 2010; that compares with $328 billion in April 2004, year when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Nearly-doubled market volumes suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF may find it very difficult stopping JPY's rise via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What's more, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF official recently told Dow Jones that if it steps into market, amount of JPY-selling would be much less than in past campaigns. BIS survey shows activity by other financial institutions including hedge funds up by 42% in 2010 survey from 2007 report, suggesting if Japan steps into market, they could have to contend with many more short-term investors taking on fresh JPY-long positions on view U.S. economic growth momentum to weaken, JPY to rise. Pair last 84.17, Tokyo dealers says pair may fall below 84.00 again in Asian trade.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia likely to post trade surplus of $850 million in July, Dow Jones Newswires' poll of 10 economists shows; not much bigger than $580 million surplus in June as imports growth continues to outpace exports growth on back of higher domestic demand and stronger IDR. Historically, country's trade surplus usually come between $1.5-$2.0 billion, but trend has changed in recent months. "Compared to export growth, import growth should still be higher, mainly driven by domestic demand and the relative strength of the rupiah," says Bank Danamon economist Anton Gunawan. Statistics agency will release trade data around 0430 GMT Wednesday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's inflation likely to accelerate to its highest level in 16 months in August, but Bank Indonesia not expected to start tightening yet; median forecast of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones tip CPI to rise 6.7% on-year vs 6.22% in July due to rising food prices during Ramadan fasting month and impact of 10% electricity tariff introduced in July; official data due around 0400 GMT Wednesday; all of economists polled tip \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its overnight policy rate unchanged at 6.50% when it meets Friday. But inflation, especially if it turns out to be higher than consensus, will raise urgency for rate hike later this year despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inclination to keep rates unchanged for as long as it can. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Thai inflation likely relatively stable in August, though rising food prices to continue applying upward pressure. CPI likely +3.2% on-year vs +3.4% in July, according to median forecast of 9 economists in Dow Jones poll. On-month, CPI expected +0.25% vs +0.16% in July; core CPI likely +1.3% on-year, +0.1% on-month, according to median projection of 8 economists. "Price pressures will remain on the upward bias, paving the way for the BOT to hike another 25 bps in the next Monetary Policy Committee meeting in October," says Standard Chartered bank senior economist Usara Wilaipich. Most economists tip rate hike in October, though view more mixed on whether it will move again at this year's final meeting in December. August CPI data due later. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures slip as market awaits weekly