\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY May Fall To 83.70; Mkts Still Risk-Averse\b0\par\par\uc2 1,970 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 1 September 2010\par 01:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2315 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may drop to 83.70 as investor sentiment remains risk-averse after FOMC minutes overnight highlighted slowdown of U.S. economic recovery, ways Fed can bolster growth, says Minoru Shioiri, senior dealer at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Says EUR/JPY may fall in tandem to 106.00. Markets focusing on U.S. ISM manufacturing PMI for August due 1400 GMT -- reading likely 52.5 vs 55.5 in July, shows Dow Jones poll of economists -- if worse than expected, market sentiment would shift further toward risk-off, sending investors into safe-heaven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  such as JPY. EUR/USD may trade in 1.2620-1.2720 range vs last 1.2688. USD/JPY may trade in 83.70-84.60 band vs last 84.15. EUR/JPY may trade in 106.00-107.20 range vs last 106.63.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2310 GMT [Dow Jones] BIS's triennial \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  survey shows USD/JPY's average daily turnover rises to $568 billion in April 2010; that compares with $328 billion in April 2004, year when \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Nearly-doubled market volumes suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF may find it very difficult stopping JPY's rise via \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . What's more, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF official recently told Dow Jones that if it steps into market, amount of JPY-selling would be much less than in past campaigns. BIS survey shows activity by other financial institutions including hedge funds up by 42% in 2010 survey from 2007 report, suggesting if Japan steps into market, they could have to contend with many more short-term investors taking on fresh JPY-long positions on view U.S. economic growth momentum to weaken, JPY to rise. Pair last 84.17, Tokyo dealers says pair may fall below 84.00 again in Asian trade.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia likely to post trade surplus of $850 million in July, Dow Jones Newswires' poll of 10 economists shows; not much bigger than $580 million surplus in June as imports growth continues to outpace exports growth on back of higher domestic demand and stronger IDR. Historically, country's trade surplus usually come between $1.5-$2.0 billion, but trend has changed in recent months. "Compared to export growth, import growth should still be higher, mainly driven by domestic demand and the relative strength of the rupiah," says Bank Danamon economist Anton Gunawan. Statistics agency will release trade data around 0430 GMT Wednesday. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Indonesia's inflation likely to accelerate to its highest level in 16 months in August, but Bank Indonesia not expected to start tightening yet; median forecast of 10 regional economists polled by Dow Jones tip CPI to rise 6.7% on-year vs 6.22% in July due to rising food prices during Ramadan fasting month and impact of 10% electricity tariff introduced in July; official data due around 0400 GMT Wednesday; all of economists polled tip \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its overnight policy rate unchanged at 6.50% when it meets Friday. But inflation, especially if it turns out to be higher than consensus, will raise urgency for rate hike later this year despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's inclination to keep rates unchanged for as long as it can. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Thai inflation likely relatively stable in August, though rising food prices to continue applying upward pressure. CPI likely +3.2% on-year vs +3.4% in July, according to median forecast of 9 economists in Dow Jones poll. On-month, CPI expected +0.25% vs +0.16% in July; core CPI likely +1.3% on-year, +0.1% on-month, according to median projection of 8 economists. "Price pressures will remain on the upward bias, paving the way for the BOT to hike another 25 bps in the next Monetary Policy Committee meeting in October," says Standard Chartered bank senior economist Usara Wilaipich. Most economists tip rate hike in October, though view more mixed on whether it will move again at this year's final meeting in December. August CPI data due later. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2249 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures slip as market awaits weekly U.S. inventory data from DOE Wednesday, and after API releases own data showing much larger-than-expected build in oil inventories; API says crude stockpiles grew by 4.8 million bbls, more than 5 times analysts' expectations; API says gasoline inventories fell by 600,000 bbls, 3 times expected level; API says distillate inventories fell by 1.9 million bbls vs expectations for 1.1 million-bbl build. "Bearish for crude, but bullish for heating oil," says Citi analyst Tim Evans. October crude recently 16 cents down at $71.76/bbl. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2248 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional markets likely to drift with mostly flat Wall Street performance; investors will look to stock-specific cues, also cautious ahead of China PMIs today, U.S. data, particularly jobs numbers Friday. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  majors likely in tight ranges, look to equities for cues; EUR/USD at 1.2685 vs 1.2673 late NY, EUR/JPY at 106.74 vs 106.25, USD/JPY at 84.15 vs 84.83. On data slate: Korea CPI at 2300 GMT, Australia PMI at 2330 GMT, Korea manufacturing PMI at 0000 GMT, CFLP China PMI at 0100 GMT, Korea trade at 0100 GMT, Australia GDP at 0130 GMT, Taiwan manufacturing PMI at 0200 GMT, China \uc2 HSBC\uc2  PMI at 0230 GMT, Indonesia CPI, trade balance at 0400 GMT, India manufacturing PMI at 0500 GMT, Thailand CPI at 0700 GMT, Japan's ruling party begins campaigning for leadership election. Later there's EU PMI, UK CIPS manufacturing PMI, U.S. MBA mortgage applications, ADP employment report, ISM manufacturing PMI, construction spending, EIA oil report, Fed governor Duke speaks, Dallas Fed president Fisher speaks. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2243 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD is vulnerable to soft reading of Aussie 2Q GDP, says RBC, with market experts expecting a strong reading of 0.9% quarter-on-quarter growth. But if data match or even beat, AUD/USD likely won't surge as wave of global data--including China PMI, U.S. jobs--later in week weigh on risk. Another AUD cross, however, could get a lift on a robust 2Q Aussie GDP: "If data surprise to the upside, keep an eye on AUD/NZD, which broke through a key medium-term resistance level at 1.2726, highlighting resistance at 1.2801," says Matthew Strauss, senior fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with RBC. AUD/NZD recently at 1.2759, up from morning low of 1.2733. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2240 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices to fall today after U.S. wheat futures overnight snapped a 3-session winning streak as traders booked profits and pre-placed sell orders accelerated a dramatic sell-off. Month-end profit-taking dragged down prices in late dealings. Nearby September wheat down 18.75 cents, or 2.8%, at US$6.5250/bushel on Chicago Board of Trade, down 22% from its recent high but up 53% from a 9-month low in June. AUD/USD latest 0.8925, rallying from 0.8860 yesterday evening, will likely support Australian wheat prices, having softened steadily from 0.9025 early yesterday. On Tuesday, most active ASX milling wheat deliverable January in NSW eased A$1.50 to A$291.00/metric ton; ASX milling wheat deliverable January in WA down A$3.30 at A$309.50. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2239 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD tipping higher early, largely on 'buy on rumor, sell on news' ahead of Aussie 2Q GDP at 0130 GMT, though strategists at NAB warn move may not last. "Any boost to the AUD from this morning's Australian GDP report for 2Q could prove short-lived unless economic reports improve sentiment towards the global economic outlook in the major economies," says NAB \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist John Kyriakopoulos, noting today's China PMI data is key. AUD/USD recently at 0.8932, up from early morning trade around 0.8902, with NAB tipping support at 0.8860 and then 0.8815. NAB says resistance is at 0.8980 to 0.9000. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2232 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold early in Sydney bid $1,248.40/oz, up 70 cents early this morning in Asia. Gold futures in NY rose overnight on continued insecurity about economic recovery as global equity markets struggled. Uncertainty in other markets recently has increased demand for safe havens, and near-record low yields on U.S. government debt have enhanced gold's appeal among investors seeking a refuge. "It's a September rally starting early," says Jimmy Tintle, analyst with Transworld Futures in Tampa. "We're back to fear and apprehension" in the wider economy, says George Gero, vice president with \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  Global Futures in New York. LME base metals mostly fell yesterday, but copper pared its losses after fresh U.S. data showing a rise in consumer confidence helped to ease concerns over global recovery. CMC Markets analyst Michael Hewson says this news helped markets regain "some of their poise." LME 3-month copper settled $18 lower vs Monday PM kerb at $7,440/metric ton, while zinc fell $28 to $2,064, lead $19 to $2,069, nickel $355 to $20,695, and tin $640 to $21,000, but aluminum up 50 cents at $2,057.50. Of other precious metals, silver down 8 cents from late NY at $19.32/oz, platinum unchanged at $1,521, palladium up $2 at $502. Nymex October crude futures fell $2.78 overnight to $71.92/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 08-31-10 1915ET ]\par \par \uc2 2211 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD fell against JPY, dropped to lowest level against CHF as investors continued to worry about pace of U.S. recovery. Minutes from Fed's rate-setting committee offered no surprises to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, but put spotlight on slowing U.S. economy, how Fed officials might kickstart growth, which has weighed on investor sentiment. Investors will now turn to key U.S. data, such as nonfarm payrolls report. EUR/USD at 1.2673 late Tuesday vs 1.2663 late Monday, USD/JPY at 83.83 vs 84.55, EUR/JPY at 106.25 vs 107.09, GBP/USD at 1.5346 vs 1.5459, USD/CHF at 1.0147 vs 1.0261. USD Index at 83.104 from 83.160. Stocks mixed in conclusion of bruising month for market as steady trail of disappointing economic data stoked fears economy could be headed toward double dip. Agribusiness giant \uc2 Monsanto\uc2  dropped 5.8% now expects FY earnings to come in at the low end of its prior view. Dow +0.1%, Nasdaq declined 0.3%, Philly semicons down 1.9%. Treasury prices rose as market benefited from month-end demand, lingering worries about strength of economic recovery; 2-year yields down 2.0 bps to 0.481%, 10-year yields down 5.6 bps to 2.474%. Gold futures rose on continued insecurity about economic recovery. December Comex gold settled up $11.10 or $1,250.30/oz. Crude-oil futures tumbled as investors turned toward increasingly high U.S. oil supplies, slowing economy. October Nymex crude settled $2.78 lower, at $71.92/bbl. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 08-31-10 1915ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par