dollar-yuan pair today." Adds, traders wondering how high PBOC willing to set fixing amid recent USD strength. "We would like to see if it's possible for the fixing to rise above levels before June 19," he says. Fixing was at 6.8275 on June 18. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped higher on renewed concerns over pace of global economic recovery following U.S. data showing slow income growth; pair may initially head to 45.30 after settling at 45.20 late Friday (Manila markets shut for holiday yesterday); support 45.00, resistance 45.50. Manila-based trader says inflows during extended weekend may limit USD/PHP gain today. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY drops to 106.80 vs around 107.14 early in Asia as EUR/CHF marks fresh all-time low at 1.2935; speculators selling EUR due to risk aversion amid sharply weaker Nikkei, EUR/JPY stop loss orders may also have been triggered, says senior dealer at major Japan bank. Says cross may drop to 105.50 vs last 106.84. Says markets focused on economic indicators later in global day, including German jobless claims at 0800 GMT, Euro zone CPI at 0900 GMT, U.S. Case-Shiller home price index at 1300 GMT, ISM Chicago business survey due 1345 GMT. If indicators turn out to be worse than expected, market sentiment will likely shift further toward risk-off, sending investors to safe-heaven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as JPY, CHF. EUR/USD drops to 1.2632 vs last 1.2639, 1.2666 earlier. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0057 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to face downward pressure if Tokyo decides to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to push down JPY, because action would reverse recent downtrend in USD/JPY, Tokyo shares, and because that may increase oversupply concerns as government will need to issue more discount bills to fund any foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities strategist Katsutoshi Inadome; brokerage expects government to directly step into \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, only if a USD/JPY breach below 80 becomes possible (pair last around 84.52). Lead September JGB futures currently +0.24 at 142.69. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 down 0.7% at 4421.7 after hitting 4413.7 amid broad-based retreat following 1.5% decline in the S&P 500, as U.S. economic data continued to disappoint. Australian equities market is showing signs of nervousness, with cyclicals generally weak and defensives outperforming. Banks are down 0.4%-1.6% and Macquarie (MQG.AU) is down 1.2%, while in resources, BHP (BHP.AU) is down 1.6% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) is down 1.4%. Among defensives \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 0.5%, CSL (CSL.AU) up 0.5% and Foster's (FGL.AU) up 0.5%. But S&P/ASX 200 looks to be finding support below the 50-day moving average at 4427 and may regain this level if domestic funds support the market before month end. "Our market looks pretty good," says \uc2 UBS\uc2  Head of Sales George Kanaan. "The reporting season was reasonably good. It's just this volatile U.S. market that is pushing us around but it was very thin overnight. I don't expect Asian markets to go too badly today." Expects optimism to return if Wednesday's China PMI and U.S. ISM data show signs of improvement. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.6% at 1749.09, weighed by continued worries about global economic growth that pushed U.S. markets lower. \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2  analyst Bae Sung-young says market likely supported around 1742 levels for day, to trade in 1710-1770 band for week; "The Bernanke factor wore off in the U.S. after a day, and generally the economic data coming out this week, including U.S. August non-farm payrolls report and ISM manufacturing report, won't look very good. Unless there are definitive new measures to support growth the market's going to remain weak." Notes banks, which posted sharp gains Monday, and tech plays considered vulnerable to weaker 2H10 global growth. KB Financial (105560.SE) off 1.9% at KRW48,550, \uc2 Woori Finance\uc2  (053000.SE) 1.8% lower at KRW13,500. Hynix (000660.SE) off 2.9% at KRW21,800. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW rising towards psychological 1,200 in early trade as concerns over pace of global economic recovery continue to weigh on risk-sensitive \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like KRW, EUR, traders say. If pair rises to as high as that level, "it would be the fourth time (this month) that exporters deal with KRW1,200 level," says local bank trader. Adds, it's going to be interesting to see whether pair could test 1,200 resistance successfully today but this unlikely scenario if exporters sell as actively as they had been doing in previous occasions. Tips pair in 1,193-1,206 range for rest of session. Players also keeping close eye on USD/JPY as stronger JPY tends to push JPY/KRW higher. USD/KRW last at 1,197.7 vs 1,192.0 late yesterday in Seoul amid Kospi's 0.6% loss. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0042 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to rise in 32.050-32.100 band vs 32.060 yesterday, boosted by expected losses in local equities, gains in USD/KRW, but gains will likely be limited due to exporters' month-end TWD settlement demand, says local bank trader. Adds, "the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may lift the pair higher in late session" to help island's exporters; would be continuation of suspected pattern of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over most of month. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares set to consolidate but biased down following Wall Street's fall overnight amid ongoing worries about sustainability of global economic recovery. Shanghai Composite Index support tipped at 2600 after ending +1.6% at 2652.66 yesterday. "The banks' recent string of fundraising efforts put pressure on the (banking) sector, and since the sector has a big weighting, its weakness will hurt the broader market," says Qian Qimin at Shenyin Wanguo Securities. Xinjiang-based companies may be in spotlight today after China's top securities regulator Shang Fulin reiterated Monday that Beijing will continue to support companies in region to raise capital. Shenzhen index +2.6% at 1158.22. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0039 GMT [Dow Jones] Home sale transactions in Singapore likely to fall 30% over next 3-6 months, with some moderation in prices for mass-market projects, following latest government measures to rein in housing market, says \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Cites doubling of minimum cash payment to 10%, which applies to home owners with outstanding housing loans seeking another property, as probably strongest deterrant. Says restrictions on public housing flats - such as increasing minimum occupation period before owners can sell, requiring private home owners buying resale public housing flats to sell private home - will indirectly affect demand from upgraders for mass-market private housing; "we see the measures casting a shadow on near-term property sales." Rates \uc2 Keppel Land\uc2  (K17.SG), UOL (U14.SG), Allgreen (A16.SG), \uc2 Wing Tai\uc2  (W05.SG) at Neutral with respective targets of S$3.92, S$4.18, S$1.33, S$1.93. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 08-30-10 2113ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100831e68v0000z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
