\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-Ex-BOJ Mizuno:  \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention \highlight\uc2  may gain understanding\b0\par\par\uc2 By Leika Kihara   \par \uc2 616 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2010\par 10:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 31 (Reuters) - Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may gain some understanding in the global community if it is aimed at curbing yen gains driven by factors Tokyo cannot control, a former Bank of Japan board member said.\par \par \uc2  Japan needs to get serious about stemming sharp yen gains, which hurt the economy by prompting companies to shift jobs and capital spending overseas, and act on its words when it warns of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Atsushi Mizuno said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2  "I know some people think Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't be able to gain the understanding of its counterparts, and that solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  won't work. Some also say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  without the backing of monetary easing won't work," Mizuno said.\par \par \uc2  "It's true \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at directly pushing up (the dollar/yen rate) won't gain understanding. But the yen's current rise is driven by the outlook for the U.S. economy and passive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to deal with something \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  cannot control, may gain a certain level of understanding," he told Reuters in an interview.\par \par \uc2  Mizuno, who sat on the BOJ board until last December, is currently vice chairman of \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2 's  fixed-income business in the Asia-Pacific region.\par \par \uc2  Japanese policymakers have tried to talk down the yen and signalled the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the yen hit a 15-year high against the dollar last week.\par \par \uc2  Mizuno, who was a prominent bond strategist before sitting on the BOJ board, said Tokyo needed to send a clear message to the market promptly if it cannot tolerate current yen rises.\par \par \uc2  CLEAR MESSAGE\par \par \uc2  "It's important for the government to express concern over the yen's rise," he said.\par \par \uc2  "It's also counterproductive to say it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but not act on its words. The government needs to be strategic when communicating with the market on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it also needs to be bold when necessary."\par \par \uc2  Tokyo would probably have to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone if it were to step in to curb yen gains, as its Group of Seven counterparts, happy with the benefits to exports from their weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , are in no mood for coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since 2004, when it ended a massive yen-selling campaign to beat deflation and a domestic banking crisis.\par \par \uc2  With its fiscal options constrained by Japan's huge public debt, the government has been leaning on the BOJ to do more to ease the pain from sharp yen rises on the economy.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  responded by expanding a fund supply tool at an emergency meeting on Monday. But the move did little to weaken the yen, as markets saw it as having a limited effect on the economy and as more a token gesture to appease the government and show that the bank was doing its part to beat deflation.\par \par \uc2  Mizuno said the remaining options for the BOJ were limited, and that the key was for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to communicate more clearly with markets what exactly it wants to achieve in loosening policy.\par \par \uc2  He ruled out the possibility of the BOJ increasing its outright government bond purchases from the current 21.6 trillion yen ($255.5 billion) per year in the future.\par \par \uc2  "It's not an option for the BOJ and it will not be welcomed by Japanese investors," who have strong appetite for bonds, he said.  ($1=84.53 Yen)  (Additional reporting by Mari Terawaki, Shigeo Kodama; Editing by Edmund Klamann)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/MIZUNO (INTERVIEW, URGENT)|LANGEN|J|ABN|E|RBN|M|D|U|NAT   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100903e68v000h4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
