\par\par \b \uc2 Japan stays out of  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  mkt in Aug as yen rises vs dlr\b0\par\par\uc2 190 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2010\par 12:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 31 (Reuters) - Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it did not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from July 29 to Aug. 27, during which the yen rose to its highest level against the dollar since 1995.\par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities have stayed out of the market for more than six years. The last time they \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen was in March 2004, when they conducted yen-selling, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Aug. 24, the dollar fell to 83.58 yen on trading platform EBS, the lowest since June 1995. The greenback has been hovering near the 15-year low in the past week, standing at 84.40 yen <JPY=> on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Many market particpants believe Japanese authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market to rein in the yen's strength if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  nears an all-time high of 79.75 per dollar hit in April 1995. (Reporting by Rika Otsuka; Editing by Joseph Radford)\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 JAPAN\b0 \highlight\uc2 -\b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2 /DATA|LANGEN|NAT|J|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|U|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100831e68v000m4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
