\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Ikeda: would want BOJ help in  \highlight22\uc2 fx intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2010\par 09:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be effective -vice finmin\par \par \uc2  * Says BOJ should not rule out inflation target, zero rate\par \par \uc2  * Vows to take decisive steps if needed vs rapid yen rise\par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2  By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 31 (Reuters) - The Japanese deputy finance minister on Tuesday said he would want the Bank of Japan to leave extra yen in the market if the government conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is threatening the country's export-reliant economy.\par \par \uc2  If Japan were to step in to stem yen gains, the BOJ might decide not to drain the yen it sold, some traders said. This "unsterilised" \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would leave extra yen in the market, effectively a form of monetary easing.\par \par \uc2  Motohisa Ikeda echoed their view, saying that it would be more effective to have unsterilised than sterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "We undoubtedly won't rule out using our authority," Ikeda said, signalling the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  He was speaking at a conference attended by lawmakers and economists, as a guest speaker but not as a government representative.\par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the prerogative of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the BOJ is its agent ... It would be no good if the BOJ absorbs money from the market in such a case and I'd want the BOJ to carry out unsterilised (\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 )."\par \par \uc2  He also said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should not rule out cutting its policy rate to zero percent from the current 0.1 percent in case unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is carried out.\par \par \uc2  Ikeda added that the "secret of success" for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past was the BOJ's cooperation in unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and consent from other countries.\par \par \uc2  But it is now difficult to gain support from other nations, which Ikeda said appear to be letting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken.\par \par \uc2  He added that Tokyo is ready to take decisive steps on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary to prevent a rapid rise in the yen, which is hovering near a 15-year high against the dollar despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  easing and efforts by the government to talk down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  The BOJ boosted a cheap loan scheme on Monday, bowing to government pressure for action to protect a fragile recovery by curbing the yen's rise. But that failed to scare investors from betting on it climbing further.\par \par \uc2  Ikeda said he appreciated that the BOJ eased policy in line with the government's efforts to beat deflation.\par \par \uc2  But in the long term, he said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should adopt a rigid inflation target, rather than the reference rate it currently has.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004, when a 15-month yen-selling spree came to an end. It sold 35 trillion yen ($414 billion) in that period.  ($1=84.53 Yen)  (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Joseph Radford)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/IKEDA (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100831e68v000dl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
