\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Fall As Economic Woes Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 2,716 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2010\par 00:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Malaysian markets will be closed for the National Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10009.73  -140.92  -1.39% \par Nasdaq     2119.97   -33.66  -1.56% \par S&P 500    1048.92   -15.67  -1.47% \par Xetra DAX  5912.41   -38.76  -0.65% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 24/32  +4/32 0.501%  -5.9BP \par 5Y Tsy    99 9/32 +15/32 1.396%  -9.8BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 23/32 +29/32 2.543% -10.3BP \par  \par USD/JPY       84.56-59     -0.94% \par Range         85.92-84.51 \par EUR/USD       1.2662-67    -0.78% \par Range         1.2769-1.2660 \par AUD/USD       0.8915-20    -1.14% \par Range         0.9030-0.8919 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   11.86/12.24% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   11.65/12.05% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   13.20/13.50% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,239.20   +$1.30 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,235.00-$1,240.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $74.70   -$0.47 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell Monday despite deal activity from Intel and 3M as investors continued to fret about the health of the economy.\par \par \uc2 The drop came in thin trading. NYSE Composite volume Monday totaled about three billion shares, making it the lowest-volume day this year.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average declined 140.92 points, or 1.39%, to 10009.73. With just one day left in the month, the measure is off 4.4% for August.\par \par \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  was one of the Dow's weakest performers with a drop of 32 cents, or 2.5%, to 12.32. Ohio Attorney General Richard Cordray pledged to move forward aggressively with two investor lawsuits over \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 's disclosures before its acquisition of investment bank \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2 . "The court ruled that companies cannot pick and choose what they will tell their shareholders," Cordray said.\par \par \uc2 Intel was also weak with a decline of 41 cents, or 2.2%, to 17.96, after the chip giant agreed to buy the wireless unit of Germany's Infineon Technologies for $1.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 Conglomerate 3M shed 1.35, or 1.7%, to 79.65. The company agreed to acquire small-capitalization Cogent in a deal valued at $943 million, though 3M will only pay $430 million to account for the fingerprint-identification systems maker's large cash pile.\par \par \uc2 Deal activity failed to excite investors as worries about the sustainability of the economic recovery lingered. Weak readings on personal income and Texas-area manufacturing activity added to the market's concerns at the start of a week that includes key reports on manufacturing, unemployment and retailers' same-store sales.\par \par \uc2 "There's a lot of uncertainty on the economy," said Christian Thwaites, president and chief executive of Sentinel Investments. "Every piece of economic data seems to be hitting sideways or being revised down. There's just not enough of a foothold to get into equities right now."\par \par \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  was the Dow's lone gainer, with an increase of 56 cents, or 1.5%, to 38.56. The company, which remains in a battle with Dell for 3Par, authorized an additional $10 billion to buy back its own stock and said it has been selected by the U.S. Air Force for a five-year purchase contract that could be worth up to $800 million.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite declined 33.66, or 1.56%, to 2119.97. It is off 6% for the month.\par \par \uc2 The Standard & Poor's 500 index lost 15.67, or 1.47%, to 1048.92, putting its drop for the month at 4.8%. All of the measure's sectors ended the day in the red, led by financials.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended higher Monday, buoyed by the Federal Reserve's pledge to support the U.S. economy and Beijing's reiteration of the need to keep its macroeconomic policy stable for the rest of this year.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index could test the psychologically important resistance level of 2700 later this week as investors continue to digest recent strong corporate earnings reports.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Monday, but pared much of their early gains as investors wasted little time to take profits after the Bank Of Japan disappointed them with new easing measures that were seen as too little to tackle Japan's deflationary woe.\par \par \uc2 "The market was expecting something more drastic, like monetary easing in full-blown quantitative terms like it did in the early 2000s," said Susumu Kato, chief economist at \uc2 Calyon Capital Markets Asia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Foreign investors would have loved to hear an inflation target being set," said Matthew Lutter, head of equity sales at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa said after market close that downside economic risks are growing more than upside risks, and uncertainty over the economic outlook is increasing.\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures settled slightly lower Monday in U.S. trading, dropping after a three-session rally as data due this week are expected to show that the economy continues to struggle.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures continued their incremental march higher Monday as market participants remain wary about the economic recovery and anticipate a return of more robust buying of the metal in September.\par \par \uc2 Copper futures hit a four-month high in New York Monday amid holiday-thinned volume and some residual support from comments last week by the Federal Reserve chief. U.K. markets were closed Monday for a bank holiday.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar declined against the yen Monday after moves by the Bank of Japan to stem the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's appreciation fell flat.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback also lost ground to the Swiss franc but gained against other widely traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as investors nursed worries about the global recovery and abandoned last week's move into riskier assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar erased strides made ahead of Monday's emergency BOJ meeting. Investors had sold off the yen on expectations that stronger measures than an extension of the BOJ's loan program would be announced.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The markets were not impressed," said Vassili Serebriakov, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors took the view that the BOJ steps weren't really new and were therefore already priced into the yen's current value. However, thin market conditions because of a U.K. holiday tended to contain the dollar's losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The bias for the market is to take the yen higher," Serebriakov said. "Our sense is that we are going to see a retest of [last week's] 15-year highs and perhaps new highs beyond that."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback remains close to the 15-year low it hit last week and is only about four yen above the all-time low at Y79.75 from April, 1995.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday afternoon in New York, the dollar was at Y84.55 from Y85.35 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The euro was at Y107.09 from Y108.72.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was at $1.2663 from $1.2734. The U.K. pound was at $1.5459 from $1.5513. The U.S. dollar was at CHF1.0261 from CHF1.0293. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 83.160 from 82.875.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the general push to safe harbors, the euro approached its all-time low of CHF1.2971 against the Swiss franc before a modest uptick. The dollar had also dropped to within striking distance of its earlier seven-month lows against the Swiss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The North American session showed a marked decline in risk sentiment, said Matthew Strauss, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Toronto. The retreat from risk reflects in part a refocusing on the fiscal and economic issues faced by some peripheral countries in the euro zone, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During Asian hours, the BOJ policy board agreed to loosen monetary policy by adding another Y10 trillion in six-month funds into the market at an ultra-low rate of 0.1%, expanding on its current program of three-month loans.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The announcement appears to have disappointed dealers looking for either a larger amount or a deeper initiative," said Andrew Wilkinson, senior market analyst at Interactive Brokers in Greenwich, Conn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The unknown piece in the puzzle is whether a frustrated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will now turn to the next step of open \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market by selling yen and buying dollars. The yen will continue as the focus for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets as traders go on watch for any signs of direct market action, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys rallied Monday as concerns about the global economic recovery lingered, driving market participants to seek refuge in the low-risk U.S. government bond market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest catalyst fueling fears about the health of the global economy Monday was a move by the Bank of Japan to further ease monetary policy. Meantime, the week's first pieces of U.S. data reinforced the idea that the U.S. recovery remains very fragile.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys gained across the board, but those issues maturing in the next five- to 10 years outperformed. With shorter-term Treasury yields bouncing around record lows, issues in the five to 10-year sectors have become more popular with market participants who are still seeking safety, but are looking for a higher rate of return.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gains came as stocks fell and reversed a sharp government bond market sell-off late last week, as investors poured into stocks and sold Treasurys. Comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke Friday that policymakers would do whatever it takes to help the U.S. economy heal supported stocks Friday. Investors, however, were feeling less optimistic Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Monday afternoon trading in New York, the two-year note was up 4/32 to 0.501%, the 10-year was up 29/32 to 2.543% and the 30-year was up 1 21/32 to 3.600%. Gains, however, came in thin trading. London was closed for a bank holiday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "There's still a flight to quality going on" in the Treasury market, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Kevin Walter\f28 \uc2 \f2 , head of trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas Securities Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York. Many market participants also believe that Friday's jobs report could be especially weak, Walter said, which could fuel further buying in the U.S. government bond market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended lower Monday, reversing an earlier rise as strong gains in the local equity market triggered selling of safe-haven assets. [ 30-08-10 2212GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ICBC's CNY25 billion convertible bond sale, due Tuesday, continued to add liquidity pressure to the money market as investors scrambled for funds for the bond subscription, a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer said. However, further downside is likely limited as the liquidity crunch is expected to ease after the bond issue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Short-term support for the Shanghai exchange government bond index is tipped at 126.5, the trader said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond prices fell Monday after U.S. Treasurys slid Friday, but recouped some losses after the Bank of Japan announced it will extend an emergency loan program.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At an emergency meeting, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy board said it will lend another Y10 trillion in six-month cash at a fixed rate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the move was largely in line with expectations for further easing, some JGB market participants had expected the Bank of Japan would expand its current three-month lending facility. For that reason, some investors treated the introduction of the new six-month program as a mild positive, some analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also helping JGBs to make up some lost ground was buying by Japanese banks, insurance companies and pension funds for month-end bookkeeping.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The impact on the JGB market should be limited, given that today's action had been much anticipated," \f28 \uc2 \f2 Royal Bank of Scotland\f28 \uc2 \f2  wrote in a report. But further yen appreciation could support JGBs, the bank said. "If the recent trend of (yen) appreciation continues and the market possibly starts to expect a further easing, it will likely make the short- to intermediate- sectors firm."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The focus for the JGB market for the rest of the week will likely shift to economic data in Japan and the U.S, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the data reveal further slowing in the countries' economic recoveries, that could sustain dip-buying in the JGB market, said Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities's Hasegawa said. In that case, lead September JGB futures could rise to 142.90 while the benchmark 10-year yield could fall to 0.970%, she said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's government Monday unveiled an outline of a new economic stimulus package worth Y920 billion that is aimed at curbing the negative impact of the stronger yen on the economy and putting a stop to the current deflationary trend.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government "announced the outline of the package today to give the impression that its economic measures and the Bank of Japan's monetary easing steps are properly coordinated," Minister of State for Economic and Fiscal Policy Satoshi Arai said at a press conference.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Tuesday, Japanese industrial output likely fell 0.4% on the month in July, according to the median forecast in a survey of economists by Dow Jones and Nikkei. That would mark the second straight month of falls, after production dropped 1.5% on the month in June, and would likely add to concerns over a slowdown in country's economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US July Consumer Spending Rises As Expected \par Tax Credit Boosts 2Q Home Prices \par BOJ Eases, Government Outlines Steps, But Yen Rises \par Dallas Fed Aug Business Activity Falls \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  Adds $10B To Buyback \par Weather Stalls Gulf Spill Repair \par Chrysler To Sell Fiat Subcompact In 2011 \par IMF To Broaden Loan Offerings \par RIM Gives India Tools To Monitor Email \par Japan PM Kan: BOJ Move Timely Response For Econ - Nikkei \par Japan Government Unveils Around Y920 Bln Economic Stimulus Package \par BOJ Eases, Government Outlines Steps, But Yen Rises \par S Korea Manufacturers September Outlook Index Rebounds To 104 \par S Korea Banks' 2Q Average Capital Adequacy Ratio 14.29% Vs 14.70% 1Q \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2100  S Korea      Aug Business Survey Index \par 2245  New Zealand  Jul Building Consents Issued \par 2300  S Korea      Jul Service Industry Activity Index \par