\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,345 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2010\par 11:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UNDER PRESSURE, BOJ BOOSTS LOANS; MORE MOVES POSSIBLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan failed to curb the strong yen with an emergency easing Monday, raising pressure on policymakers to do more in coming days to stop the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise and boost the struggling economy. (published 0833 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ GOV SHIRAKAWA: TO TAKE APPROPRIATE POLICY STEPS AT PROPER TIME \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said Monday that the BOJ would "take appropriate steps at the proper time" to support the economy, though he didn't say what monetary policy options remain for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to take. (published 0635 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: EURO-ZONE CONFIDENCE IMPROVES FOR THIRD MONTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Consumers in the 16 nations that use the euro became more upbeat about the outlook for the economy and less fearful of losing their jobs in August, driving a revival of confidence in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  area. (published 0900 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY DIW: ECONOMIC GROWTH TO LOSE SOME MOMENTUM IN AUTUMN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's economic recovery will continue this autumn albeit at a slower pace, the DIW economic research institute said Monday. (published 0600 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DATA SNAP: SPAIN AUG HCPI +1.8% ON YR VS +1.9% IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Spanish consumer price inflation eased slightly in August on lower fuel and food prices, Spain's National Statistics Institute, or INE, said in a statement Monday. (published 0700 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par POLAND'S 2Q GDP +3.5% ON YEAR, EXPECTED +3.2% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Poland's gross domestic product rose at an annual rate of 3.5% in the second quarter, compared with an annual increase of 3.0% in the first quarter, the Central Statistical Office, or GUS, said Monday. (published 0820 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TENSIONS FLARE IN AUSTRALIA'S POLITICAL NEGOTIATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Discussions to form a minority government in Australia have become embroiled in tension after a key independent lawmaker Monday detailed claims of intimidation tactics by members of the opposition coalition which he said are a bid to derail the talks process and force fresh elections. (published 0447 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HOLMAN, THIRD CZECH CENTRAL BANKER, CALLS FOR RATE HIKE-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Czech \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is likely to lift its benchmark interest rate at the end of the year or early next year to 1% from the current historic-low of 0.75%, monetary policy member Robert Holman said over the weekend supporting recent comments from his peers. (published 0737 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU: HUNGARY MUST CUT 2011 BUDGET DEFICIT BELOW 3% OF GDP-REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary must reduce its budget deficit to below 3% of gross domestic product next year and focus on sustainable economic growth, European Economic and Monetary Affairs Commissioner Olli Rehn told Hungarian Sunday paper Vasarnapi Hirek. (published 0702 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PM: HUNGARY'S IMF DEAL WAS BORROWING, NOT COOPERATION-AGENCY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's agreement with the International Monetary Fund was based on borrowing, not on cooperation, state news agency MTI cited Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orban as saying Monday at the conference of Hungarian foreign embassy leaders. (published 0755 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RUSSIA TO HIKE IMPORT DUTY ON CARS TO STIMULATE DOMESTIC INDUSTRY - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russia will gradually raise import duties on foreign cars in order to stimulate the opening of new auto plants in the country, the Interfax news agency reports Monday, citing Prime Minister Vladimir Putin. (published 0655 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGAPORE UNVEILS NEW CURBS FOR PROPERTY MARKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's government Monday introduced new curbs against housing market speculation in the city state after prices surged to record highs, stoking fears of a bubble. (published 0834 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NEW ZEALAND JULY TRADE DEFICIT WIDER THAN MARKET EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's trade balance swung into deficit in July from a surplus in June, driven by increased imports of cars, petroleum and fertilizer and a seasonal decline in exports of milk, one of the country's main products. (published 0250 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par NZ BUSINESS CONFIDENCE EASES FURTHER IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's business confidence eased in August from July on increasing concerns about the tepid nature of the economic recovery, a leading survey showed Monday. (published 0445 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN MINING BOOM LIGHTS FIRE UNDER PROFIT GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A booming mining sector boosted Australian corporate profits massively in the second quarter, a result that highlights the transforming effect surging coal and iron ore prices will have on the economy well into the future, helping it to grow even as major economies battle stiffening headwinds. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN NEW HOME SALES FALL 7% IN JULY VS JUNE - HIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian new home sales fell 7% in July from June, the third consecutive monthly decline and further confirmation that higher interest rates are eroding demand in the sector. (published 0058 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par INDIA PLAN PANEL HEAD: FOOD PRICES TO EASE BY MID-OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's high food prices are likely to ease by mid-October as normal rains help boost farm output, Montek Singh Ahluwalia, head of the Planning Commission, said Monday. (published 0546 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par THAI FINANCE MINISTER: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAY RAISE RATES 25 BPS THIS YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may hike its policy interest rate by 25 basis points before the year ends to cool the domestic economy, Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said Monday. (published 0115 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FINANCE MINISTRY: THAILAND'S 2010 GDP GROWTH LIKELY ABOVE 7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday that economic growth this year will likely exceed 7%, thanks to robust growth during the first half of the year. (published 0545 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA FACES POLICY DILEMMAS, SHOULD KEEP POLICY STABLE - PEOPLE'S DAILY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China faces multiple "dilemmas" in macroeconomic policy as growth is slowing but inflation expectations remain strong, an editorial in the People's Daily said Monday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MOST ECONOMISTS FAVOR EXTENDING BUSH TAX CUTS -NABE SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most U.S. economists want the Bush-era tax cuts to be extended given growing concerns about the strength of the recovery, but a survey released Monday also showed divisions on what path the Federal Reserve should take. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK ECONOMY TO SLOW SHARPLY OVER MEDIUM TERM - BCC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pace of the U.K.'s economic growth will slow sharply over the medium term as the coalition government's tough measures to reduce the deficit set in, the British Chambers of Commerce said Sunday. (published 2301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par KOREA HYDRO & NUCLEAR POWER LOOKING TO SELL $500M IN GLOBAL BONDS -SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State-run Korea Hydro & Nuclear Power Corp. is looking to sell dollar-denominated global bonds soon after meeting global investors next week, people familiar with the company's plans said Monday. (published 0556 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par SNB WATCH: JORDAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WARNING TO KEEP MARKET ON TOES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's warning that it is keeping a very close watch on the Swiss franc's movements against the euro will keep a holiday-thinned \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market on its toes, but is unlikely to be translated into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , according to analysts. (published 0832 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WOULD FACE POWERFUL CROSS CURRENTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does dive into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to try to quell strength in the yen, it will find itself struggling against some strong cross currents. (published 2200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: MALAYSIA JULY EXPORTS LIKELY +12.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's exports likely rose for the eighth consecutive month in July, but at a slower pace than a month earlier as overseas demand continues to cool from its peak in the first half, economists said. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PREVIEW: HK JULY RETAIL SALES GROWTH LIKELY SLOWED FROM JUNE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's retail sales likely continued to post double-digit growth in July, supported by strong tourist spending, although growth slowed for a second straight month and could fall to single-digit territory in the fourth quarter as domestic economic activity is slowing, economists said. (published 0439 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTION VIEWS: BUY AUSSIE/YEN 3M VS 1Y VOL; SPOT TO PERK UP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TRADE: Buy A$180 million AUD/JPY 3-month vs A$100 million 1-year straddle, taking in net A$2.35 million. (published 0509 GMT) [ 30-08-10 0902GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100830e68u000iy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
