\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 905 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2010\par 08:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =UPDATE: UNDER POLITICAL PRESSURE, BOJ BOOSTS LOANS; MARKETS UNIMPRESSED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan, under strong political pressure to stem the yen's surge, extended an emergency-loan program Monday, but markets were unimpressed by the monetary easing. (published 0438 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par JAPAN PM KAN, BOJ GOVERNOR SHIRAKAWA TO MEET MON AFTERNOON -NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Prime Minister Naoto Kan said he will meet with Bank of Japan Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa on Monday afternoon, the Nikkei reported. (published 0058 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =TENSIONS FLARE IN AUSTRALIA'S POLITICAL NEGOTIATIONS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Discussions to form a minority government in Australia have become embroiled in tension after a key independent lawmaker Monday detailed claims of intimidation tactics by members of the opposition coalition which he said are a bid to derail the talks process and force fresh elections. (published 0447 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =3RD UPDATE: SINGAPORE MINISTER: RISKS PROPERTY MARKET BUBBLE WILL FORM \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's National Development Minister Mah Bow Tan said Monday that the island state's property market is at risk of forming a bubble that would hurt everyone if it bursts, following the announcement of a series of measures to put the brakes on soaring home prices. (published 0402 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: NEW ZEALAND JULY TRADE DEFICIT WIDER THAN MARKET EXPECTED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's trade balance swung into deficit in July from a surplus in June, driven by increased imports of cars, petroleum and fertilizer and a seasonal decline in exports of milk, one of the country's main products. (published 0250 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =UPDATE: NZ BUSINESS CONFIDENCE EASES FURTHER IN AUGUST \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New Zealand's business confidence eased in August from July on increasing concerns about the tepid nature of the economic recovery, a leading survey showed Monday. (published 0445 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: AUSTRALIAN MINING BOOM LIGHTS FIRE UNDER PROFIT GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A booming mining sector boosted Australian corporate profits massively in the second quarter, a result that highlights the transforming effect surging coal and iron ore prices will have on the economy well into the future, helping it to grow even as major economies battle stiffening headwinds. (published 0430 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par AUSTRALIAN NEW HOME SALES FALL 7% IN JULY VS JUNE - HIA \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian new home sales fell 7% in July from June, the third consecutive monthly decline and further confirmation that higher interest rates are eroding demand in the sector. (published 0058 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par INDIA PLAN PANEL HEAD: FOOD PRICES TO EASE BY MID-OCTOBER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 India's high food prices are likely to ease by mid-October as normal rains help boost farm output, Montek Singh Ahluwalia, head of the Planning Commission, said Monday. (published 0546 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par THAI FINANCE MINISTER: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MAY RAISE RATES 25 BPS THIS YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may hike its policy interest rate by 25 basis points before the year ends to cool the domestic economy, Finance Minister Korn Chatikavanij said Monday. (published 0115 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par FINANCE MINISTRY: THAILAND'S 2010 GDP GROWTH LIKELY ABOVE 7% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Monday that economic growth this year will likely exceed 7%, thanks to robust growth during the first half of the year. (published 0545 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CHINA FACES POLICY DILEMMAS, SHOULD KEEP POLICY STABLE - PEOPLE'S DAILY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China faces multiple "dilemmas" in macroeconomic policy as growth is slowing but inflation expectations remain strong, an editorial in the People's Daily said Monday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =MOST ECONOMISTS FAVOR EXTENDING BUSH TAX CUTS -NABE SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most U.S. economists want the Bush-era tax cuts to be extended given growing concerns about the strength of the recovery, but a survey released Monday also showed divisions on what path the Federal Reserve should take. (published 0401 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par UK ECONOMY TO SLOW SHARPLY OVER MEDIUM TERM - BCC \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The pace of the U.K.'s economic growth will slow sharply over the medium term as the coalition government's tough measures to reduce the deficit set in, the British Chambers of Commerce said Sunday. (published 2301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par KOREA HYDRO & NUCLEAR POWER LOOKING TO SELL $500M IN GLOBAL BONDS -SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 State-run Korea Hydro & Nuclear Power Corp. is looking to sell dollar-denominated global bonds soon after meeting global investors next week, people familiar with the company's plans said Monday. (published 0556 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VIEW: BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  WOULD FACE POWERFUL CROSS CURRENTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  does dive into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to try to quell strength in the yen, it will find itself struggling against some strong cross currents. (published 2200 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: MALAYSIA JULY EXPORTS LIKELY +12.1% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Malaysia's exports likely rose for the eighth consecutive month in July, but at a slower pace than a month earlier as overseas demand continues to cool from its peak in the first half, economists said. (published 0408 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =PREVIEW: HK JULY RETAIL SALES GROWTH LIKELY SLOWED FROM JUNE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hong Kong's retail sales likely continued to post double-digit growth in July, supported by strong tourist spending, although growth slowed for a second straight month and could fall to single-digit territory in the fourth quarter as domestic economic activity is slowing, economists said. (published 0439 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTION VIEWS: BUY AUSSIE/YEN 3M VS 1Y VOL; SPOT TO PERK UP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TRADE: Buy A$180 million AUD/JPY 3-month vs A$100 million 1-year straddle, taking in net A$2.35 million. (published 0509 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 30-08-10 0600GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100830e68u000bl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
