\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT:  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japan Running Out Of Options To Anchor Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 738 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2010\par 21:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (In "\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Japan Running Out Of Options To Anchor Yen," published at 2:57 p.m. ET, the dollar's levels against the yen Monday and Friday were incorrectly stated. The correct version follows.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Karen Johnson \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan's monetary-easing measures took no wind from the yen's sails Monday, calling into question the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's ability to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb toward an all-time high against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ is running out of policy options, with even the heaviest weapon in its arsenal, direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , viewed as an unlikely solution.\par \par \uc2 So far, whatever they've tried, Japanese financial officials haven't been able to convince investors to stop selling dollars and buying yen. The greenback remains close to the 15-year low it hit last week and is only about four yen above the all-time low at Y79.75 from April 1995.\par \par \uc2 This puts Japan's leaders in an extremely difficult position. They are facing increased domestic pressure to do more to help Japan's export-driven economy by lowering the value of the yen to make Japanese goods more competitive. But on a practical level, there's not much they can really do to hinder the yen's appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "It really is hard for a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reverse \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trends," said Vassili Serebriakov, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  in New York. "What [Japanese officials] can do is to perhaps slow or reduce volatility of certain trends."\par \par \uc2 The steps announced Monday certainly didn't dissuade yen buying. The Y920 billion stimulus program is part of a two-pronged strategy intended to prevent the rising yen from undermining Japan's fragile, export-led recovery. At an emergency meeting, the BOJ's policy board voted to offer domestic financial institutions Y10 trillion of six-month loans at an ultralow rate of 0.1%, in addition to the Y20 trillion in three-month loans it has been offering.\par \par \uc2 The added funding is meant to lower interbank interest rates and thereby help stop, or at least slow, the recent heavy fund flow into the yen. But the market took the view the steps were no more than what had been expected and therefore were already priced into the yen's value. The yen moved higher, with the dollar now down at Y84.70, from Y85.35 late Friday.\par \par \uc2 "There was always a high threshold for impressing the market," Serebriakov said. "And this was not a big enough move to really impact the yen in a way that would cause yen weakness."\par \par \uc2 History has proven it would be difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  successfully on its own. The BOJ spent an estimated Y35 trillion over a 15-month period that ended March 2004, in an attempt to slow the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's appreciation. It had only limited success at a huge cost and that effort marked the last time it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2 Joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  involving the Federal Reserve and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have a bigger impact, but neither the Fed nor the ECB is seen as willing to participate in a concerted action.\par \par \uc2 In the end, the Japanese government could be forced simply to wait it out. Eventually, the U.S. economy will emerge from its current soft patch, its recovery will pick up again and the Fed will again look to tighten monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 "That really is what is going to turn around the yen," Serebriakov said, "rather than any sort of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 When traders deem the greenback a better investment than the yen, they will sell yen and buy dollars. Until then, the yen will appreciate, no matter what the BOJ tries to do.\par \par \uc2 (Karen Johnson writes about Canadian fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires. She can be reached at 416-306-2022; or via email: karen.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 08-30-10 1520ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100830e68u000ar\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
