\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Down Vs Yen After BOJ Measures Fail To Impress\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 853 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2010\par 16:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--The dollar slumped against the yen Monday after measures announced by the Bank of Japan in response to the yen's strength fell short of market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The greenback reversed gains made in anticipation of the outcome of the BOJ's emergency meeting. The announcement of an extension of the BOJ's loan program was deemed insufficient to curb the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's advance.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ policy board bank agreed to loosen monetary policy by adding another Y10 trillion in six-month funds into the market at an ultra-low rate of 0.1%, expanding on its current program of three-month loans. The extra funding is meant to lower interbank interest rates and thereby help stop, or at least slow, the recent heavy fund flow into the yen.\par \par \uc2 But even as the BOJ measures were announced, they were met skeptically, with the market taking the view the steps were no more than what had been expected all along and therefore were already priced into the yen's current value.\par \par \uc2 "The announcement appears to have disappointed dealers looking for either a larger amount or a deeper initiative," said Andrew Wilkinson, senior market analyst at Interactive Brokers in Greenwich, Conn.\par \par \uc2 The unknown piece in the puzzle is whether a frustrated \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will now turn to the next step of open \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by selling yen and buying dollars. The yen will continue as the focus for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as traders go on watch for any signs of direct market action, said analysts.\par \par \uc2 In early New York trading, the dollar was at Y84.69 from Y85.35 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The euro was at Y107.67 from Y108.72.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was modestly higher against most other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors shifted away from risk, but with London markets closed for a holiday, subdued trading flows blunted the move.\par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2709 from $1.2734. The U.K. pound was at $1.5520 from $1.5513. The U.S. dollar was at CHF1.0244 from CHF1.0293. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 82.903 from 82.875.\par \par \uc2 Following the Bank of Japan's action, the Japanese government is now expected to do its part by announcing a new stimulus package Tuesday. The targeted spending program will likely draw on Y920 billion reserve fund from the current budget with a potential for another Y800 billion.\par \par \uc2 Traders said that they are now looking to a meeting planned later in the day between Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa and Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan for further clues on the next steps by authorities. Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said over the weekend he would take "decisive" steps to prevent a further sharp appreciation of the yen, a phrase previously used to signal that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could come soon.\par \par \uc2 Dai-ichi Life Research Institute chief economist Hideo Kumano said that "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors had expected too much from BOJ." He added that the new funding provision will have an impact on Japanese government bond rates. But he said if that fails the next step would be up to the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "If the yen keeps rising despite the BOJ's move today, I think it will be the MOF's turn to step into the market," he said.\par \par \uc2 The dollar's gains against the euro and slight advance against the Australian and New Zealand dollars reflected a shift away from riskier assets Monday morning, although the move was constrained by thin trading.\par \par \uc2 "The market has been taking risk off the table...so the dollar itself is now better bid," said Jack Spitz, managing director for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , financial markets and derivatives at National Bank Financial in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 The market was little affected by news that consumer spending rose 0.4% last month after staying flat in June. Incomes increased less than expected, up 0.2% after remaining unchanged in June.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Canada Morning \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian dollar is holding steady against the U.S. dollar early Monday, despite the modest strengthening for the greenback.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. dollar was at C$1.0519, from C$1.0522 late Friday. It had given up some ground overnight, slipping to C$1.0474.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian unit was little changed after Canada's second-quarter current account deficit data missed expectations. It was reported at C$11.02 billion, from a deficit of C$8.46 billion the quarter before. Economists were expecting a C$10.7 billion deficit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Eyes now turn to the second quarter real gross domestic product data for Canada, due Tuesday. It's the last major domestic data point before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 's scheduled policy decision on Sept. 8.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Don Curren, Dow Jones Newswires; 416-306-2020; don.curren@dowjones.com\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Miho Nakauchi in Tokyo and Karen Johnson in Toronto contributed to this article.) [ 08-30-10 1003ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100