\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Gold To Breach $1,300 In 4Q10 - Standard Bank\b0\par\par\uc2 1,751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2010\par 06:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0422 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Bank reiterates view that gold will hit new nominal record highs later in year on seasonal physical demand and as Treasury yields edge lower. Bank says yield on 10-year US inflation-linked bond is below 1%, implying that market expects real interest rates to average only around 1% over next 10 years. "Back in June, the same yield traded around 1.3%. A lower implied real yield favours gold investment. We maintain our view that gold will breach $1,300 in 4Q10," says house. Spot gold at $1,236.10/oz, down $2 since Friday's NY close and likely to stay in a tight range with major bullion trading centre London closed for UK national holiday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds down as improving risk appetite erodes demand for safe-haven assets, also partial devolvement of benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at Friday's auction weighing on market, says senior trader with primary dealership. Benchmark 2020 bond at 98.70 vs 98.83 Friday close. "The market may not be in a position to take a further devolvement. RBI should clarify why it chose to devolve the benchmark bond at an aggressive cutoff (8.0308%). It could easily have got full subscription had it pegged the yield at a cutoff of 8.05%," trader says. Adds, market may tone down aggressive shorting. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 98.50-98.85 band in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0414 GMT incorrectly stated the name of the BOJ Governor. The correct version follows:) JPY rises vs EUR, USD as non-Japan hedge funds, Japan interbank players lock in earlier gains as BOJ's monetary policy "came without surprises," while Nikkei trims gains, denting risk-tolerance, says senior trader at Europe bank. "While views linger that the BOJ's action came from the government's pressure, BOJ Gov. Shirakawa won't likely say something meaningful at his press conference" from 0530 GMT, which might limit any JPY moves, trader says. Adds, JPY may strengthen more in coming days if U.S. economic reports, such as today's personal spending, Friday's non-farm payrolls data, come in weaker than expected, darkening outlook for economy; but USD/JPY might not fall sharply down road as impact of BOJ monetary easing may take effect, expectations of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  loom. USD/JPY's support at 85.00 vs 85.40; EUR/JPY's downside at 108.70 vs 108.80. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia's July exports may rise 12.1% on-year vs +17.2% in June, according to median forecast of 13 economists in Dow Jones poll. "The continued moderation in growth rate should be quite marked in July to reinforce our view that Malaysia's export growth peaked in the first quarter and we are heading for a less rosy second half," says Standard Chartered economist Alvin Liew. Action Economics' David Cohen says clouding outlook for 2H will be concerns over economic growth in U.S., cooling off in China. Imports likely +18.6% on-year vs +30.1% in June, trade surplus likely at MYR6.1 billion ($1.95 billion) for the month vs MYR6.04 billion in June, according to poll. Data due 1001 GMT Thursday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0415 GMT [Dow Jones] Economic impact of BOJ's latest monetary easing will be "close to zero," says Macquarie economist Richard Jerram; says BOJ holding "emergency" policy meeting to decide on the steps in absence of any fresh economic information to act on suggests political pressure forced the move; "It's largely a charade," he says; Normally, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  independence considered a good thing in most countries, but in Japan's case, where BOJ has let deflation eat away at the economy for so long, political pressure to do something to stop it may not be so bad; "You can argue that BOJ independence hasn't served Japan well over the past 12-15 years," he says. "You can expect further moves to appease the politicians." (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY rises vs EUR, USD as non-Japan hedge funds, Japan interbank players lock in earlier gains as BOJ's monetary policy "came without surprises," while Nikkei trims gains, denting risk-tolerance, says senior trader at Europe bank. "While views linger that the BOJ's action came from the government's pressure, BOJ Gov. Shiraka won't likely say something meaningful at his press conference" from 0530 GMT, which might limit any JPY moves, trader says. Adds, JPY may strengthen more in coming days if U.S. economic reports, such as today's personal spending, Friday's non-farm payrolls data, come in weaker than expected, darkening outlook for economy; but USD/JPY might not fall sharply down road as impact of BOJ monetary easing may take effect, expectations of possible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  loom. USD/JPY's support at 85.00 vs 85.40; EUR/JPY's downside at 108.70 vs 108.80. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 2.1% at 9182.13 vs morning close of 9265.39, trimming gains as market disappointed with BOJ easing measures, USD/JPY also falling, Nikkei may hover near 9200, say market analysts. "The market was expecting something more drastic, like monetary easing in quantitative terms," says Susumu Kato, chief economist at \uc2 Calyon Capital Markets Asia\uc2 . "Shirakawa came back from the U.S. earlier than planned and held an emergency meeting, so the market's expectations were high." Bank's policy board says BOJ will lend another Y10 trillion in 6-month cash at 0.1% fixed rate to banks. Exporters trimming gains, TDK (6762.TO) up 3.4% at Y4,565 vs morning close of Y4,620, Honda (7267.TO) up 2.3% at Y2,875 vs Y2,910. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend gains midday, tracking rallies in region on continued Fed-induced optimism over U.S. economy, Beijing's reiteration of keeping policy stability in 2H. Shanghai Composite Index +1.5% at 2648.57, with psychological resistance at 2700. "The commodities stocks have always had the support of solid earnings, good valuations; now with Bernanke's assurance, investors are more confident about the pace of global recovery," says Zhang Yongfen at \uc2 Sinolink Securities\uc2  Wealth Management Center. People's Daily's front-page editorial today says China's macroeconomic policy should stress stability in 2H to maintain support for economy. ICBC (601398.SH) +1.0% at CNY4.14, Baoshan Iron & Steel (600019.SH) +2.9% at 6.50, Chalco (601600.SH) +2.2% at CNY10.27. Shenzhen Composite Index +2.2% at 1152.98. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive JPY lower may happen sooner than previously anticipated, Nomura says, following BOJ easing measures just announced. Reckons that BOJ's expansion of fixed-rate loan program from JPY20T to JPY30T and extension of their duration from three to six months are in line with market consensus, but more can be done as politics appear to key consideration since PM Kan and former Chief Secretary General Ozawa will vie for the DPJ presidency Sep 14. "The Kan administration wants to appeal to its ability to address JPY appreciation, which is a very public concern," Nomura says; notes that Ozawa's faction includes several members of the DPJ's radical anti-deflation lawmakers, who advocate revising the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Law. Warns that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now may happen above the previous suspected trigger at the Y82-Y83 area. JPY/KRW last at 14.02 vs 14.00 before BOJ announcement, AUD/JPY at 76.70 vs 77.39, USD/JPY at 85.39 vs 85.86. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD, AUD, GBP falling vs JPY, taking cue from falls in USD/JPY, EUR/JPY, where non-Japan hedge funds taking a lead, says senior dealer at major non-Japanese bank in Tokyo. Says "announced measures look a bid odd and are not as aggressive as we had expected," he says. Adds, hedge funds may keep buying JPY ahead "to test the government's will to see if they would really go ahead and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. NZD/JPY last 60.72, may fall to 60.50. AUD/JPY last at 76.74, may fall to 76.50. GBP/JPY at 132.60, may fall to 132.40.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely to rise to 4800 at year end and to 5400 at the end of 2011, says CommSec. "While Aussie companies are in good shape, we remain cautious on prospects for the ASX 200. The Australian share market is still tracking other international markets, in particular the U.S. Foreign investors are also inclined to look for growth opportunities or turnaround stories. In Australia the coming year looks to be more of the same--the economy getting back to 'normal' growth with 'normal' interest rates and 'normal' profit growth. Overall these are hardly the type of conditions that would prompt investors to take big bets on Australia." Index last up 1.5% at 4437.1. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bond prices edge lower on mild profit-taking by interbank players after recent steep gains. Capital inflows that boosted bond demand in recent weeks seem to have faded this morning, though most don't expect foreign players to rush out of Thai bonds just yet. "The capital flows will be key determinate for the market's direction. Should there be further inflows, which is fairly likely, then yields could continue to head further south," says trader. Yields expected to move in just few bps range rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.68%/2.65% vs 2.66%/2.63% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.77%/2.74% vs 2.75%/2.72%, June 2019 at 2.83%/2.76% vs 2.80%/2.76%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com) [ 08-30-10 0022ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100830e68u00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
