\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Down;Hedge Funds Sell; 85.00 Floor Tipped\b0\par\par\uc2 3,347 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2010\par 06:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY rises vs EUR, USD as non-Japan hedge funds, Japan interbank players lock in earlier gains as BOJ's monetary policy "came without surprises," while Nikkei trims gains, denting risk-tolerance, says senior trader at Europe bank. "While views linger that the BOJ's action came from the government's pressure, BOJ Gov. Shirakawa won't likely say something meaningful at his press conference" from 0530 GMT, which might limit any JPY moves, trader says. Adds, JPY may strengthen more in coming days if U.S. economic reports, such as today's personal spending, Friday's non-farm payrolls data, come in weaker than expected, darkening outlook for economy; but USD/JPY might not fall sharply down road as impact of BOJ monetary easing may take effect, expectations of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loom. USD/JPY's support at 85.00 vs 85.40; EUR/JPY's downside at 108.70 vs 108.80. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Corrected August 30, 2010 00:21 ET (04:21 GMT)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 2.1% at 9182.13 vs morning close of 9265.39, trimming gains as market disappointed with BOJ easing measures, USD/JPY also falling, Nikkei may hover near 9200, say market analysts. "The market was expecting something more drastic, like monetary easing in quantitative terms," says Susumu Kato, chief economist at \uc2 Calyon Capital Markets Asia\uc2 . "Shirakawa came back from the U.S. earlier than planned and held an emergency meeting, so the market's expectations were high." Bank's policy board says BOJ will lend another Y10 trillion in 6-month cash at 0.1% fixed rate to banks. Exporters trimming gains, TDK (6762.TO) up 3.4% at Y4,565 vs morning close of Y4,620, Honda (7267.TO) up 2.3% at Y2,875 vs Y2,910. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0406 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares extend gains midday, tracking rallies in region on continued Fed-induced optimism over U.S. economy, Beijing's reiteration of keeping policy stability in 2H. Shanghai Composite Index +1.5% at 2648.57, with psychological resistance at 2700. "The commodities stocks have always had the support of solid earnings, good valuations; now with Bernanke's assurance, investors are more confident about the pace of global recovery," says Zhang Yongfen at \uc2 Sinolink Securities\uc2  Wealth Management Center. People's Daily's front-page editorial today says China's macroeconomic policy should stress stability in 2H to maintain support for economy. ICBC (601398.SH) +1.0% at CNY4.14, Baoshan Iron & Steel (600019.SH) +2.9% at 6.50, Chalco (601600.SH) +2.2% at CNY10.27. Shenzhen Composite Index +2.2% at 1152.98. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive JPY lower may happen sooner than previously anticipated, Nomura says, following BOJ easing measures just announced. Reckons that BOJ's expansion of fixed-rate loan program from JPY20T to JPY30T and extension of their duration from three to six months are in line with market consensus, but more can be done as politics appear to key consideration since PM Kan and former Chief Secretary General Ozawa will vie for the DPJ presidency Sep 14. "The Kan administration wants to appeal to its ability to address JPY appreciation, which is a very public concern," Nomura says; notes that Ozawa's faction includes several members of the DPJ's radical anti-deflation lawmakers, who advocate revising the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Law. Warns that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now may happen above the previous suspected trigger at the Y82-Y83 area. JPY/KRW last at 14.02 vs 14.00 before BOJ announcement, AUD/JPY at 76.70 vs 77.39, USD/JPY at 85.39 vs 85.86. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD, AUD, GBP falling vs JPY, taking cue from falls in USD/JPY, EUR/JPY, where non-Japan hedge funds taking a lead, says senior dealer at major non-Japanese bank in Tokyo. Says "announced measures look a bid odd and are not as aggressive as we had expected," he says. Adds, hedge funds may keep buying JPY ahead "to test the government's will to see if they would really go ahead and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. NZD/JPY last 60.72, may fall to 60.50. AUD/JPY last at 76.74, may fall to 76.50. GBP/JPY at 132.60, may fall to 132.40.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely to rise to 4800 at year end and to 5400 at the end of 2011, says CommSec. "While Aussie companies are in good shape, we remain cautious on prospects for the ASX 200. The Australian share market is still tracking other international markets, in particular the U.S. Foreign investors are also inclined to look for growth opportunities or turnaround stories. In Australia the coming year looks to be more of the same--the economy getting back to 'normal' growth with 'normal' interest rates and 'normal' profit growth. Overall these are hardly the type of conditions that would prompt investors to take big bets on Australia." Index last up 1.5% at 4437.1. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bond prices edge lower on mild profit-taking by interbank players after recent steep gains. Capital inflows that boosted bond demand in recent weeks seem to have faded this morning, though most don't expect foreign players to rush out of Thai bonds just yet. "The capital flows will be key determinate for the market's direction. Should there be further inflows, which is fairly likely, then yields could continue to head further south," says trader. Yields expected to move in just few bps range rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.68%/2.65% vs 2.66%/2.63% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.77%/2.74% vs 2.75%/2.72%, June 2019 at 2.83%/2.76% vs 2.80%/2.76%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to remain higher as bluechips buoyed by Wall Street's rally Friday, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke; "We have tracked upwards all day today." Telecom (TEL.NZ) +2.5% at NZ$2.05, Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +1.3% at NZ$7.62, Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) +3.9% at NZ$2.92. Says Sky City (SKC.NZ) only large cap in red as investors wary about where growth potential due to come from. NZX-50 last +1.2% at 3042.79. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR down as local shares open higher, mirroring Asian indexes, says trader with private bank. Pair at 46.79 vs 46.90 late Friday. Adds, gains in EUR and GBP against greenback on improving risk appetite also lending support to local unit. But, "pair may find support around 46.65-46.70 levels and oil companies may buy USD." Trader tips pair in 46.65-46.85 band in session. Sensex +1.0% at 18,181.65. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] National Bank of NZ's Business Outlook survey highlights fragile recovery, says NZ \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Strategist Bernard Doyle; "the turnaround in sentiment over the past quarter has underlined the fragility of momentum in this economy." Says survey underscores why RBNZ should not be raising interest rates. Says only rationale given weak tone of data would be concerns around inflationary pressure but "the fact that pricing intentions have stabilised ahead of a Goods and Services Tax increase is indicative of contained pricing pressure in our view." Says hike on Sept. 16 unlikely, expects market pricing to further marginalize the likelihood of this outcome. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +1.0% at 18,183.66, tracking gains in global markets, after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke Friday says he is ready to do what it takes to support economic recovery and on better-than-expected revision of second-quarter U.S. economic growth. "A slightly better than expected U.S. GDP data and some inspiring talk by Ben Bernanke lifted the sagging spirits on Wall Street and elsewhere," Brokerage \uc2 India Infoline\uc2  says. But advises investors to be cautious as global situation still looks precarious. "At best, what we will see is a relief rally. Things may soon turn choppy and range-bound." Local dealer tips resistance at 18,300. Index up broadly with 29 out of 30 issues trading higher. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +3.1% at INR526.15, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) +2.0% at INR976.80, \uc2 Larsen & Toubro\uc2  (500510.BY) +1.2% at INR1,855.05, ONGC (500312.BY) +1.4% at INR1,336.65. S&P CNX Nifty +0.9% at 5459.45. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0348 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ's announced easing measures were "completely in line with market expectation, so I doubt there will be enough impact to push the JGB yields down, especially in the two-year and five-year zones," says RuiXue Xu, rates strategist at RBS Securities Japan. Therefore, "The BOJ step may not be able to weaken the yen much," she says. Adds JGB markets not showing big reaction at start of afternoon session "because investors had completely factored in the announced steps." Lead 10-year September JGB futures last at 142.27, down 0.28 while 10-year yield up 4.5bps at 1.045%, 2-year at 0.130%(up 0.5bps), 5-year at 0.300% (up 2bps.)(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com) [ 08-30-10 0014ET ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0414 GMT incorrectly stated the name of the BOJ Governor. The correct version follows:) JPY rises vs EUR, USD as non-Japan hedge funds, Japan interbank players lock in earlier gains as BOJ's monetary policy "came without surprises," while Nikkei trims gains, denting risk-tolerance, says senior trader at Europe bank. "While views linger that the BOJ's action came from the government's pressure, BOJ Gov. Shirakawa won't likely say something meaningful at his press conference" from 0530 GMT, which might limit any JPY moves, trader says. Adds, JPY may strengthen more in coming days if U.S. economic reports, such as today's personal spending, Friday's non-farm payrolls data, come in weaker than expected, darkening outlook for economy; but USD/JPY might not fall sharply down road as impact of BOJ monetary easing may take effect, expectations of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loom. USD/JPY's support at 85.00 vs 85.40; EUR/JPY's downside at 108.70 vs 108.80. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia's July exports may rise 12.1% on-year vs +17.2% in June, according to median forecast of 13 economists in Dow Jones poll. "The continued moderation in growth rate should be quite marked in July to reinforce our view that Malaysia's export growth peaked in the first quarter and we are heading for a less rosy second half," says Standard Chartered economist Alvin Liew. Action Economics' David Cohen says clouding outlook for 2H will be concerns over economic growth in U.S., cooling off in China. Imports likely +18.6% on-year vs +30.1% in June, trade surplus likely at MYR6.1 billion ($1.95 billion) for the month vs MYR6.04 billion in June, according to poll. Data due 1001 GMT Thursday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0415 GMT [Dow Jones] Economic impact of BOJ's latest monetary easing will be "close to zero," says Macquarie economist Richard Jerram; says BOJ holding "emergency" policy meeting to decide on the steps in absence of any fresh economic information to act on suggests political pressure forced the move; "It's largely a charade," he says; Normally, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  independence considered a good thing in most countries, but in Japan's case, where BOJ has let deflation eat away at the economy for so long, political pressure to do something to stop it may not be so bad; "You can argue that BOJ independence hasn't served Japan well over the past 12-15 years," he says. "You can expect further moves to appease the politicians." (arran.scott@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY rises vs EUR, USD as non-Japan hedge funds, Japan interbank players lock in earlier gains as BOJ's monetary policy "came without surprises," while Nikkei trims gains, denting risk-tolerance, says senior trader at Europe bank. "While views linger that the BOJ's action came from the government's pressure, BOJ Gov. Shiraka won't likely say something meaningful at his press conference" from 0530 GMT, which might limit any JPY moves, trader says. Adds, JPY may strengthen more in coming days if U.S. economic reports, such as today's personal spending, Friday's non-farm payrolls data, come in weaker than expected, darkening outlook for economy; but USD/JPY might not fall sharply down road as impact of BOJ monetary easing may take effect, expectations of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loom. USD/JPY's support at 85.00 vs 85.40; EUR/JPY's downside at 108.70 vs 108.80. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up 2.1% at 9182.13 vs morning close of 9265.39, trimming gains as market disappointed with BOJ easing measures, USD/JPY also falling, Nikkei may hover near 9200, say market analysts. "The market was expecting something more drastic, like monetary easing in quantitative terms," sa