o its ability to address JPY appreciation, which is a very public concern," Nomura says; notes that Ozawa's faction includes several members of the DPJ's radical anti-deflation lawmakers, who advocate revising the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Law. Warns that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now may happen above the previous suspected trigger at the Y82-Y83 area. JPY/KRW last at 14.02 vs 14.00 before BOJ announcement, AUD/JPY at 76.70 vs 77.39, USD/JPY at 85.39 vs 85.86. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD, AUD, GBP falling vs JPY, taking cue from falls in USD/JPY, EUR/JPY, where non-Japan hedge funds taking a lead, says senior dealer at major non-Japanese bank in Tokyo. Says "announced measures look a bid odd and are not as aggressive as we had expected," he says. Adds, hedge funds may keep buying JPY ahead "to test the government's will to see if they would really go ahead and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he says. NZD/JPY last 60.72, may fall to 60.50. AUD/JPY last at 76.74, may fall to 76.50. GBP/JPY at 132.60, may fall to 132.40.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely to rise to 4800 at year end and to 5400 at the end of 2011, says CommSec. "While Aussie companies are in good shape, we remain cautious on prospects for the ASX 200. The Australian share market is still tracking other international markets, in particular the U.S. Foreign investors are also inclined to look for growth opportunities or turnaround stories. In Australia the coming year looks to be more of the same--the economy getting back to 'normal' growth with 'normal' interest rates and 'normal' profit growth. Overall these are hardly the type of conditions that would prompt investors to take big bets on Australia." Index last up 1.5% at 4437.1. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bond prices edge lower on mild profit-taking by interbank players after recent steep gains. Capital inflows that boosted bond demand in recent weeks seem to have faded this morning, though most don't expect foreign players to rush out of Thai bonds just yet. "The capital flows will be key determinate for the market's direction. Should there be further inflows, which is fairly likely, then yields could continue to head further south," says trader. Yields expected to move in just few bps range rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.68%/2.65% vs 2.66%/2.63% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.77%/2.74% vs 2.75%/2.72%, June 2019 at 2.83%/2.76% vs 2.80%/2.76%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to remain higher as bluechips buoyed by Wall Street's rally Friday, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Craigs Investment Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  broker Bryon Burke; "We have tracked upwards all day today." Telecom (TEL.NZ) +2.5% at NZ$2.05, Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +1.3% at NZ$7.62, Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) +3.9% at NZ$2.92. Says Sky City (SKC.NZ) only large cap in red as investors wary about where growth potential due to come from. NZX-50 last +1.2% at 3042.79. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR down as local shares open higher, mirroring Asian indexes, says trader with private bank. Pair at 46.79 vs 46.90 late Friday. Adds, gains in EUR and GBP against greenback on improving risk appetite also lending support to local unit. But, "pair may find support around 46.65-46.70 levels and oil companies may buy USD." Trader tips pair in 46.65-46.85 band in session. Sensex +1.0% at 18,181.65. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com) [ 08-30-10 0019ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100830e68u00033\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichg