\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: JPY  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Be Next - Nomura\b0\par\par\uc2 1,658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 August 2010\par 06:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to drive JPY lower may happen sooner than previously anticipated, Nomura says, following BOJ easing measures just announced. Reckons that BOJ's expansion of fixed-rate loan program from JPY20T to JPY30T and extension of their duration from three to six months are in line with market consensus, but more can be done as politics appear to key consideration since PM Kan and former Chief Secretary General Ozawa will vie for the DPJ presidency Sep 14. "The Kan administration wants to appeal to its ability to address JPY appreciation, which is a very public concern," Nomura says; notes that Ozawa's faction includes several members of the DPJ's radical anti-deflation lawmakers, who advocate revising the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Law. Warns that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now may happen above the previous suspected trigger at the Y82-Y83 area. JPY/KRW last at 14.02 vs 14.00 before BOJ announcement, AUD/JPY at 76.70 vs 77.39, USD/JPY at 85.39 vs 85.86. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0404 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD, AUD, GBP falling vs JPY, taking cue from falls in USD/JPY, EUR/JPY, where non-Japan hedge funds taking a lead, says senior dealer at major non-Japanese bank in Tokyo. Says "announced measures look a bid odd and are not as aggressive as we had expected," he says. Adds, hedge funds may keep buying JPY ahead "to test the government's will to see if they would really go ahead and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he says. NZD/JPY last 60.72, may fall to 60.50. AUD/JPY last at 76.74, may fall to 76.50. GBP/JPY at 132.60, may fall to 132.40.(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely to rise to 4800 at year end and to 5400 at the end of 2011, says CommSec. "While Aussie companies are in good shape, we remain cautious on prospects for the ASX 200. The Australian share market is still tracking other international markets, in particular the U.S. Foreign investors are also inclined to look for growth opportunities or turnaround stories. In Australia the coming year looks to be more of the same--the economy getting back to 'normal' growth with 'normal' interest rates and 'normal' profit growth. Overall these are hardly the type of conditions that would prompt investors to take big bets on Australia." Index last up 1.5% at 4437.1. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0400 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bond prices edge lower on mild profit-taking by interbank players after recent steep gains. Capital inflows that boosted bond demand in recent weeks seem to have faded this morning, though most don't expect foreign players to rush out of Thai bonds just yet. "The capital flows will be key determinate for the market's direction. Should there be further inflows, which is fairly likely, then yields could continue to head further south," says trader. Yields expected to move in just few bps range rest of day. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.68%/2.65% vs 2.66%/2.63% late Friday, November 2016 at 2.77%/2.74% vs 2.75%/2.72%, June 2019 at 2.83%/2.76% vs 2.80%/2.76%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely to remain higher as bluechips buoyed by Wall Street's rally Friday, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Bryon Burke; "We have tracked upwards all day today." Telecom (TEL.NZ) +2.5% at NZ$2.05, Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) +1.3% at NZ$7.62, Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ) +3.9% at NZ$2.92. Says Sky City (SKC.NZ) only large cap in red as investors wary about where growth potential due to come from. NZX-50 last +1.2% at 3042.79. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR down as local shares open higher, mirroring Asian indexes, says trader with private bank. Pair at 46.79 vs 46.90 late Friday. Adds, gains in EUR and GBP against greenback on improving risk appetite also lending support to local unit. But, "pair may find support around 46.65-46.70 levels and oil companies may buy USD." Trader tips pair in 46.65-46.85 band in session. Sensex +1.0% at 18,181.65. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0353 GMT [Dow Jones] National Bank of NZ's Business Outlook survey highlights fragile recovery, says NZ \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  Strategist Bernard Doyle; "the turnaround in sentiment over the past quarter has underlined the fragility of momentum in this economy." Says survey underscores why RBNZ should not be raising interest rates. Says only rationale given weak tone of data would be concerns around inflationary pressure but "the fact that pricing intentions have stabilised ahead of a Goods and Services Tax increase is indicative of contained pricing pressure in our view." Says hike on Sept. 16 unlikely, expects market pricing to further marginalize the likelihood of this outcome. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +1.0% at 18,183.66, tracking gains in global markets, after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke Friday says he is ready to do what it takes to support economic recovery and on better-than-expected revision of second-quarter U.S. economic growth. "A slightly better than expected U.S. GDP data and some inspiring talk by Ben Bernanke lifted the sagging spirits on Wall Street and elsewhere," Brokerage \uc2 India Infoline\uc2  says. But advises investors to be cautious as global situation still looks precarious. "At best, what we will see is a relief rally. Things may soon turn choppy and range-bound." Local dealer tips resistance at 18,300. Index up broadly with 29 out of 30 issues trading higher. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) +3.1% at INR526.15, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) +2.0% at INR976.80, \uc2 Larsen & Toubro\uc2  (500510.BY) +1.2% at INR1,855.05, ONGC (500312.BY) +1.4% at INR1,336.65. S&P CNX Nifty +0.9% at 5459.45. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0348 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ's announced easing measures were "completely in line with market expectation, so I doubt there will be enough impact to push the JGB yields down, especially in the two-year and five-year zones," says RuiXue Xu, rates strategist at RBS Securities Japan. Therefore, "The BOJ step may not be able to weaken the yen much," she says. Adds JGB markets not showing big reaction at start of afternoon session "because investors had completely factored in the announced steps." Lead 10-year September JGB futures last at 142.27, down 0.28 while 10-year yield up 4.5bps at 1.045%, 2-year at 0.130%(up 0.5bps), 5-year at 0.300% (up 2bps.)(takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/JPY under pressure after BOJ announcement; pair last 60.79 vs 61.15 ahead of announcement. \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sue Trinh tips initial support at 60.50 after BOJ "outcome has proved most underwhelming." After emergency meeting, BOJ decides to newly lend Y10 trillion of 6-months funds, lengthening duration of low interest-rate loans available to money market; BOJ started offering 3-month loans in December. Trinh says Asian session game of two halves, with early short covering of USD/JPY and cross yen after news BOJ had called unscheduled meeting. Says equity markets gained amid hopes strong JPY would be addressed, but "all of that was turned on its head." Says BOJ delivered pretty much bare minimum and "is unlikely to keep JPY strength at bay." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0345 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY rises sharply vs EUR, USD as "the BOJ's monetary easing results (at its emergency meeting) were in line with market expectations, causing short-term players to take profits," Mitsuru Sahara, a senior dealer at the \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. BOJ to newly lend Y10 trillion of 6-months funds, lengthening duration of low interest-rate loans available to money market; BOJ started offering 3-month loans in December. "There were slight hopes for the BOJ's to take some radical measures," such as government bond purchases, which led to some selling on disappointment stronger measures not taken, Sahara added. USD/JPY down nearly 40 sen at 85.43, EUR/JPY falls almost 60 sen at 108.85. Players now await BOJ Gov. Shirakawa's press conference from 0530 GMT. USD/JPY may fall to 85.30, EUR/JPY may decline to 108.50.(miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0343 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ decides, at emergency board meeting, to loosen monetary policy further by pumping more cash, newly lending extra Y10 trillion of 6-month funds into market at rock-bottom rates, stepping up effort to fight deflation. Bank's policy board says BOJ will lend another Y10 trillion in 6-month cash at fixed rate to banks; move aimed at lowering longer term-fund rates to shrink interest-rate gap between U.S., Japan and to decrease attractiveness of JPY; may not change market view that anemic economic recovery will shift gears up, or aid Japanese officials' efforts in capping yen surge. In March, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doubled loan amount to Y20 trillion from Y10 trillion offered in December. BOJ board members vote 8 to 1 to expand scope of fund-supply \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Board member Suda disagrees with decision. Bank also leaves unsecured overnight call loan rate unchanged at 0.1%. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 08-30-10 0005ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100830e68u0002