\par\par \b \uc2 Sources: BOJ May Hold Emergency Meeting As Early As Monday\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megumi Fujikawa and Tomoyuki Tachikawa  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 1,119 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 August 2010\par 04:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan will likely hold an emergency policy board meeting early this week to discuss additional monetary easing measures aimed at slowing the steep rise of the yen, said people familiar with the situation.\par \par \uc2 Officials are worried that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent acceleration is clouding the outlook for the export-dependant Japanese economy and starting to hurt business sentiment, these people said.\par \par \uc2 Any move would likely be timed to come out about the same time as new government fiscal stimulus measures, in order to maximize the impact on market players looking for signs that Japan is serious about policies and addressing the yen's impact. "It's important [for the BOJ] to coordinate with the government's action," one of the people said, referring to Prime Minister Naoto Kan's plans to announce additional measures to try to offset the impact of the surging yen on the domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 Japanese finance officials have also hinted in recent days that they may consider \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to halt the yen's rise. Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Saturday he will take "decisive" steps against the yen if it becomes necessary--a word often used by officials in the past to signal that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be imminent.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to move sooner than its scheduled meeting on Sept. 6-7, with the prime minister meeting with BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa as soon as the BOJ chief returns from his trip to the U.S. Shirakawa attended the Jackson Hole, Wyo. economic policy symposium held by the Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 BOJ policy board members might get together as early as Monday when the governor is slated to return to Japan, or Tuesday when the government is scheduled to announce an outline of the possible stimulus measures. The stimulus package is expected to draw on Y920 billion in a reserve fund from the current budget, aiming at creating more jobs and demand. If more is needed, the government may consider a supplementary budget bill to use Y800 billion surplus from the previous fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 But lawmakers have also vowed to keep the package limited to that modest level and to avoid doing anything that would require issuing new bonds and further expanding Japan's mammoth outstanding debt.\par \par \uc2 Given the urgency of the situation and limited policy options from the fiscal side, politicians have stepped up pressure on the BOJ in the past few days. The finance minister said during meetings in Beijing Saturday that "specifics of monetary policy should be considered by the BOJ, but there is room to be creative in using monetary policy to help improve the current [economic] situation. The prime minister said Friday he expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to "flexibly implement monetary policy."\par \par \uc2 BOJ officials are also worried that the yen's steep rise may damage sentiment in the corporate sector and drag on Japan's already decelerating economic recovery. In a survey of 200 firms released Friday, the Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry found 65% of large manufacturers said that their profits have been hurt by the yen's strength against the dollar, up from 16% in a previous survey in May.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been saying that the domestic economy is showing signs of moderate recovery thanks to a pick-up in export and industrial production, but some officials lately expressed more caution due to instability in financial markets, as well as uncertain overseas economic outlook. Rocked by the strong yen, the Nikkei Stock Average last week fell to a 16-month low, dropping below the 9000 level.\par \par \uc2 The greenback fell to as low as Y83.58 early last week as the lack of action by the Japanese government against the rising yen disappointed the market, with concerns over the slowing U.S. economy also weighing on the dollar. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recovered some ground afterwards and stood around Y85.23 as of 0230 GMT Sunday.\par \par \uc2 For this time, an expansion of a three-month 0.1% emergency loan facility, which the BOJ set up in December and doubled in March, would be "the easiest choice" for the BOJ to loosen monetary conditions, one person familiar with BOJ officials' thinking told Dow Jones Newswires. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could consider increasing the supply of three-month funds from the current Y20 trillion, or extending the duration of such loans to six-months.\par \par \uc2 A flood of low-rate funds into the market would send term-fund rates, such as interbank rates, for the yen lower. In theory, if Japanese rates are significantly lower than those of other countries, that would reduce the attractiveness of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and cap its rise.\par \par \uc2 In particular, speculation lingers that the Federal Reserve may ease monetary policy further to shore up the flagging economy--which could narrow the interest-rate gap between the U.S. and Japan and could once again place upward pressure on the yen against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke said Friday the Fed "will continue to monitor economic developments closely and to evaluate whether additional monetary easing would be beneficial," and the Federal Open Market Committee "is prepared to provide additional monetary accommodation through unconventional measures if it proves necessary, especially if the outlook were to deteriorate significantly."\par \par \uc2 Thus, an expansion of the three-month loan scheme alone may not be enough to satisfy the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be forced to take other, more aggressive steps.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ may also have to increase the amount of Japanese government bonds it buys from the financial market and eventually cut its policy interest-rate target from the current 0.1%--the level it has been since December 2008, said another person familiar with BOJ officials' thinking.\par \par \uc2 BOJ officials have long opposed the idea of increasing the size of their JGB buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 --which is regarded as aggressive by many market players--because of worries that it could increase market concerns about Japan's fiscal discipline and may undermine the credibility of Japan's government bonds in the long term.\par \par \uc2 They have also repeatedly said that the bank's hands are tied by the so-called banknote rule---which limits the BOJ's JGB buying to the amount of banknotes in circulation. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has space of around Y20 trillion to purchase long-term bonds, according to the bank's latest account data.\par \par \uc2 -By Megumi Fujikawa and Tomoyuki Tachikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com [ 08-28-10 2253ET ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020100829e68t00004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
