\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Japan Official: Japan Conveys Hope For More Flexible Yuan In Talks With China\b0\par\par\uc2 206 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2010\par 15:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 28 Aug 2010 09:38 EDT DJ  Japan Official: Japan Conveys Hope For More Flexible Yuan In Talks With China\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Takashi Nakamichi \par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--Japan conveyed its desire to see a more flexible yuan during its annual high-level economic dialogue with China held Saturday, a Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official said.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese delegation, which included Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, "made clear its expectations that [China] will steadily move forward with efforts to make [the yuan] flexible" through Beijing's new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system, the Japanese official said at a news briefing.\par \par \uc2 The comments could add to speculation that even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the yen's rise, it may not go so far as to try to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  near particular levels for a long time through constant yen-selling.\par \par \uc2 Otherwise, Tokyo could face criticism from the international community for manipulating the exchange rates just like China, despite its calls for a less controlled Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7552; Takashi.Nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 28, 2010 09:38 ET (13:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100828e68s0002r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
