\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ 2nd UPDATE: Japan Finance Chief Pledges 'Decisive' Yen Action\b0\par\par\uc2 656 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2010\par 17:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from Japan's finance minister on the Chinese yuan, more background.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     By Takashi Nakamichi \par     Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 BEIJING (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister threatened "decisive" steps against the rising yen Saturday, hinting more strongly at market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the government faces growing public calls for action to halt the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's growth-damaging upward trend.\par \par \uc2 Yoshihiko Noda also turned up the heat on the Bank of Japan to further ease its monetary policy, the latest indication the government wants a concerted effort with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fight the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 His comments demonstrate how serious the government has become to try to halt the yen's advance, which economists say could snuff out Japan's fragile economic recovery by hurting its exports and exacerbating deflation. The dollar plummeted to a 15-year low of Y83.58 Tuesday--close to its record low of Y79.75 hit in 1995--causing Japan's stock prices to hit a 16-month low in the following day.\par \par \uc2 "We will monitor the market with great interest, and we will take decisive steps when necessary," Noda told reporters on the sidelines of a one-day annual economic dialogue of Japanese and Chinese government ministers.\par \par \uc2 The yen's strength is "starting to have a significant impact" on Japan's economy such as driving lower the nation's stock market, he said. "We must implement every policy available," he added.\par \par \uc2 The wording "decisive"--Noda's strongest language yet--was often used by Japanese officials in the past to signal that market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was imminent. Any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be the first since March 2004, when Tokyo ended a 15-month campaign that dumped an unprecedented Y35 trillion to curb the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 In a move unusual for the head of the Japanese government, Prime Minister Naoto Kan also pledged "decisive" action versus the yen Friday, highlighting the administration's heightened resolve to deal with \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  problems.\par \par \uc2 Even if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen, however, its impact could be limited. That's partly because Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -policy makers would likely aim at only smoothing sharp market moves rather than changing the yen's overall trend even if they ever \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said people familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking. The chance of the U.S. or Europe joining Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is also slim, they said.\par \par \uc2 That Noda urged China to allow the yuan to trade more freely in a separate news conference late Saturday is a fresh indication that Japan has no plans to run an extended campaign to prop up the dollar against the yen. Such a sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be tantamount to China's attempts to prevent the yuan's gains, the very policy Noda and his U.S. and European counterparts criticize.\par \par \uc2 During the economic talks with Chinese representatives, "I made remarks that I am expecting [China] to steadily move ahead" with efforts to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible in line with the mainland's new \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system announced in June, Noda said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have hardened their stance against the yen's gains recently as the country's business managers and major newspapers increasingly blame the government's lack of action to tame the yen rise. With the number of ruling Democratic Party of Japan lawmakers worried about the yen's strength growing, Kan is also hard-pressed to prove his ability to deal with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue ahead of the DPJ's planned presidential race on Sept. 14. He would compete with former party secretary-general Ichiro Ozawa in the election, and the winner is expected to become the prime minister because of the DPJ's dominance of the powerful Lower House of Parliament.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6895-7552; Takashi.Nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 28, 2010 11:52 ET (15:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100828e68s0008l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
