\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-Japan's Noda says ready to use all measures on yen\b0\par\par\uc2 521 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 August 2010\par 08:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Finmin says BOJ could do more through monetary policy\par \par \uc2 * Echoes PM's pledge to act decisively on yen when needed\par \par \uc2 * Says told China he wants steady progress on yuan reform\par \par \uc2 (Adds comments about Japan seeking yuan reform)\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2 BEIJING, Aug 28 (Reuters) - Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said on Saturday he was ready to employ "all possible measures" to tackle the soaring yen, which was having a big impact on the country's export-led economy.\par \par \uc2 Speaking in China's capital, Noda told reporters he wanted the Bank of Japan to guide policy while sharing its views on the economy with the government.\par \par \uc2 He also said there was room for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to improve the monetary situation, but it was up to the bank to consider specific policy steps.\par \par \uc2 The yen hit a 15-year high against the dollar this week, and Tokyo is scrambling to craft a package of steps to prop up its stuttering economy while keeping up pressure on the BOJ to do more to pull the country out of deflation.\par \par \uc2 The yen, which surged to 83.58 per dollar on Tuesday, was hovering just above 85 on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 "Many people are worried and the government is taking a serious view, and we must adopt all possible measures," Noda said, after meeting Chinese Finance Minister Xie Xuren on the sidelines of a Japan-China economic dialogue.\par \par \uc2 Noda urged China to make its yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible and said Japan would announce its basic policy on an economic package next week.\par \par \uc2 "We will take decisive measures when necessary while watching market trends with great interest," Noda said, echoing comments the previous day from Prime Minister Naoto Kan that signalled possible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Noda also said he expected Kan to meet BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa as soon as possible after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief returns from the United States on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ may hold an emergency meeting early next week to ease monetary policy, a source familiar with the matter said [ID:nSGE67Q0J1]\par \par \uc2 China, meanwhile, should continue making "steady efforts" to make its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  more flexible, Noda told reporters. The yuan has appreciated about 0.4 percent against the dollar since it was lifted from a nearly two-year peg on June 19. [CNY/]\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market since March 2004, when a 15-month yen-selling spree came to an end. During that period, they sold 35 trillion yen ($410 billion), the equivalent of more than one-third of the annual budget.\par \par \uc2 Despite the risk of possible action by Japanese authorities to curb yen strength, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or monetary easing, some traders and investors say the yen could still test a record high of 79.75 yen to the dollar, hit in April 1995.\par \par \uc2 ($1=85.37 Yen)\par \par \uc2 (Reporting by Tetsushi Kajimoto; Editing by Nick Macfie, Ken Wills, John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/CHINA (UPDATE 3)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|Z|J|ABX|BNX|FUN|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100828e68s00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
