\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Would Face Powerful Cross Currents\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren and Karen Johnson  \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \uc2 771 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2010\par 18:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--If the Bank of Japan does dive into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to try to quell strength in the yen, it will find itself struggling against some strong cross currents.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is viewed as the most powerful weapon in a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's arsenal, but considering the forces pushing the yen higher, classic \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  through selling the yen and buying the dollar may not pack enough of a punch to hold the yen down.\par \par \uc2 Direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is by no means a given, but it is seeming more of a real possibility as Japanese officials continue to escalate their language on yen strength. Early this week, the dollar fell to a 15-year low at Y83.57, perilously close to its historical low of 79.75 hit in April 1995. The dollar was trading Friday around Y85, but is having difficulty forging higher, despite jawboning from Japanese leaders.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Friday, a new round of rhetorical fire from Prime Minister Naoto Kan reinforced the possibility of action of some kind against yen appreciation.\par \par \uc2 "We will take decisive action when necessary," Kan told reporters.\par \par \uc2 It still isn't clear if direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will figure in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's response to its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength, or whether it will resort to other methods such as increased quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 Should the BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it will have to contend with a number of powerful market forces that are keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  buoyant. The significance of those forces is reflected in one telling fact: In the week ending Aug. 20, total capital exports from Japan totaled Y1241.4 billon yen, according to a report from Jessica Hoversen, fixed-income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Even in the face of those outflows, coming at a time of year when there is usually a net inflow to Japan, the yen has managed to rally.\par \par \uc2 "While normally this level of capital exporting would be bearish for the yen, it has had the opposite effect. USD/JPY continues to make new lows despite the fact that capital continues to leave the country," Hoversen's report says.\par \par \uc2 Hoversen cites two key reasons for the yen's buoyancy.\par \par \uc2 One is Chinese buying of the yen. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated in June that the Chinese increased their stockpiling of yen by about $5.3 billion, Hoversen's report says.\par \par \uc2 Chinese interest in the yen could be a powerful force supporting the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Hoversen said in an interview. "China has very deep pockets," she said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedging by Japanese investors buying foreign bonds is another factor that offsets the impact of capital outflows from Japan on the yen, Hoversen said.\par \par \uc2 There are other potential flows that could provide support to the yen and offset the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One is the likelihood of further Japanese divestment of bonds from troubled economies in the periphery of the euro zone, a factor identified in a report from \uc2 UBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Japanese institutional investors are clearly conscious of the unfolding debt crisis in the Eurozone and have not been slow to rebalance their portfolios away from fiscally challenged sovereigns," \uc2 UBS\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "Portfolio adjustment flows have already been sizable, and cumulative net sales of Greek debt since January have totalled Y0.7 trillion," \uc2 UBS\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "Approximately Y1.5 trillion of peripheral sovereign debt remains in Japanese hands, two-thirds of which is of Irish origin," it said. "That makes S&P's downgrade of Ireland's credit rating especially significant," \uc2 UBS\uc2  added.\par \par \uc2 Some of the funds realized through sales of peripheral bonds would likely be switched into Bunds, but a significant portion of what remains would most likely be repatriated, adding to appreciation pressures on the yen, it said.\par \par \uc2 "With the [\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] and the BoJ already struggling to restrain the yen's advance, the timing could hardly be less convenient," \uc2 UBS\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 (Don Curren, a reporter based in Toronto, has covered Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and fixed income markets for more than 10 years. He can be reached at 416-306-2020 or at don.curren@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 08-27-10 1220ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141  \par \p