\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ MONUMENT SECURITIES: Japan's limited options\b0\par\par\uc2 By Stephen Lewis  \par \uc2 1,207 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2010\par 14:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2    Of Monument Securities \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The global economy may still be expanding but, for many of the advanced countries, growth is falling short of policymakers' projections. The disappointment appears to be felt most acutely in Japan where, according to the 2010Q2 GDP report, the recovery came to a virtual halt in that quarter.\f28 \par \par \uc2 With the yen appreciating significantly, prospects for Japanese corporate earnings are not as bright as they once seemed. Japan's Democratic Party government, headed by Mr Kan, was weakened by a poor showing in last month's Upper House elections. It cannot afford mishaps in the economy, especially as Mr Kan faces a stiff challenge to his position as leader in a party vote due to take place on 14 September.\par \par \uc2 Another change of premier would be the sixth in four years and would hardly boost confidence in Japan's political stability. Japan's authorities, however, are finding it is far from obvious what measures they could take to stimulate their economy. They appear to have run out of road in terms of what they can do, fiscally or momentarily, to take downward pressure off demand. They are unlikely to be alone in facing this problem. When policymakers in other advanced countries feel the challenge to step up demand in their economies, they, too, are likely to come up against constraints on their freedom of action.\par \par \uc2 Mr Kan has said that his Government will decide economic stimulus measures on 31 August. It is possible that, on the fiscal front, he and his colleagues may see their way to extending incentives for the purchase of 'green' goods. This may be presented as part of a policy aimed at tackling climate change. Given the scale of the pre-existing budget deficit, the amount of stimulus to be delivered through this channel is likely to be modest.\par \par \uc2 The Kan Government would be much relieved if the Bank of Japan (BoJ) could find a way to shoulder the main part of the burden of boosting economic demand. This is probably how best to interpret ministers' repeated assurances that the Government and the BoJ will work closely together on economic policy. They are leaning on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while wishing to avoid giving the appearance they are doing so. But, in practical terms, there is not much further scope for BoJ action along the lines it has adopted so far.\par \par \uc2 The BoJ could increase the scale of its lending against collateral on fixed terms and it could extend the maturity of such loans to six months or a year. However, the yen money market rate curve is not at all steep at the moment, seeing that participants expect the BoJ to keep its policy rate at an ultra-low 0.1% for the foreseeable future. It is doubtful whether the few basis points reduction in 1-year deposit rates that an extension of BoJ lending might induce would make much difference to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr Shirakawa, the BoJ Governor, has consistently maintained that the kind of 'quantitative easing' in which the BoJ engaged in 2001-06 was ineffective. It is certainly hard to discern a link between expansion of the commercial banks' reserves at that time and their lending. Further, Japan's consumer prices fell by 1.2%, overall, in the five years from 2001 to 2006, whereas they had risen by 0.7% in the preceding five years. The BoJ's scepticism regarding 'quantitative easing' is understandable.\par \par \uc2 Also, it is wary about going too far in the direction of 'quantitative easing' (QE), as that term is understood elsewhere, that is, as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying of government securities, from fear it would look like monetisation of public sector debt. The danger that such an interpretation would gain sway is perhaps even greater in Japan, given the scale of its public debt, than in the USA and the UK.\par \par \uc2 Japan's authorities would probably not be talking about stimulus now if the yen had not strengthened recently. On the BoJ's effective rate, the yen is some 10% above its level at end-April. Admittedly, in real terms, the yen is still around 25% weaker than it was during the mid-1990s, reflecting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's negligible inflation in the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  period. What matters to policymakers in Tokyo, though, is the speed of this year's yen move, which is catching Japanese exporters off balance.\par \par \uc2 Consequently, a measure that would cap this strength, or preferably reverse some of it, would possibly be more effective in supporting Japan's economy than anything that could be achieved through conventional fiscal and monetary measures. This consideration gives credibility to ministers' assertions that they are prepared to make firm moves against a strong yen. However, for such comments to continue to be credible, they may eventually have to be backed by action.\par \par \uc2 There is little prospect of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's winning support for internationally co-ordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 fx intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold down the yen. Neither the USA nor the euro zone has an interest in seeing its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate. The logic of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's position leads in the direction of unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen and buy other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducted unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to depress the yen exchange rate in the period up to March 2004. On the whole, this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unsterilized. In other words, the BoJ did not sell securities in the domestic markets to mop up the yen deposits it had created through its dollar-buying.\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to resume, the BoJ would almost certainly leave it unsterilized. This would be a means of boosting domestic yen deposits without incurring the risks of being seen buying government securities. It would also help fend off charges from Japan's partners that it was manipulating its exchange rate.\par \par \uc2 In 2003 and 2004, the US Treasury found that Japan was not a '\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulator', on the grounds that its unsterilized \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased its money supply and was, therefore, part of a macroeconomic policy to combat deflation. However, we might observe that, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  occurred during the period when the BoJ was engaged in 'quantitative easing', the evidence adduced against the effectiveness of the latter policy could also be lodged against the monetary benefits of the \b \highlight22\uc2 fx intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dollar-buying would be no magic bullet for the BoJ.\par \par \uc2 -By Stephen Lewis: (+44) 20 7190 7193; sjlewis@monumentsecurities.com\par \par \uc2 (Stephen Lewis is chief economist at Monument Securities Ltd., London, independent brokers specializing in institutional business.)\par \par \uc2 Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.\par \par \uc2 This column is published for information only, and it neither constitutes, nor is to be construed as, an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources the author believes to be reliable, but he cannot represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd., employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this article.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 27, 2010 08:23 ET (12:23 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100827e68r002ha\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
