\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Stocks hold above 7-wk low before GDP, Bernanke\b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2010\par 04:21\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * MSCI world equity index unchanged\par \par \uc2 * Fed chair, US GDP awaited as growth concerns persist\par \par \uc2 * Oil lower; yen slips on speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Natsuko Waki\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 27 (Reuters) - World stocks held near a recent seven-week low on Friday while government bonds rose as investors braced for an update on the U.S. economic outlook from the country's top central banker following a run of weak data.\par \par \uc2 The yen slipped against the dollar as speculation persisted that Tokyo might take steps to stem export-damaging strength in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve chairman Ben Bernanke is expected to signal in a speech at 1400 GMT that the U.S. economy is facing uncertain prospects. The second estimate for second-quarter U.S. growth, due out before he speaks, is expected to show a slower rate of expansion than initially reported.\par \par \uc2 "We are going to have a bumpy couple of weeks, maybe even months, ahead. We need to have some clear evidence for the U.S. economy to turn the corner to get people to look at the equity market again," said Franz Wenzel, strategist at AXA Investment Managers in Paris. The MSCI world equity index was unchanged on the day at 278.18, off a seven-week low hit earlier this week. The index has lost four percent in August. The Thomson Reuters global stock index was also steady.\par \par \uc2 The FTSEurofirst 300 index was down 0.1 percent while emerging stocks dropped 0.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures rose around 0.4 percent, pointing to a slightly firmer open on Wall Street later.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil gained 0.4 percent to $73.66.\par \par \uc2 German Bund futures rose 44 ticks. The 10-year benchmark Bund yield lost almost one basis point to 2.141 percent, just above record lows of 2.092 percent set on Wednesday. The yield has fallen more than 50 basis points in August, the biggest monthly fall since November 2008.\par \par \uc2 The yen fell a quarter of a percent to 84.68 per dollar, off its 15-year high near 83.60 hit earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan said he will take firm measures on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when needed and will meet the Bank of Japan governor, increasing the possibility the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease policy soon as it confronts a surging yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was almost unchanged on the day against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Friday's second estimate for U.S. second-quarter growth is expected show the economy grew 1.4 percent last quarter, down from an initial reading of 2.4 percent.\par \par \uc2 "In light of weak data releases seen over the past three months, it seems no longer a question of if the Fed heads towards additional easing, but when," \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  said in a note to clients.\par \par \uc2 "Hence, the market reaction will be seen in the context of the Fed's willingness to operate ahead or behind the curve."\par \par \uc2 (Additional reporting by Atul Prakash; Editing by John Stonestreet)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL (WRAPUP 5)|LANGEN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|ABX|BNX|FUN|RNA|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100827e68r0005s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
