\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Sell JPY/KRW At 14.08, Target 12.95 - MS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,167 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2010\par 10:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0845 GMT [Dow Jones] \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  recommends selling JPY/KRW at 14.08, targeting 12.95, with a tight stop at 14.30. With JPY/KRW trading above 14.00, the rate is "extremely misaligned," on valuation basis as long term valuation models suggest fair value for pair to be at 11.70, it says in report; "The trade-weighted yen is close to all-time highs, and the risks are rising of further policy easing from the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as well as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . If the Fed does pursue more aggressive policy stimulus, it is perhaps likely to benefit the high-beta KRW." JPY/KRW at 14.1342 late Friday in Seoul. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] The 10-year gilt/bund yield spread crept wider Friday after the UK 2Q GDP preliminary estimate was revised up from +1.1% on the quarter and +1.6% on the year to +1.2% and +1.7%, respectively. The 10-year gilt bund yield spread is up 1bp on the day at 76bps, compared to 74.5bps before the data. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0841 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP/USD is some 20 ticks lower than levels seen just before the UK 2Q GDP was upwardly revised to +1.2% Q/Q from +1.1%. It now trades at 1.5512 from around 1.5535, however the dollar is also a tad higher across the board which probably explains this move. EUR/USD trades at 1.2716, USD/JPY is at the session high of 84.80. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0838 GMT [Dow Jones] Essay by Bank of China chairman Xiao Gang may lead to reassessment of causes of Chinese banks' lending binge over last 18 months, says Heard on the Street. Xiao says banks weren't ordered by government to churn out loans to state-owned enterprises; in fact this was entirely rational behavior for banks, since SOEs have implicit state guarantee and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  mandates profitable spread between lending, deposit rates; all government had to do was raise its annual lending limits, and banks were ready to go. The downside, says Xiao, is that this doesn't help China develop banking sector which can properly assess risk, or encourage lending to more risky SMEs; changing system to encourage this will be tricky. As Xiao concludes: "Things are very complicated, so will be the solutions." He can say that again. See: =HEARD ON THE STREET: Reassessing China's Lending Binge (andrew.peaple@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0836 GMT [Dow Jones] Gilts are stable despite UK 2Q GDP data being revised slightly higher. UK 2Q GDP growth revised to 1.2% quarter-on-quarter vs initial estimate of 1.1%. The on-quarter growth marks the fastest pace since 1Q 2001. September gilts are up 0.20 at 126.20. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0833 GMT [Dow Jones] The much awaited comments from Japan's PM Kan offered little new. He said firm measures would be taken on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when necessary, excessive \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves can have negative impact on financial stability and he expects the BOJ to take the necessary policy step and that he will meet BOJ governor Shirakawa on his return from Jackson Hole. USD/JPY has barely reacted to Kan's comments, trading at 84.63 from around 84.68. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0824 GMT [Dow Jones] The mysterious hand of an unusual northern European bank buyer in USD/JPY is back in the market early Friday after a brief absence, according to London-based traders. This account--not exactly a frequent player in this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pair--has been a major buyer in recent days, traders say. USD/JPY trades at 84.66. Japan PM Kan is due to speak about the strong yen shortly. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0823 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities tad lower as spot remains sluggish, but strong interest showing up on extreme front-end of curve as traders eye signals from Bernanke, Trichet at central banks symposium at Jackson Hole later in global day; 1-month ATMF at 7.0%/7.20% vs 6.80%/7.80% late Thursday. "The market will be watching particularly carefully for divergence between the ECB and the Fed, which would have major implications then for the EUR/USD," says trader with foreign bank. Notes, almost $80 million traded in 1-week ATMF so far, with early deals beginning at 6.60%, slowly pushed up to 7.0% as long gamma interest driven by event risk from Jackson Hole. More interest may be on wings. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian forint is hardly changed, continues to hover around 283 against the euro, currently at 282.90 versus 282.50 late Thursday. Uncertainty over the government's IMF plans is a negative, \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  says. Raiffeisen Bank expects the EUR/HUF to trade in the 281.85-286.70 range for the rest of the day. Investors' patience toward Hungary will probably last until October, when local elections and the 2011 budget are due. Until then, the forint will likely weaken a bit and trade around 285 against the euro as global economic indicators are expected to deteriorate, Raiffeisen adds. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0815 GMT [Dow Jones] Both the UK and US deliver revised 2Q GDP numbers Friday. RBS is looking for 2Q UK GDP (083 0GMT) to be unrevised at 1.1% with some upside risks, while for the US 2Q revision it looks for growth to be halved to 1.2% from 2.4% on weaker inventories and trade. The US data hits the wires at 1230 GMT. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher late at 3.1400 vs 3.1388 Thursday; local dealer says pair tad higher but essentially "directionless" in thin volume. "Many investors are staying on the sidelines... although there are players expecting the ringgit to trend higher, concern about where the global economic recovery is headed is throwing in very mixed signals," says dealer. Adds, U.S. Fed Bernanke's speech later Friday eyed for direction. Tips 3.1350-3.1650 range Monday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0803 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish July retail sales, +0.5% on the month and +2% on the year, were fairly much in line with expectations, although revisions to the seasonal adjustment for July make the numbers a bit difficult to interpret, SEB says. "Indicators are a bit of a mixed bag with very confident consumers but some signs of less optimistic retailers," SEB says. "Still, we think that retail sales will continue to trend upwards going into 2011. EUR/SEK trades at 9.3861. (ian.edmondson@dowjones.com) [ 08-27-10 0445ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100827e68r0005h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
