\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(8/27) Heard On The Street: Tokyo Is Focused On The Wrong 'To Run'\b0\par\par\uc2 374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 19:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 28\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \par     By James Simms \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent days, Japan's media has been transfixed by two stories: The first, whether a political heavyweight will run in a contest to become the nation's next leader. The second, whether Tokyo can do anything to stop the nation's economy from grinding to a halt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Japan got its answer to both questions: Ichiro Ozawa will challenge Prime Minister Naoto Kan for control of the Democratic Party of Japan next month. That news ends any expectation of action by the current administration.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's ability to effectively respond to either an economic slowdown or the rising yen was always under a cloud with the party's Sept. 14 leadership vote looming. Any request by Mr. Kan to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Masaaki Shirakawa for a policy response might have been taken with a grain of salt until his leadership was affirmed.\par \par \uc2 But the battle between Mr. Ozawa and Mr. Kan means any finalization of a stimulus plan, which is expected to be at least $20 billion, is unlikely, and the government's ability to talk down the yen, which has been relatively effective so far, could be put to the test. On both fronts, Tokyo needs to persuade the Bank of Japan to come along, by enlarging its emergency-lending facility to banks and not removing liquidity generated by any \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 True, there's a case to be made that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  needn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets yet. And few beyond a speck of expensive Tokyo real estate should care about whether Mr. Ozawa wins his challenge. Mr. Kan, Japan's fifth prime minister in three years, has been in office for only three months, and the policy differences between the two, in the grand scheme of things, aren't notable.\par \par \uc2 Except for one thing: A campaign-finance scandal could lead to Mr. Ozawa's indictment, extending the leadership vacuum.\par \par \uc2 The situation ought to have someone reminding the Democrats that the verb "to run" has other meanings. How about running the country?\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 26, 2010 13:28 ET (17:28 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100826e68q004iz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
