\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Tokyo's Queer Definition of "To Run"\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Simms   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN   \par \uc2 484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 13:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 In recent days, Japan's media has been transfixed on two stories: The first, whether a political heavyweight will run in a contest to become the nation's next leader. The second, whether Tokyo can do anything to stop the nation's economy from grinding to a halt.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, Japan got its answer to both questions -- Ichiro Ozawa will challenge Prime Minister Naoto Kan for control of the Democratic Party of Japan in an internal contest next month. That news ends any expectation of action by the current administration.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo's ability to effectively respond to either an economic slowdown or the suddenly rising yen was always under a cloud, with the party's Sept. 14 leadership vote looming. Certainly any request by Kan to \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Masaaki Shirakawa for a policy response might've been taken with a grain of salt, until his leadership was affirmed.\par \par \uc2 But the Ozawa vs. Kan battle means any finalization of a promised stimulus plan, which is expected to be worth at least $20 billion, is unlikely, and the government's ability to talk down the yen -- which has been relatively effective so far -- could be put to the test by \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders. On both fronts, Tokyo needs to convince the Bank of Japan to come along by enlarging its emergency lending facility to banks and not removing, from the market, liquidity generated by a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 True, there's a case to be made that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  needn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets yet. And few outside of a speck of expensive Tokyo real estate should care about whether Ozawa wins his challenge. Kan, Japan's fifth prime minister in three years, has been in office for only three months, and the policy differences between the two, in the grand scheme of things, aren't notable.\par \par \uc2 Except for one thing: A campaign finance scandal could lead to Ozawa's indictment extending the leadership vacuum.\par \par \uc2 The current situation ought to have someone reminding the Democrats that the verb "to run" has other meanings. How about running the country?\par \par \uc2 (James Simms, a writer on the Heard on the Street team, has been a correspondent in Japan for 14 years, including over ten years at Dow Jones covering economics, politics and regulatory issues. He can be reached at 81-(0)3-6269-2799 or by e-mail: james.simms@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 26-08-10 1144GMT ]\par \par \uc2 5090   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100826e68q0014h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
