\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dovish Policy Talk Hits Dollar, Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 635 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 11:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Both the dollar and the yen are under pressure Thursday because of speculation that Japan and the U.S. might reveal plans for more monetary easing at the Jackson Hole central bankers' symposium at the end of this week.\par \par \uc2 Of the majors, the euro was the main beneficiary despite renewed fears of sovereign debt problems and an increase in the risk premiums associated with fiscally weaker members of the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro has rallied against the dollar:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4493"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4493}}}\par \par \uc2 For the moment, though, Jackson Hole is casting a long shadow over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the U.S., a string of disappointing economic data this week, including the latest new home sales and durable goods orders Wednesday, have left markets speculating that the U.S. will edge closer to extending its quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 This could well be signaled by Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke in his keynote speech, although it remains unclear whether Bernanke could command support for such a move because of a split among members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's open market committee.\par \par \uc2 "Fed Chairman Bernanke's Jackson Hole speech this Friday will therefore be critical," says Geoffrey Yu, a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with UBS AG in London.\par \par \uc2 The market is also speculating that Japanese officials will use the symposium to get endorsement for plans to ease Japanese monetary policy.\par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, as the dollar has fallen to Y85 and below, the Japanese government appears to have been at a loss over how to stop the yen's advance.\par \par \uc2 Although market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a possibility, with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling the yen, the authorities appear to be leaning more towards further unconventional monetary easing as a way to both reduce deflationary pressures that remain in the economy and weaken the yen at the same time.\par \par \uc2 This possibly dovish policy shift in both the U.S. and Japan is hurting the dollar and the yen for now, with the euro managing to post gains despite a rise in the cost of credit default swaps for Greece, Ireland and other fiscally weaker debtors as risk of a sovereign default keeps investors wary of exposing themselves too much to the region.\par \par \uc2 The euro was also able to get some support from the latest German consumer confidence survey by Gfk earlier Thursday, which rose to 4.1 for September from 4.0 in August. Also, despite the continued worries about the global recovery, there was a distinct recovery in risk appetite, with global equity markets staging a recovery after several days of losses.\par \par \uc2 European stock markets rose as much as 0.6%, following on from a 0.7% gain in the Nikkei and a 0.2% rise in the Dow Jones Industrial Average.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the euro had risen to $1.2701 from $1.2655 late Wednesday in New York, according to EBS. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also up at Y107.31 from Y107.20.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was a slightly lower at Y84.50, compared with Y84.70.\par \par \uc2 The dollar was also down at CHF1.0285 from CHF1.0291, while the pound rose sharply to $1.5540 from $1.5450.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 26-08-10 0925GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100826e68q000vq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
