\par\par \b \uc2 Bernanke, Trichet, and markets: looking to Jackson Hole\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Weir   \par \uc2 809 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 23:44\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Kevin Weir is a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market analyst for Reuters. The opinions expressed are his own.)\par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 26 (Reuters) - On Friday morning, Federal Reserve Ben Bernanke takes center stage at the Kansas City Fed's Economic Symposium in Jackson Hole, Wyoming for a speech on the economic outlook that will be closely watched by the markets.\par \par \uc2 He will be followed roughly two hours later by his counterpart at the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Jean-Claude Trichet.\par \par \uc2 This event in some years has produced policy initiatives and even candor from the speakers.\par \par \uc2 Bernanke takes the podium with significant challenges. He will be addressing an audience that is more jittery than usual about the state of the economy, worried the Fed has employed most of its weapons to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 Below is a snapshot of the major markets and how they might react, depending on the commentary. THE DOLLAR\par \par \uc2 The dollar index <.DXY>, while an imperfect proxy for overall dollar value, is used as by many equity managers as a shorthand look at sentiment. It is at present in a downtrend. Despite a recent rebound it has, on balance, been falling against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  dominated by the euro.\par \par \uc2 If Bernanke announces that his concern for the economy is such that the Fed will undertake more radical stimulus, the dollar seems likely to sell off. Since the ebbing of crisis conditions in Europe, traders have tended to focus on U.S. difficulties, a key reason for the dollar's decline.\par \par \uc2 Should Bernanke only restate the view recently reported in the Fed minutes, the dollar rebound of the past few sessions might encourage a new round of selling, but perhaps not with the vigor that might be generated by a more malign outlook. THE YEN AND EURO\par \par \uc2 Another market preoccupation is the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the yen by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The yen <JPY> has been so strong for such an extended period of time that despite recent profit-taking, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have some impact.\par \par \uc2 In order to maximize the scope of the move in response, it would be useful to have other central banks participate; the New York Fed's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk has acted in the past on behalf of the BoJ. If a non-Japanese central banker makes a reference to the possibility, the yen has a good chance to spike lower on the assumption that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is increasingly likely.\par \par \uc2 It may be worth noting that even concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could fail. In addition, the current Chinese enthusiasm for Japanese assets means that a conceivably large and probably persistent bid will exist regardless of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For the euro, market participants hope ECB President Trichet's speech will address the outlook for Europe in a way that could affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The general market view appears to be that he will be at pains to emphasize the positive. GOVERNMENT SECURITIES\par \par \uc2 Since the FOMC's most recent statements, the U.S. 10-year note's yield has dropped to 2.48 from 2.84 percent.\par \par \uc2 The decline has been surprising; many players expecting a solid recovery found themselves wrong-footed by the tendency of other investors to flock to Treasuries during periods of risk aversion. With equities perceived to be unacceptably volatile, and money market funds yielding virtually nothing, the Treasury market became, if only by default, a refuge.\par \par \uc2 Should Bernanke's speech indicate that additional quantitative easing is coming, distaste for other assets seems likely to keep investors flocking to bonds despite current low yields. A presentation emphasizing gradual recovery might, at current levels, generate some profit-taking in long positions.\par \par \uc2 One possible external factor in the FOMC's deliberations may be the attitude of China, which is likely to regard additional pledges of quantitative easing with distaste, despite the recent rally in government securities, which of course they hold in quantity. The U.S. government's need to sell bonds is ongoing, and presumably the opinions of large customers will at least be noted. EQUITIES\par \par \uc2 Stocks have been on the defensive for a large part of 2010. Any suggestion by Bernanke that conditions are such as to warrant aggressive additional easing is likely to be greeted by significant selling, particularly since volumes in late August are generally light, and bids are likely to be pulled when faced by a selling tsunami.\par \par \uc2 A "status quo" speech in the wake of an (expected) weak GDP update seems unlikely to do major incremental damage, but hardly seems likely to provoke much short-covering.\par \par \uc2 Trichet is unlikely to have much impact on U.S. stocks. (Additional reporting by IFR rates analyst Kenneth Logan; Editing by Eric Walsh) Reuters Messaging: kevin.weir.reuters.com@reuters.net\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-BERNANKE/SCENARIOS|LANGEN|ABN|M|D|J|Z|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100827e68q0000g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
