\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Daily Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 1,733 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 17:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Will Bernanke Throw A Curve Call? \par  \par    By Richard Barley \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market is bracing for QE2. Market expectations of more quantitative easing have led U.S. bond yields to fall sharply. Friday's speech from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke might include his latest thinking on asset purchases. But are investors thinking through the consequences, particularly for very long-dated bonds?\f28 \par \par \uc2 Both low rates and dismal data have forced yields at the short end of the Treasury curve to extremely low levels. The long end of the curve hasn't kept pace, despite this year's enormous gains in 30-year bonds. In fact, the gap between 10-year and 30-year bond yields, at just over 1 percentage point, is near a record and far above the long-run average of 0.2 point.\par \par \uc2 This can be explained in two ways. If the market believes the Fed will succeed in generating inflation, then 30-year yields should include a premium for this. But if the market believes even a renewed effort by the Fed might not help, that isn't necessarily reason for the gap to contract. The 30-year yield might start to contain a credit-risk premium due to the high level of government debt and deficits combined with low growth and low inflation or worse. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  argues that this phenomenon can be seen in Japan, where despite quantitative easing the 10-30 year curve has stayed relatively steep, averaging 0.85 percentage point over the last decade.\par \par \uc2 Credit risk in U.S. Treasurys might seem outlandish. But in a sovereign state with control over its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and monetary policy, credit risk and inflation risk are close to synonymous. As Mr. Bernanke pointed out in his 2002 speech on deflation, the logic of the printing press must assert itself in the end. That may well not stop 30-year Treasury yields falling further if investors continue to chase higher-yielding paper. But investors should pause for thought as they do so.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: BHP Finds Strength In Diversity \par  \par    By Liam Denning \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Variety is the spice of mining. Or so \f28 \uc2 \f2 BHP Billiton Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (BHP.AU, BHP) argues in taking a pot-shot at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Potash Corp. of Saskatchewan\f28 \uc2 \f2  (POT, POT.T).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors don't always concur. Five years ago, rival \f28 \uc2 \f2 Anglo American PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (AAL.LN, AAUKY) was penalized for its very diversity. Profits were healthy, but could not match the supercharged growth of rivals like BHP and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xstrata PLC\f28 \uc2 \f2  (XTA.LN), which had greater exposure to iron ore and base metals.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the two years leading up to Anglo's October 2005 announcement that it would restructure, its stock gained 44%. But BHP's rose 88%. Lo and behold, over the following year Anglo's stock rose 46% while BHP's increased by just 22%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BHP's diversification has not been rewarded with a premium stock multiple. From 2004 until bidding for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  in November 2007, BHP's one-year forward price/earnings multiple was, on average, at a 21% discount to Rio's, according to data from FactSet. It was also lower than Anglo's for most of that period.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, Paul Galloway at Sanford C. Bernstein points out, BHP's stock is actually a bit more volatile than Rio's, relative to movements in the broader market. This is remarkable when you consider that Rio's business is less diversified, with iron ore accounting for 58% of operating profit in the 12 months ended June, compared with 29% at BHP. In addition, Rio has been subject to a hostile bid (by BHP itself) and undergone a recapitalization.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet market fashions come and go. Seeming indifference to a diversified strategy over the past decade coincided with a boom in prices for oil and industrial metals. From a short-term perspective, it made sense to buy the stocks of companies more leveraged to them, like \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xstrata\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If you expect commodities to repeat that surge over the next decade, then such leveraged bets again make sense. But similar gains look unlikely as the world deals with the excesses of the last boom.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since the beginning of 2000, BHP's stock has delivered a total return, including dividends reinvested, of more than 570%. Rio, Anglo and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Xstrata\f28 \uc2 \f2  have turned in 249%, 259% and 202%, respectively. That gap is explained in large part by the fact that, according to data from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Capital IQ\f28 \uc2 \f2 , BHP paid out more than $21 billion in dividends--more than its three rivals combined.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Anyone fretting that diversification makes BHP too much like a commodities index misses the point that, soundly managed, BHP can enhance performance \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operationally\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and financially rather than just relying on market swings. The S&P GSCI Total Return commodities index is up just 44% since the start of 2000.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It is telling that BHP's stock has outperformed that of its peers since October 2007's market peak, as reliability has become more fashionable. Coincidentally, that appetite for safety has also fueled demand for fixed income paper, enabling BHP to finance its bid for Potash so cheaply. Yes, the miner is in danger of overpaying. But strategically, its move is hard to argue with.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Tokyo's Queer Definition Of "To Run" \par  \par    By James Simms \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In recent days, Japan's media has been transfixed on two stories: The first, whether a political heavyweight will run in a contest to become the nation's next leader. The second, whether Tokyo can do anything to stop the nation's economy from grinding to a halt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Thursday, Japan got its answer to both questions -- Ichiro Ozawa will challenge Prime Minister Naoto Kan for control of the Democratic Party of Japan in an internal contest next month. That news ends any expectation of action by the current administration.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo's ability to effectively respond to either an economic slowdown or the suddenly rising yen was always under a cloud, with the party's Sept. 14 leadership vote looming. Certainly any request by Kan to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Masaaki Shirakawa for a policy response might've been taken with a grain of salt, until his leadership was affirmed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But the Ozawa vs. Kan battle means any finalization of a promised stimulus plan, which is expected to be worth at least $20 billion, is unlikely, and the government's ability to talk down the yen -- which has been relatively effective so far -- could be put to the test by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders. On both fronts, Tokyo needs to convince the Bank of Japan to come along by enlarging its emergency lending facility to banks and not removing, from the market, liquidity generated by a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 True, there's a case to be made that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needn't \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets yet. And few outside of a speck of expensive Tokyo real estate should care about whether Ozawa wins his challenge. Kan, Japan's fifth prime minister in three years, has been in office for only three months, and the policy differences between the two, in the grand scheme of things, aren't notable.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Except for one thing: A campaign finance scandal could lead to Ozawa's indictment extending the leadership vacuum.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current situation ought to have someone reminding the Democrats that the verb "to run" has other meanings. How about running the country?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Taxing Times For US Investors \par  \par    By Rolfe Winkler and Laura Saunders \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another uncertainty facing U.S. investors: impending tax-law changes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If Bush tax cuts expire as scheduled in 2011, long-term capital-gains rates will jump from 15% to 20%, and dividends will again be taxed as ordinary income, not at 15%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Are asset markets being pressured as a result? It is hard to say definitively, of course, as investors have economic worries that probably outweigh tax concerns. But at the margin, the tax uncertainty is increasing incentives to sell, especially for the well-heeled.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One constituency apparently rushing to avoid higher capital-gains rates: longtime owners of private businesses. Midmarket bankers, including veteran Beatrice Mitchell of Sperry Mitchell, say they are seeing a significant rise in deal flow as clients that might have sold in the next few years race to get deals done.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These aren't proverbial moms and pops, of course. They are sophisticated owners that have built businesses generating tens of millions of dollars of earnings. The vast majority of their net worth is often tied up in their business, and the cost basis for capital-gains tax liability can be low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cliffstar, a large juice maker, recently sold for about $500 million. The Star family, owners for more than 100 years, likely saved millions of dollars in taxes selling before any rate changes, and says potential tax increases were a factor. For buyers willing to brave the turbulent economy, sellers on a deadline could offer opportunities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While there could be a similar effect at the margin with small investors, most have fewer capital gains to cash out, given the weak stock market. And flows into domestic and international stock mutual funds are, in fact, positive to the tune of $8.9 billion this year after outflows in 2008 and 2009. Their biggest gains may be in bonds, where inflows remain strong as investors seek safety.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The most comparable increase in capital-gains taxes came from 1986 tax overhauls, which raised the top rate on long-term gains from 20% to 28% for 1987. Realized gains almost doubled from $172 billion in 1985 to $328 billion in 1986. But the increase also was spurred by a surge in deals ahead of a new law that significantly raised taxes on transactions done after 1986.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This time round, another effect of potential tax changes could be to discourage investment in high-yielding stocks. The Bush tax cut dropped the top tax rate on qualified dividends to 15%. If it returns to a level as high as 39.6% for high earners, those dividends would suddenly look a lot less juicy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Congress could yet extend the Bush tax cuts a year, given the weak economy and coming elections. But some sellers, it appears, aren't waiting to find out. [ 08-26-10 1157ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4257   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100826e68q000b1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
