\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Fiscal Weapons In Short Supply - Taking Stock\b0\par\par\uc2 1,300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 12:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1007 GMT [Dow Jones] Another period of economic weakness could find the world's developed economies short of the sort of fiscal weapons they deployed quite effectively in round one, says Thursday's Taking Stock column. (david.cottle@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0958 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares end +0.2% at 886.10 in active trade, but market breadth neutral with number of gainers, losers about even; gains contained as investors take profit in banks, other recent outperformers, while foreign fund inflows slowed. "I guess it's sort of healthy; I kind of like the fact that we've got this bit of a correction and not outperforming too much," says Andrew Yates, head of sales for international equities at Asia Plus Securities. Bourse lacking drivers at moment, with buying mostly in plays with stock-specific news. Resistance at 890, support at 885. Among top actives, \uc2 Italian-Thai Development\uc2  (ITD.TH) +6.9% at THB3.42, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.4% at THB258, Thaicom (THCOM.TH) +7% at THB8.45 on optimism of new China deal soon, \uc2 Charoen Pokphand Foods\uc2  (CPF.TH) +1% at THB25.25, but \uc2 Jasmine International\uc2  (JAS.TH) down 6.1% at THB1.54 after recent run-up, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 1.9% at THB103.50. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0958 GMT [Dow Jones] Dealers betting Malaysian government may soon lift ban on offshore trading of MYR may be in for disappointment; participants have been speculating Bank Negara may do away with ban after bank last week further liberalized \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rules; but relaxation is administrative in nature, modest step to help companies reduce their exposure to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations and lower \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  conversion costs. Bank may also want to assess impact of latest rule changes on market before moving towards full \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  convertibility, which existed before 1998. If history is any guide, markets shouldn't expect further relaxation of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading rules for a few more years. See: =MONEY TALKS: Malaysia Not Ready To Let Ringgit Trade Offshore. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0957 GMT [Dow Jones] Most Thai government bonds down on profit-taking by some foreign banks after recent steep gains. However, offshore players continue to seek medium and long-term notes, limiting downside for day. Foreign investors parked funds in bonds after they piled up THB positions, pushing THB to fresh 28-month high. "Despite profit-taking by some players, persistent capital inflows will continue to keep bonds well-bid and this likely to prevent any sudden yield reversal," says bond trader. Yields expected to move in tight range tomorrow. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.70%/2.67% vs 2.70%/2.65% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.77%/2.72% vs 2.73%/2.70%, June 2019 at 2.82%/2.75% vs 2.79%/2.75%. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0957 GMT [Dow Jones] The spread between Irish and German bond yields remains stable after the Irish government debt agency, NTMA, sold EUR200 million worth of six month T-bills and EUR400 million eight-month T-bills at average yields of 1.978% and 2.348%, respectively. Coverage is 10.1 times in the six-month maturity and 4.1 times in the eight-month maturity. The 2-year Ireland/Germany yield spread is up 8 bps at 264.5 bps, 5-year spread 2 bps tighter at 286.5 bps and 10-year spread unchanged at 344 bps, all are around the same levels they are at before the T-bill auction. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0955 GMT [Dow Jones] Pakistan shares up on gains in refinery, cement stocks with benchmark KSE-100 index +0.4% at 9589.37. "Cement stocks are in the limelight on expectations that the government will soon start constructing homes, bridges, roads and highways in flood-hit areas, which will boost demand," says local dealer. Says market sentiment aided by foreign investments as data show offshore fund houses invested $1.1 million in equities Wednesday. "There is a likelihood that the IMF (in response to floods) could relax conditions tied to the release of loan installment of $1.56 billion to Pakistan, out of the total aid package of $11.2 billion approved in November 2008," adds local analyst. National Refinery (NRL.KA) +3.9% at PKR192, Attock Cement (ACPL.KA) +1.3% at PKR65.85. (djn.in@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0954 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB slips to fresh 28-month-low of 31.36 vs 31.48 late yesterday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected of buying to slow THB's rise but pair's fall below 31.40 caught many players by surprise, raising expectations THB will gain much further, say local dealers. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems helpless, says one dealer. Adds, intuitional investors may continue selling as market widely expecting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hike its policy rate by another 25 bps at October meeting. Pair tipped in 31.30-31.40 range for rest of session. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0950 GMT [Dow Jones] From the view of an American or Asian investor the Swiss franc has the enviable characteristic of a high correlation with the euro while remaining unaffected by the problems of the EMU debt crisis says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . That means the franc will always be seen as the "better euro" when the euro-zone debt crisis rears its ugly head. The bank adds with the SNB seemingly convinced there are no dangers of deflation, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is off the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's radar. EUR/CHF now at 1.3065. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0939 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR implied volatilities tad lower as spot remains in range with no signs of imminent breakout, says trader with foreign bank; 1-month ATMF at 6.80%/7.80 vs 7%/7.80% late Wednesday. "The market is pretty quiet, except for sustained client driven interest on 1-year risk reversals," says trader with foreign bank. But client-side selling interest on 1-year RR met by buying interest from prop traders, leaving contract locked at bid-ask midpoint of around 0.4% towards USD calls. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] The markets have corrected in the last 24 hours but without throwing out reversal signals says \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . In the run-up to the end of the holiday season such price action is not a surprise, but it doesn't mean that suddenly the world is a safer place. RBS notes internal euro-zone strains are intensifying, equity markets remain in a downtrend and safe havens like gold, CHF and SGD remain in demand. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0931 GMT [Dow Jones] Italy/Germany bond yield spreads edge wider after auctions of Italian 2-year CTZ August 2012 and 6-month T-bill. EUR4 billion worth of CTZ and EUR9.5 billion worth of T-bills were sold, at yields of 1.721% and 0.958%, respectively. 2-year Italy/Germany yield spread is up 2 bps at 110.5 bps, 5-year spread unchanged at 141 bps, 10-year spread up 1 bp at 162 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0915 GMT [Dow Jones] At this juncture there seems little obvious reason to buy any major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  says RBS. The USD is under pressure on recent poor data while renewed evidence of financial stress in the euro zone and the threat of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes it difficult in sell USDs against other majors. RBS does say gold continues to be the alternative store of value. (gary.stride@dowjones.com) [ 08-26-10 0607ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100826e68q0007a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
