\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-MARKET SIGNALS-Yen looks likely to stay strong\b0\par\par\uc2 985 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 04:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats item from late Tuesday with fresh links to Wednesday's latest story) (For more on the Japanese economy, click on [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 By Masayuki Kitano and Eric Burroughs\par \par \uc2 TOKYO/HONG KONG, Aug 24 (Reuters) - The yen may hold near 15-year highs against the dollar around the 80-85 zone in coming months as a confluence of factors play in favour of the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite the country's weak economy.\par \par \uc2 Japan's focus on hedging any purchases of U.S. Treasuries, the downward dollar pressure from falling U.S. yields, the prospect of more Federal Reserve easing and the Bank of Japan's inaction on deflation all suggest the yen can remain strong.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on Wednesday further sharpened his rhetoric on the yen's steep gains after the Nikkei newspaper reported \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider selling the yen in unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if speculators drive up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [ID:nTOE65T00N]\par \par \uc2 Here are some graphics explaining why the yen could remain strong against the dollar <JPY=> and euro <EURJPY=R>.\par \par \uc2 For the graphics on the themes below, click on: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/puw56n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/puw56n}}}\par \par \uc2 To see a full PDF report on the yen's strength: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/vaz26n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/vaz26n}}}\par \par \uc2 TREASURIES AND FALLING YIELDS\par \par \uc2 Japanese investors, especially banks, have been huge buyers of U.S. Treasuries, especially in recent months.\par \par \uc2 The latest \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data shows Japanese investors snapped up 2.2 trillion yen ($26 billion) of U.S. sovereign bonds in June. Total foreign bond buying accelerated to $55 billion last month and has been hefty so far in August. [JP/CAP]\par \par \uc2 All things equal, such purchases would weaken the yen against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Japanese banks tend to fund their Treasury buying with dollars via the U.S. repo market. Institutional investors such as life insurers are taking advantage of lower U.S. interest rates to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hedge their purchases by selling dollars in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards <JPYF=> -- thus neutralising the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  impact.\par \par \uc2 Still, the U.S. bond buying has been big enough to play a role in yields falling to 16-month lows, eroding the traditional U.S. yield advantage over Japanese bonds whose yields are also falling, though not at the same pace.\par \par \uc2 The spread between five-year Treasury yields <US5YT=RR> and JGB yields <JP5YTN=JBTC> has shrunk a full percentage point in the past four months, while the 90-day correlation between that yield spread and dollar/yen has climbed to a very strong 0.96 -- showing the role that yield spreads are playing.\par \par \uc2 The same is true for German Bund yields <DE5YT=TWEB> and euro/yen.\par \par \uc2 The deleveraging of banks has meant that many in major countries are increasing their holdings as a share of total assets, just as inflation slows and could turn into deflation -- keeping G3 yields under pressure relative to JGB yields.\par \par \uc2 YEN NOT THAT STRONG, REALLY\par \par \uc2 The yen's climb to a 15-year peak against the dollar and a nine-year high against the euro has spurred a barrage of warnings from Japanese officials about the dangers to the country's exporters and fragile economy.\par \par \uc2 The rise against those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has resulted in the yen's trade-weighted index -- or nominal effective exchange rate (NEER) -- hitting an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 The yen's inflation-adjusted index (REER) is at an 18-month high, but is still 32 percent below its 1995 peak because of falling prices in Japan for the better part of 15 years.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the relatively weak yen REER matters little for big Japanese firms that had planned for a dollar/yen rate closer to 90 this year and are scrambling to limit the hit to their earnings from its strength, on top of a slowing global economy.\par \par \uc2 YEARNING FOR YEN\par \par \uc2 One surprise this year has been foreign central banks finding a new place for the yen in their foreign reserves.\par \par \uc2 The bulk of overseas buying of Japanese assets this year has been in short-term money market instruments, as seen most clearly in China's record buying spree of Japanese debt. [ID:nTOE67802Z] [ID:nTOE66705G]\par \par \uc2 Such demand for Japanese debt, either as part of foreign reserve diversification or due to investors seeking a temporary parking place for their money, has likely helped support the yen.\par \par \uc2 YEARNING FOR HIGHER YIELDS\par \par \uc2 While the yen has climbed broadly, it has not risen as much as higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the Brazilian real <BRLJPY=R> and South African rand <ZARJPY=R> thanks mainly to sizable Japanese investment abroad.\par \par \uc2 Japanese investors shovelled $173 billion into overseas equities, bonds and money market instruments in January-July. Such overseas investments by Japanese investment trusts, or "toushin" funds, are heading towards higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and emerging market assets rather than the dollar.\par \par \uc2 BOJ STANDS PAT, FED SEEN ACTING\par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan has showed no inclination to fight the deflationary forces gripping the Japanese economy, even as the Fed looks poised to increase quantitative easing, and many market watchers believe it has little power to turn the tide anyway.\par \par \uc2 So far, increases in its special funding \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  have done little to stem the yen's rise or spur bank lending.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ's total balance sheet size remains well below its quantitative easing peak in 2006, while the Fed's has nearly tripled since the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The perception that the Fed may be more aggressive than the BOJ has been one factor undermining the greenback.\par \par \uc2 So in many ways, the yen's rise simply reflects that it is the anti-dollar and anti-euro among G3 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a world where the developed world is struggling after the financial crisis but emerging markets are still thriving. (Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-YEN (MARKET SIGNALS)|LANGEN|J|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|DNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100825e68p00024\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
