\par\par \b \uc2 INSTANT VIEW 5-Japan export growth slows for 5th month\b0\par\par\uc2 956 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 02:47\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (For more stories on the Japanese economy, click [ID:nECONJP])\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 25 (Reuters) - Japan's annual export growth slowed for a fifth consecutive month in July as a surging yen and slowing overseas demand threaten to inflict more pain on Japan's export-dependant recovery. ***************************************************************\par \par \uc2 KEY POINTS:\par \par \uc2 -- Exports rose 23.5 percent in July from a year earlier, more than the median forecast for a 21.9 percent annual rise. Imports were up 15.7 percent against an expected annual gain of 20.6 percent. [JPEXPY=ECI] [JPIMPY=ECI]\par \par \uc2 -- The annual rise in exports in July was smaller than the 27.7 percent increase in June as export growth slowed for the fifth month.\par \par \uc2 -- Exports to Asia, which account for more than half of Japan's total, increased 23.8 percent from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 -- Exports to China rose 22.7 percent from a year earlier, while U.S.-bound shipments gained an annual 25.9 percent.\par \par \uc2 -- Shipments to the European Union rose an annual 13.3 percent.\par \par \uc2 -- The trade balance came to a surplus of 804.2 billion yen ($9.56 billion), up 119.9 percent from a year earlier. That compared with the median estimate for a 458.8 billion yen surplus and a 25.4 percent rise. [JPTBL2=ECI]\par \par \uc2 -- Japan's corporate service price (CSPI) index fell 1.2 percent in July from a year earlier, a bigger decline than the previous month, Bank of Japan data showed.\par \par \uc2 -- The index, which tracks prices of business-to-business services, fell 0.4 percent from the previous month.\par \par \uc2 COMMENTARY:\par \par \uc2 TATSUSHI SHIKANO, SENIOR ECONOMIST, MITSUBISHI UFJ MORGAN STANLEY SECURITIES\par \par \uc2 "July exports were slightly better than expected, which showed Japan's exports remain strong.\par \par \uc2 "Exports to all major regions including the United States, Europe and Asia were strong. But as for the outlook, uncertainty has been rapidly increasing. I am especially worried about the negative impact from the strong yen.\par \par \uc2 "The government should conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  even without cooperation from other nations. It needs to show that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is worried about an excessively strong yen. But solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't be effective without further monetary easing by the BOJ."\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 AKIYOSHI TAKUMORI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, SUMITOMO MITSUI ASSET MANAGEMENT\par \par \uc2 "The growth in exports is probably magnified by a larger-than-expected drop in imports, which was likely the result of calendar factors and sluggish oil prices. So exports are within expectations.\par \par \uc2 "Growth in exports to the United States, despite concerns about the U.S. economy slowing, and continued firm exports to China suggest Japan's exports have remained solid even when there were worries about slower global growth hurting the Japanese economy."\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 TAKESHI MINAMI, CHIEF ECONOMIST, NORINCHUKIN RESEARCH INSTITUTE\par \par \uc2 "It's clear from the data that the slowdown in global economic growth is hurting exports. The strong yen is also having an impact on exports, but the global slowdown is evident in the data.\par \par \uc2 "The government and the BOJ have already been slow to respond to the stronger yen. If the yen stays at these levels it will have a considerably negative impact on the economy.\par \par \uc2 "One reason that the yen is strengthening is the view that the government and the BOJ can't do anything. It would be hard to make the yen go back to previous levels with solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it's important to show that the government is doing something.\par \par \uc2 "If the BOJ supplies funds at the same time, then it will have a monetary easing effect and curb the yen's rise."\par \par \uc2 - - - -\par \par \uc2 KIICHI MURASHIMA, ECONOMIST, CITIGROUP GLOBAL MARKETS, TOKYO\par \par \uc2 "These numbers look firm. Overseas economies are slowing, so I was expecting things to be worse.\par \par \uc2 "But in the big picture, exports are likely to continue to slow after such a fast pace of growth earlier this year. A slowdown in the United States and Europe will eventually have an impact.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar went to 83 yen, so the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased, but it would take more than \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It has to be coupled with easing by the Bank of Japan to have any impact."\par \par \uc2 MARKET REACTION:\par \par \uc2 -- For yen updates click <FXNEWS>; for prices click <JPY=>\par \par \uc2 -- For JGB updates click <MMNEWS>, for prices click <2JGBv1>\par \par \uc2 -- For stocks click [.T], for Nikkei average click <.N225>\par \par \uc2 LINK:\par \par \uc2 -- To view the government trade tables, click on:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.customs.go.jp/toukei/info/index_e.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.customs.go.jp/toukei/info/index_e.htm}}}\par \par \uc2 -- To view CSPI data:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.boj.or.jp/en/type/stat/boj_stat/cspi/cspi1007.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.boj.or.jp/en/type/stat/boj_stat/cspi/cspi1007.pdf}}}\par \par \uc2 BACKGROUND:\par \par \uc2 -- Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider unilateral yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if speculators drive up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nikkei newspaper reported, after failed attempts to verbally curb sharp yen gains.\par \par \uc2 -- A strong yen threatens exporters by making their products more expensive overseas and reducing corporate earnings. Many companies have been shifting production overseas, making them more resilient to a rising yen. A prolonged period of yen gains could still damage the economy as many small and medium-sized firms are exposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk.\par \par \uc2 -- There is also a growing chance that the Bank of Japan could ease monetary policy further to deter yen gains, but economists doubt this would be effective as the yen is rising versus the dollar due to signs the U.S. economy is faltering.\par \par \uc2 -- Ruling party politicians are working on stimulus measures that may offer help to young job seekers and extend incentives on purchases of energy-efficient goods to try to prevent a surging yen from derailing an already slowing economy. (Reporting by Rie Ishiguro; Editing by Michael Watson)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/TRADE (INSTANT VIEW 5)|LANGEN|J|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|Z|U|C|GRO|MTL|SOF|O|OIL|NAT|UKI   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100825e68p0000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
