\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Gain As Data Discounted\b0\par\par\uc2 2,895 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 August 2010\par 00:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10060.06  +19.61  +0.20% \par Nasdaq     2141.54  +17.78  +0.84% \par S&P 500    1055.33   +3.46  +0.33% \par FTSE 100   5109.40  -46.55  -0.90% \par Xetra DAX  5899.50  -35.94  -0.61% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 22/32  -2/32 0.528% +3.8BP \par 5Y Tsy  101 24/32  -9/32 1.380% +5.7BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 23/32 -14/32 2.543% +5.0BP \par  \par USD/JPY       84.62-64     +0.69% \par Range         84.83-84.08 \par EUR/USD       1.2654-56    -0.07% \par Range         1.2725-1.2607 \par AUD/USD       0.8837-41    +0.08% \par Range         0.8895-0.8772 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   11.50/11.80% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   12.20/12.50% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   13.65/13.95% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,241.30 +$7.90 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,230.90-$1,243.40 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $72.52   +$0.89 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Home-building companies including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toll Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2 , \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.R. Horton\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 PulteGroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  led U.S. stocks modestly higher Wednesday as the market looked past disappointing economic data in the hopes that the housing market has finally bottomed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average edged up 19.61 points, or 0.20%, to 10060.06, snapping a four-session losing streak. The measure was led by home-improvement retailer \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , which gained 55 cents, or 2%, to 28.33. Home-building companies also led the gains in the \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-share index, which rose 3.46, or 0.33%, to 1055.33.\par \par \uc2 Toll Brothers\uc2  added 94 cents, or 5.8%, to 17.13 and \uc2 PulteGroup\uc2  rose 24 cents, or 3.1%, to 8.08. \uc2 D.R. Horton\uc2  gained 46 cents, or 4.6%, to 10.43, climbing after Ticonderoga Securities lifted its stock-investment rating on the company to "buy" from "hold," citing "improvement in operating metrics that, generally speaking, far surpass competitors."\par \par \uc2 The market had initially slid Wednesday after the Commerce Department said new-home buying dropped in July to the lowest level on record going back to 1963. Upsetting forecasts for a slight gain, sales decreased 12.4% from the previous month to a seasonally adjusted annual rate of 276,000. But speculation that the housing market may, at last, have hit bottom helped to boost home builders, which led the broad market's modest rally. Investors also said the recent flight to bonds has helped stock valuations.\par \par \uc2 "Initially, the stock market took the weaker-than-expected durable goods and home-sales data negatively but, over the course of the day, it's been discounted," said Jim O'Sullivan, chief economist at \uc2 MF Global\uc2 . "On the one side, people are worried about the economy weakening but, on the other, arguments can be made the stock market is cheaper relative to the bond market."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Nasdaq Composite posted a much larger gain on Wednesday, rising 17.78, or 0.84%, to 2141.54, its first gain in three sessions. The technology-heavy index benefited from gains for Apple, which raised speculation it will update its line of iPods or other technology by sending out invitations to a company event on Sept. 1. Apple, which has historically held a media event in September to unveil new digital music and media products, rose 2.96, or 1.2%, to 242.89. The Nasdaq also got a boost from \uc2 Costco\uc2 , the wholesale-style retailer, which gained 1.47, or 2.6%, to 57.07.\par \par \uc2 Trading volume was light on Wednesday, continuing the month's trend. At just under four billion shares per day, average NYSE composite daily volume this August is on track to be the lowest for a month since May of 2008 and the lowest August since August 2006.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended sharply lower Wednesday, as banks and energy firms fell amid renewed concerns over the global economic outlook, while real estate developers weakened after Beijing said it was still mulling a property tax.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index could test immediate support at 2550 this week, with the next target at 2500.\par \par \uc2 "The string of disappointing U.S. data, coupled with Beijing's unrelenting stance on property sector tightening is dragging down shares across sectors," said Han Hao, an analyst at China Minzu Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell for the fourth-straight session Wednesday, with the Nikkei Stock Average sinking to a 16-month low, as the government failed to provide details to an increasingly impatient market of how it intends to curb the strengthening yen.\par \par \uc2 Selling picked up in the afternoon after Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda told reporters that he didn't receive specific guidance from Prime Minister Naoto Kan to stem the yen's rise at a much-anticipated lunchtime meeting. The market gained little comfort from Noda's comments that his ministry will "take appropriate action when necessary."\par \par \uc2 "We can guess some action will come out soon, but it's meaningless if they're just going to keep holding talks," said Yoshinori Nagano, senior strategist at \uc2 Daiwa Asset Management\uc2 . "It's very hard to see where the short-term bottom will be since selling will continue until something comes out."\par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to expect a genuine rebound in shares unless there is more clarity on where the U.S. economy is heading," said Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures settled higher Wednesday in U.S. trading, snapping a five-day losing streak despite a report from the Department of Energy showing U.S. inventories of oil and oil products remain at record highs.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures printed their highest settlement in nearly two months Wednesday as investors wanted the perceived safety of the metal following weaker-than-expected U.S. new-home sales and disappointing durable goods reports.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange pared losses going into the close but still ended lower Wednesday following the disappointing U.S. data.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar gained against the yen Wednesday amid growing speculation that Japanese authorities will step in to counter the yen's strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The probability that the Bank of Japan may step into the market "has clearly increased over the past few weeks," said Matthew Strauss, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 RBC Capital Markets\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors chose to focus on the likelihood of some sort of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , rather than continued worrying signs about the health of the U.S. economy. The yen is seen as a safe haven in times of economic turmoil and investors have been piling into the yen in recent weeks, sending it to its highest levels against the greenback since June 1995. The dollar rose to Y84.78 from Y84.15 late Tuesday, rebounding off the 15-year low of 83.58 hit Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the Swiss franc, considered another haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , continued its march higher, following the release of dismal U.S. housing data. The franc hit its highest level against the dollar since January, and a new record high against the euro.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is testing whether the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  will come into the markets" to counter the Swiss franc's appreciation, Strauss said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "So far, there has been no real indication that they're going to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," which allowed investors to file into the perceived safety of the franc, said Strauss.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday afternoon in New York, the euro was at $1.2655, down from $1.2674 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The euro was up to Y107.20 from Y106.64. The U.K. pound was at $1.5450 from $1.5433. The U.S. dollar was at CHF1.0291 from CHF1.0312, after falling as low as CHF1.0247. The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 83.275 from 83.139.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. data disappointed, with new-home buying in the U.S. decreasing in July to the lowest level on record going back to 1963. A slight rise in demand for U.S. manufactured durable goods was also well under expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given the run of very weak data, investors are now focused on the policy response," making an annual meeting of the world's top central bankers in Jackson Hole, Wyo., the most important event of the week, said Steven Englander, head of G10 strategy at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. Investors are waiting for Friday's speech from Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke to see whether the Fed will take action--such as further quantitative easing--to prop up the faltering U.S. recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The commodity-backed dollar bloc, including the Canadian, New Zealand and Australian dollars, all fell following the data, but recovered late in New York trading as U.S. equity markets pulled into the black.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, the yen's movements were largely driven by increased "jawboning" by Japanese officials in response to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 15-year high against the dollar and nine-year high against the euro hit Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan moved closer to action to curb the surging yen, threatening to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for the first time in more than six years, while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be set to ease monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan "must take appropriate action when needed" to combat unwelcome moves in the yen, which have recently been "one-sided," Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda told reporters before meeting with Prime Minister Naoto Kan to discuss the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro fell against the dollar as concerns about the financial health of countries on the periphery of the euro zone overshadowed a better-than-expected Ifo business sentiment reading out of Germany. Such fears were stoked when \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services trimmed its rating on Ireland to AA- late Tuesday. The agency said the projected fiscal cost to the Irish government of supporting the hard-hit financial sector has increased significantly above prior estimates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also watched nervously as the Hungarian forint hit a new all-time low against the Swiss franc. Because as many as 70% of Hungarian households and over half of businesses have loans denominated in Swiss francs, the current exchange rates will increase loan repayment costs for many Hungarian borrowers and pose a potential threat to the country's banks, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 25-08-10 2205GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasury securities fell Wednesday, pushing the benchmark 10-year note's yield up from a 19-month low, as the bond market took a breather from the recent rally.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1955 GMT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was 14/32 lower to yield 2.543%. The 30-year bond was 13/32 lower to yield 3.585%. The five-year note was down 9/32 to yield 1.380% and the two-year note was down 2/32 to yield 0.528%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bonds rose earlier as investors sought safe assets on growing worries about the economic outlook. The anxiety was fueled by a weaker-than-forecast durable goods report and a drop in new home sales to a record low, which followed Tuesday's data showing a decline of existing home sales to a 15-year low.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rally started to fade in late morning as the market set up for a new government debt auction. Bond prices weakened further following the $36 billion sale in five-year notes, demand for which was still healthy but trailed the expectations of many market participants.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A rebound in the U.S. stock market added to the decline in bond prices. Treasury investors are also bracing for $29 billion in seven-year notes Thursday, which will wrap up this week's $102 billion in nominal government notes supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The auction was not blown out so any excuse to push up yields to more attractive levels, people are going to take," said Mary Ann Hurley, vice president of trading in Seattle at \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "The rally has come a long way in such a short period of time. People like the Treasury market given the economic data but they want to own Treasurys at higher yields, or cheaper levels."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John Briggs, U.S. interest rate strategist at RBS Securities Inc. in Stamford, Conn., noted this is the first time over the past couple of weeks the bond market has not held its bid in response to poor data, which is "often a sign we have run out of buyers, and that we may be due for a correction."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the peak of the buying in the morning session, the 10-year note yield touched 2.418%, the lowest level since Jan. 21, 2009. The 30-year bond's yield hit 3.461%, the weakest level since March 18, 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended slightly higher Wednesday, reversing earlier losses as a decline in the local equity market boosted demand for safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It seems investors are worried about the economic recovery pace domestic and worldwide as well," a Shanghai-based analyst with a local insurer said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index may test 126.8 by the end of the week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plans to sell CNY17 billion of three-month bills and CNY80 billion of three-year bonds in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended higher Wednesday but gave up some of their gains when it became clear the Bank of Japan wasn't likely to take immediate steps to ease monetary policy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, analysts said the BOJ will take easing measures eventually. People familiar with the BOJ's thinking told Dow Jones Newswires that the BOJ is considering ways to loosen monetary policy further to ease the yen's negative impact on the domestic economy. The BOJ could, for example, expand its three-month 0.1% emergency lending facility.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Therefore, JGB yields will keep falling for now, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is behind the curve in responding to the high yen, so it may end up acting multiple times, said Akito Fukunaga, chief rates strategist at RBS Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The BOJ may add the supply of the lending facility, and if that fails to stop the yen's rise, the BOJ may act again by increasing JGB buying," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RBS expects the 10-year yield to fall to 0.80%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Since JGB yields are likely to remain low for the time being, demand at Thursday's two-year JGB auction should be strong, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategist Kenro Kawano.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the tender, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will sell Y2.6 trillion of two-year notes, with an expected coupon at 0.1%, its lowest level since the July 2005 offering.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Buying two-year JGBs, even with a low yield, will likely be justified soon as the BOJ's policy-easing is now very likely," and this would push yields down further, Kawano said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, banks will likely be active buyers at Thursday's auction as their deposits have been increasing as investors shy away from buying risk-sensitive investments, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Thursday, the Philippine economy likely extended its recovery into the second quarter, albeit at a slower annual pace than in the first quarter, helped by a rebound in exports and consumer spending underpinned by steady growth in overseas workers remittances and election-related expenditures. The economy likely expanded 6.1% in the second quarter from a year earlier, according to the median forecast of 10 economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US July New-home Sales Drop To Record Low \par US Durable Goods Orders Rise Less Than Expected \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toll Brothers\f28 \uc2 \f2  Posts First Profit Since 2007 \par Refinancing Drives US Mortgage Market \par Credit Card Charge-offs Drop In July \par BHP Hopes To Foil Potash's Poison Pill \par Apple To Hold Music-related Event Sept 1 \par Interior Defends Drilling Ban \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  May \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  If Yen Rises Too Far Or Too Fast - Sources \p