\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,201 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 13:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  MOVES TOWARD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , EASING AS YEN SOARS \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moved closer Wednesday to concerted action to curb the surging yen, threatening to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market for the first time in more than six years, while the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may be set to ease monetary policy. (published 1027 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PORTUGAL BOND SALE CONVINCING, IGNORES IRISH DOWNGRADE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Portugal's Treasury and Government Debt Agency raised a more-than-planned EUR1.301 billion at a bond auction Wednesday at only marginally higher yields than previously, largely distancing itself from news that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . downgraded its closest proxy, Ireland, Tuesday evening. (published 1050 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SWISS FRANC HITS RECORD HIGH BUT SNB UNLIKELY TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  YET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc hit an all-time high against the euro Wednesday as a ratings downgrade of Ireland bolstered its safe-haven status, but the franc will have to rise further before the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  steps into the market, analysts said. (published 0956 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPAIN REVISES 2009 GDP TO -3.7% FROM -3.6% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Spanish economy contracted more deeply last year than previously thought, the national statistics institute INE said Wednesday. (published 0924 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par IRELAND SOVEREIGN CDS RISE BRIEFLY, RECOVER ON S&P CUT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The cost of insuring Irish sovereign debt against default using credit default swaps rose briefly Wednesday morning before moving back down towards Tuesday's closing level after \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . downgraded the country one notch to AA- overnight. (published 0905 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN IFO SURPRISES BUT DOESN'T BANISH MARKET BLUES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business confidence in Germany rose again in August, defying recent indications of a global slowdown that could dent demand for its exports, the research institute Ifo said Wednesday. (published 0917 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH BUDGET MINISTER: FRANCO-GERMAN CONSENSUS ON BANK TAX \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French Budget Minister Francois Baroin said Wednesday that there is a consensus between France and German on taxing banks' profits. (published 0705 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN'S EXPORTS RISE IN JULY, BUT GROWTH PACE SLOWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's exports rose in July as shipments to Asia remained firm, but the pace of growth slowed for the fifth consecutive month, fanning fears that the country's broader economy may be losing steam. (published 0254 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TOP JAPAN GOVERNMENT OFFICIALS MEET; NO CLEAR STEPS ON YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's prime minister, finance minister and chief Cabinet secretary met at lunchtime Wednesday to discuss the soaring yen, but no clear initiatives emerged to cope with the problem. (published 0519 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN ECONOMY MINISTER SEES TAX CUTS BEFORE ELECTIONS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German economics minister Rainer Bruederle wants to see tax cuts during the current legislative period, he said in an interview in Bunte magazine. (published 0645 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK BUDGET TIGHTENING CLEARLY REGRESSIVE - IFS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The budget announced by the U.K. government in June failed to live up to its claim to be "progressive," and instead much of the burden of cutting the fiscal deficit will fall on poorer households, an influential think tank said Wednesday. (published 0727 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BANK OF THAILAND\f28 \uc2 \f2  UPS RATES; FURTHER HIKES TIPPED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thailand's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  raised its key interest rate as expected Wednesday to 1.75%, carrying on its rate-normalization efforts and trying to dampen an anticipated rise in price pressures amid strong economic growth. (published 0854 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINE IMPORTS TAD UP IN JUNE; TO BUOY ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The value of Philippine imports rose in June from a year earlier, fuelled by double-digit growth in purchases of plastics, industrial machinery, transport equipment, iron and steel, and telecommunications equipment--all primarily used by businesses to drive domestic economic activity. (published 0355 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SOUTH AFRICA PRESIDENT: AIM FOR 7% ANNUAL ECONOMIC GROWTH \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Africa aims to achieve an annual economic growth rate of at least 7% in the "near future," South Africa President Jacob Zuma said Wednesday in a speech in Beijing. (published 0721 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PHILIPPINE \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CENTRAL BANK\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : COMMODITY PRICES DOWNSIDE RISK TO INFLATION \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 International commodity prices pose little upside risk and may even help soften Philippine inflation, Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas Governor Amando Tetangco said Wednesday. (published 0520 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIA'S INDEPENDENTS NOT YET A UNITY TICKET \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Independent lawmakers in Australia will begin talks Monday on an agreement backing a new government, edging closer to ending the political uncertainty threatening investor and consumer confidence. (published 0936 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN ECONOMY WEANED OFF STIMULUS AS CONSTRUCTION GROWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's economy is successfully being weaned off economic stimulus, with growth in private sector construction spending in the second quarter outpacing government outlays for the first time since the global financial crisis was unleashed on the world economy in mid-2008. (published 0422 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S&P ANALYST: PAKISTAN RATING SAFE AS AID MAY CUSHION FLOOD IMPACT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Pakistan's B- credit rating remains safe, as help from international donors and easier lending terms with the International Monetary Fund should cushion the negative impact caused by the country's ongoing floods, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  Ratings Services said Wednesday. (published 0308 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par BOJ WATCH: SOURCES: BOJ CONSIDERING ADDITIONAL MONETARY EASING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Japan is considering further monetary easing steps to counter the negative economic impact of the surging yen, people familiar with the BOJ's thinking said. (published 0722 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: DOLLAR WILL GAIN AS GLOBAL WORRIES RISE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global risk sentiment will probably deteriorate further, playing more into the dollar's hands than ever before. Sentiment has becoming increasingly gloomy in recent weeks as the risk of a double dip recession in the U.S. has grown and the outlook for the global recovery has been downgraded. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: BONDS TO CORRECT GAINS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday BUND: Support in the 133.40 area will look to contain corrective weakness Wednesday before dominant bulls attempt to retest Tuesday's spike high at 134.26. A long-term bull wave equality projection target off the June 2009 continuation chart low at 117.52 lies at 134.40 and is part of a sequence of projected resistance levels protecting the psychological 135.00 level. A break below 133.40 would extend the corrective setback towards 133.02 and 132.30. Weekly chart BUND trend: Bullish. (published 0655 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  TECHNICALS: EUR/USD TO TEST 1.2490 SUPPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday EUR/USD: Consolidation off Tuesday's low at 1.2588 is likely to be short-lived, and will struggle to break resistance at 1.2730. Renewed bear pressure is expected on 1.2588, and a break below there would extend the short-term downtrend into fresh five-week lows towards significant pivotal support around 1.2490. Only above 1.2730 would lift the tone, opening 1.2785. Weekly chart EUR/USD trend: Bearish. (published 0621 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: AS DOLLAR SLIDES, SAFE-HAVEN OPTIONS SHRINK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 What's a bear to do? Most of the time since the 2008 financial crisis, the dollar has fulfilled its traditional haven role, rallying on bad news, falling on good. Except when the news is really, really bad, as with Tuesday's record 27.2% plunge in existing U.S. home sales for July, which sent the dollar lower against the euro and the yen. (published 2009 GMT) [ 25-08-10 1102GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100825e68p000yb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
