\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY At Day's High On  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Hints\b0\par\par\uc2 1,305 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 09:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY rises to the day's high of 84.54 after a series of source headlines on Dow Jones Newswires that hint at BOJ \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if the JPY continues to srengthen, especially sharply. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] Is the BOJ about to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the JPY? "Central bankers around the world will be heading to Jackson Hole shortly, meaning the risks for action from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  or BOJ are strongly skewed to either today or nothing until next week," says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  says. Also, it adds, "we continue to think the risks are much more slanted to quantitative easing in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  than \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from both a cost and efficacy argument, but politics will dominate this process." (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds little changed in subdued trading as investors await benchmark 5-year MGS auction results due out tomorrow; dealers say stronger U.S. Treasurys lent some support to MGSs, but gains capped by profit-taking in absence of fresh fundamental leads. "The outcome of the auction will determine the tone of the market for tomorrow. So far, market expectations are for demand to be strong," dealer says, citing recent auctions, which covered as much as 3X. Among benchmark MGSs, yield on May 2013 MGS unchanged at 3.15%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.24%, September 2017 down 2 bps at 3.49%, November 2019 unchanged at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0745 GMT [Dow Jones] Sterling is off to a shaky start with Tuesday's dovish comments from MPC new member Weale still ringing. GBP/USD has sold-off to back below 1.54 while EUR/GBP continues to build on the recovery started last Friday and has just printed this week's high of 0.8219. Chartists have turned negative on GBP/USD following the break of its 200-day MA at 1.5462 with 1.51 area now seen as a likely target. For EUR/GBP, resistance comes in at 0.8238 and then 0.8280 above which point stops are likely. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0744 GMT [Dow Jones] FOCUS: The Philippine stock market, already one of Asia's best performers, could soar to new heights before 2010 is out, as strong corporate results, robust economic prospects, and low political risk lure more investors. Benchmark PSEi, which ended up 0.7% Wednesday at 3554.16 after dropping Tuesday following the "hostage incident, is up 16% since end-2009--one of strongest gainers among Asian bourses, behind only Sri Lanka, Indonesia and Thailand--and analysts believe it could eclipse all-time high 3896 tapped in early October 2007. "We can do 4000 (on the PSEi) assuming there is no external noise. Fundamentally, the domestic market is sound," said Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital. Risk to bright prognosis: double-dip global recession. See =FOCUS: Philippine Stocks May Scale New Heights Amid Strong Econ (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0744 GMT [Dow Jones] August HSI futures +0.1% at 20,587, reversing early losses (intraday low at 20,460), as cash market also comes off low in afternoon, supported by bargain-hunting with index falling for 4th straight session. Contract trading around 10-20-point discount to cash in late afternoon, vs August fair value at single-digit discount, signalling negative (albeit only slightly) bias in market's near-term direction. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0742 GMT [Dow Jones] The Swiss franc is on an upward path due to risk aversion and a downgrade by \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  of its credit rating on Ireland, says BayernLB. However, the likelihood of SNB resuming its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy to weaken the franc seems fairly small, the bank says. EUR/CHF is at 1.3030 from a high of 1.3061. (martin.gelnar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0734 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex off 0.5% at 18,218.01, as investors cut positions on fears of slowdown in global economic recovery. "There is so much uncertainty in the market that it's hard to take a call. There is some profit-taking and volatility due to the settlement of monthly derivatives contracts (due Thursday) as well," says R.K. Gupta, managing director of Taurus Asset Management. Adds, market in overbought position, further correction cannot be ruled out. \uc2 HDFC Securities\uc2  tips support at 18,067. \uc2 Tata Steel\uc2  (500470.BY) down 3.0% at INR498.50, ICICI Bank (532174.BY) off 1.3% at INR987.50, \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) down 1.1% at INR998.95, \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY) off 1.2% at INR318.20. S&P CNX Nifty down 0.5% at 5475.75. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0733 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY has risk toward 82.91 says \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  analyst Karen Jones. That's the level where 2008-2010 support line comes in. Jones expects this area to hold on a first attempt. If it doesn't, the 1995 low of 79.92 will become exposed. Topside, the rate will remain directly offered while it trades below 85.65 which is where the 4-month downtrend line and 20-day MA are situated. USD/JPY at 84.42. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0731 GMT [Dow Jones] Have Japanese officials gone beyond the point on no return? \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  reckons they might have no choice now but to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not only because of 