\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei hits 16-mth closing low, strong yen weighs\b0\par\par\uc2 885 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 01:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Lack of Japan policy action on yen weighs on Nikkei\par \par \uc2  * Nikkei's next target at 8,697, 61.8% retracement\par \par \uc2  * Nikkei slow stochastic, RSI both oversold\par \par \uc2  * 9,000-level could become strong resistance-analyst\par \par \uc2  By Elaine Lies and Aiko Hayashi\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 25 (Reuters) - The Nikkei average hit a 16-month closing low on Wednesday, at one point falling over 2 percent, as disappointment spread over the lack of policy action by Japan to rein in the strong yen, which threatens a fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2  Hopes for action by the government and the Bank of Japan on the yen, which rose to a 15-year high on Tuesday, had provided early support after the Nikkei business daily reported that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may consider unilateral yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if speculators drive up the yen.\par \par \uc2  But these hopes gradually faded as the day wore on without signs of any new policy moves, while a raft of statements by Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda had little impact.\par \par \uc2  Noda, who met with Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku, said he had not received any specific instructions from Kan on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, adding that he could not comment on the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.\par \par \uc2  The Nikkei pared losses slightly in late trade, though, after government sources said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was not ruling out \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a policy option.\par \par \uc2  "There have been a lot of reports hinting that something might happen with the BOJ, and a lot of statements, but the growing market feeling is 'just do it'," said Koichi Ogawa, chief portfolio manager at \uc2 Daiwa SB Investments\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "The consensus is that solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  might not have much of an impact, but investors keep on expecting something -- and all we have is talk."\par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei shed 1.7 percent to 8,845.39, its lowest close since late April 2009, though still well above the 26-year closing low just over 7,000 hit in March 2009. The broader Topix lost 1.3 percent to 807.31.\par \par \uc2  The Nikkei has lost some 3.6 percent so far this week.\par \par \uc2  The dollar recouped some ground to 84.34 on electronic trading platform EBS after hitting a 15-year low of 83.58 yen the day before.\par \par \uc2  Market players said the longer it took for the Nikkei to recover above 9,000, which long served as a key support level, the gloomier the outlook was likely to be.\par \par \uc2  The benchmark Nikkei broke below 9,000 on Tuesday for the first time since May 2009. The 9,000 to 9,100 area had been strong support since last year, and market players said there were few technical targets to break the benchmark's fall.\par \par \uc2  "That the Nikkei has broken below 9,000, which has been very strong support for a while, is a significant event and it's urgent that Japanese authorities take action," said Koichi Nosaka, a market analyst at Securities Japan Inc.\par \par \uc2  "If the index stays below that level for too long, 9,000 will eventually become solid resistance."\par \par \uc2  Some market players said speculative selling of Nikkei futures helped drive the benchmark lower, and while there was talk of buying at the lows by pension funds it was difficult to confirm.\par \par \uc2  By some technical measures, the Nikkei is also starting to look oversold and perhaps due for a bit of a rebound.\par \par \uc2  Its relative strength index (RSI) fell to 32, with 30 and under considered oversold, while its slow stochastic fell deeper into oversold territory. The Nikkei also fell through its lower Bollinger Band.\par \par \uc2  GROWING PESSIMISM\par \par \uc2  Still, pessimism about global growth has become contagious in recent weeks after lacklustre U.S. employment and consumer reports. Fears were reinforced on Tuesday by a report showing U.S. existing house sales slid much more than expected in July after the government ended homebuyer tax credits.\par \par \uc2  "There are also a lot of important indicators coming up, and bad numbers could increase the chance of the Federal Reserve taking some kind of policy steps," said Nagayuki Yamagishi, a strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\par \par \uc2 Honda Motor Co\uc2  and other exporters slid.\par \par \uc2  Many Japanese exporters have set their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate assumptions around 90 yen per dollar for the financial year to next March, although Honda cut its assumption to 87 yen from 90 yen. A stronger yen eats into exporter profits when repatriated.\par \par \uc2  Honda lost 3.1 percent to 2,718 yen, \uc2 TDK Corp\uc2  shed 3.2 percent to 4,240 yen and stepper maker \uc2 Nikon Corp\uc2  fell 2.6 percent to 1,372 yen.\par \par \uc2  Among other notable stocks, Nippon Sheet Glass sank 5 percent to 189 yen after it said it would sell up to 49 billion yen ($576 million) of new shares to raise funds to ramp up output of glass for emerging economies.\par \par \uc2  The offering will boost the number of shares outstanding by 35 percent.\par \par \uc2  Trade picked up, with some 1.78 billion shares changing hands on the Tokyo exchange's first section, its highest volume in about two weeks.\par \par \uc2  Declining stocks outnumbered advancing ones by nearly 3 to 1.  (Editing by Michael Watson)        \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-STOCKS (UPDATE 6)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|SXNA   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100824e68o001cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
