\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: Getting Ready To Deal With Yen Bulls?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 820 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2010\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--We could be watching Japan build a trap for yen bulls.\par \par \uc2 With concern over the global recovery still rising and with global appetite for risk still falling, the yen is likely to remain in demand as a safe haven.\par \par \uc2 This isn't good news for Japanese exporters, who have watched a decline in the dollar down to nearly Y85 erode their profits in the last few months.\par \par \uc2 See the dollar's fall against the yen in recent months:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4476"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4476}}}\par \par \uc2 Pressure on the Japanese authorities to do something about the yen's strength is only likely to grow later this week as new data from Japan, including unemployment, consumer prices and household spending, will reinforce forecasts for continued slow growth and more deflation.\par \par \uc2 Also on Tuesday, the Nikkei Index fell under 9000 for the first time since May 2009 because of exasperation over the lack of policy response.\par \par \uc2 So far, though, there has been little concrete sign of action by either the government or the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and yen bulls have been getting bolder.\par \par \uc2 Direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been largely ruled out, given the accusations Tokyo would face from the U.S. of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.\par \par \uc2 This is certainly something the Japanese authorities would want to avoid ahead of the next meeting of finance ministers from the Group of Seven nations early next month.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there has been a lot of talk that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will ease monetary policy instead. This is being seen as another, more diplomatic, means of weakening the yen.\par \par \uc2 Speculation over such a move has been rife over the last week with the Tokyo rumor-mill going into overdrive about a meeting between Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Bank of Japan Governor Misaaki Shirakawa.\par \par \uc2 In the event, the two are said to have held only a 15-minute telephone conversation that didn't cover \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This lack of policy action has only encouraged the view that there is no consensus between the government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  over how to tackle the strong-yen issue.\par \par \uc2 With the risk of early official action apparently removed, the yen has become that much more attractive as a safe haven, and investors have pushed it a little higher.\par \par \uc2 The latest data from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange showed that although yen long positions may have been reduced a little in the week to last Tuesday, they remained close to historical highs.\par \par \uc2 Also, the increase in safe-haven demand that has taken place since last Tuesday means that there is a good chance that yen positions have been increased again.\par \par \uc2 And this could be just what the Japanese authorities want.\par \par \uc2 By boosting market confidence that nothing is imminent, and encouraging more investors to go long of the yen, the Bank of Japan could be building the element of surprise.\par \par \uc2 So if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does decide to make an unexpected policy change, the yen could fall sharply, springing a trap that would squeeze long positions and push the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  much lower.\par \par \uc2 This would certainly help to explain the complete lack of coherent policy coming out of Tokyo despite the growing pressure from corporate Japan for action.\par \par \uc2 Early Tuesday in Europe, the dollar slipped a little more against the yen, falling to Y84.94 from Y85.25 late on Monday in New York, according to EBS, as global risk sentiment took another hammering.\par \par \uc2 Contributing to the deterioration are a disappointing performance in global stock markets, a warning by a newest member of the Bank of England monetary policy committee that there is a "significant" risk of the U.K. falling back into recession, and expectations of another sharp fall in U.S. existing home sales later in the day.\par \par \uc2 The euro fell to Y107.29 from Y107.95, at one stage hitting a 8-year 9-month low at Y107.21.\par \par \uc2 The euro was also down at $1.2635 from $1.2665.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 24-08-10 0715GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1066   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100824e68o000i3\par