\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: NZX-50 Down 0.2%; More Weakness Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,907 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 01:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.2% at 3019.51, likely to drift down further once Australian market opens, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker Grant Williamson; "I think when Australia opens its likely to be weak on the back of a weak (DJIA) and weak commodity pricing." Adds Michael Hill International (MHI.NZ) +3.1% at NZ$0.67 after Hill family says wants to increase stake to 50.1% from 48%; "obviously the market is viewing it as positive if the family want to increase their holding." Says Skellerup (SKL.NZ) +8.2% at NZ$0.79 after strong results, positive outlook; "they're talking of a further profit increase in the new financial year and that's got investors very encouraged." Adds \uc2 NZ Farming Systems Uruguay\uc2  (NZS.NZ) +2.9% at NZ$0.72 as investors excited about bidding war following \uc2 Olam International\uc2  (032.SG) putting in revised takeover offer yesterday; "it looks like it's going to be an ongoing battle there between a number of different parties - investors love that sort of thing." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely down about 1.0%-1.5% this morning after S&P 500 fell 1.5% on weak U.S. existing home sales data. On the charts, recent support near 4380 should turn into resistance and the July 19 low at 4345.2 should be tested in early trading. There's also Fibonacci support at 4340, so that area could prove solid today, although IG Markets tips 1.3% opening fall to 4325. In that case, index could bounce off the lower Bollinger band at that level, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. With BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) expected to report a very strong full-year profit later today, materials probably won't fully reflect the 2.3% fall in U.S. peers, although steep declines in U.S. materials, financials and industrials should limit upside potential. With traders focussed on earnings reports today, S&P/ASX 200 might stall after an opening slide. However, the bear flag pattern targets 4223, so S&P/ASX 200 could well retest major support from May/July lows at 4170-4182 in coming days. Index last 4381.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2339 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may open lower on overnight falls in U.S. stocks; "the record drop in U.S. existing-home sales escalated investors' concerns about a double-dip recession. If revised 2Q U.S. GDP turns out lower this Friday than the previously forecast 2.4%, domestic stock markets will also get a blow from it," says Bae Sung-young at \uc2 Hyundai Securities\uc2 ; tips benchmark index may fall to 1740 today, but 1700 expected to hold even if 2Q GDP revised lower. Yesterday, Kospi ends down 0.4% at 1760.53. (kyong-ae.choi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2339 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD fell after U.S. housing data for July came in well under expectations, further fueling doubts about pace of U.S. recovery but news at end of session \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  had downgraded Ireland's long-term sovereign credit rating to AA- from AA put pressure on risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . USD fell against JPY though it managed to pull back above its 15-year low, at Y83.58, hit in immediate aftermath of housing data. Dismal housing data prompted "broad knee-jerk reaction against the dollar," with concerns growing Fed will take further steps to stimulate economy, says \uc2 UBS\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Brian Kim. With thin summer volumes exacerbating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, fears of global slowdown mounting, "people are more jumpy and jittery than usual," says Kim. EUR/CHF fell to 1.3049, dropping below previous all-time low of 1.3073 from July 1. Early in NZ pair then broke through that level, touched 1.3023 in wake of \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  news; last 1.3037. EUR/USD at 1.2674 late NY vs 1.2665 late Monday in New York; pair dipped to 1.2617 early in New Zealand trade, last at 1.2633. GBP/USD at 1.5433 late NY vs 1.5520; also fell early in NZ, touching 1.5392, last at 1.5453. EUR/JPY at 106.64 late NY vs 107.95, last 106.39. USD/CHF at 1.0312 late NY vs 1.0405, last 1.0323. USD Index 83.139 late NY vs 83.143, last at 83.312. U.S. Stocks fell as record drop in U.S. existing-home sales added to investors' worries about health of economy. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  declined 3.7%, \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  fell 3%, Caterpillar slipped 2.7%. \uc2 Medtronic\uc2  fell 11% on lowered earnings forecast. DJIA down 1.3%, Nasdaq off 1.7%, Philly Semicons fell 1.7%. Treasury market posted broad-based rally on demand for safer assets; 2-year yields down 1.2 bp at 0.47%, 10-year down 10.0 bp at 2.50%. Crude futures settled at lowest level in more than two months, falling on weakening equities markets, housing data. October Nymex Crude settled $1.42 lower at $71.63/bbl. December Comex gold rose $4.90 to $1,233.40/oz as investors fled to perceived safety of metal. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2336 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to rise, possibly aim for a test above key 1,200 resistance vs 1,191.0 late yesterday on escalating concerns over global economic health after disappointing U.S. existing-home sales data overnight. Offshore players may opt for betting on stronger JPY/KRW as well, with JPY heading for fresh highs vs USD in broader market on increasing investor preference for safer \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. Spot USD/KRW tipped to gap higher after 1-month NDFs last end around 1,197 in NY. Some resistance expected near 1,200 level, as pair's attempt at breaching that level had failed