kely to fall after USD/JPY (now 84.20) slid to 15-year low in New York trading following sharply disappointing existing U.S. homes sales data for July. Analysts put Nikkei range for today at 8750-9000 vs 8995.14 close Tuesday (lowest level since May 2009). Close attention on further comments from government or BOJ officials after Nikkei newspaper and broadcaster NHK reported \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering taking additional easing steps; MOF may also consider unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on any further sharp single-day rise in yen, according to Nikkei. "While many see unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as ineffective, markets will see lack of inaction by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as acceptance of stronger yen," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Exporters likely to face selling pressure while flight to defensive shares expected to continue. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 8850 vs Osaka close at 8970.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall sharply again if BOJ fails to hold emergency meeting to take additional easing steps today, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior dealer Kenichi Nishii. "It's not a matter of whether the BOJ wants to do it or does not want to do it. They should, otherwise markets will get disappointed," he says. Adds USD/JPY fell to 83.58 overnight but has since backed up, last at 84.13. Nishii says that's due to expectations that BOJ will do something later in global day. Note, national broadcaster NHK reported Wednesday BOJ considering taking monetary easing measures after yen's overnight rise, Nikkei's fall below 9,000 mark. Tips 83.50-84.50 range. EUR/JPY may fall as well; tipped in 105.00-107.00 range vs last 106.32. EUR/USD may trade in 1.2550-1.2700 range vs last 1.2630. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 