 where it was expected: over 50-day average at $1,210, from where it bounced sharply higher. Yesterday's trading pattern "implies strong demand at lower levels and keeps our focus higher over the rest of the week," it says; adds resistance between $1,242/$1,245 represents primary obstacle ahead, likely a formidable obstacle this week, ahead of all-time high at $1,262.31. \uc2 Barclays\uc2  bullish and break above $1,245, opens way for all-time high, then $1,300, it says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2323 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 11.1 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 5th straight day of premarket selling may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 23.2 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 12.1 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to fall after USD/JPY (now 84.20) slid to 15-year low in New York trading following sharply disappointing existing U.S. homes sales data for July. Analysts put Nikkei range for today at 8750-9000 vs 8995.14 close Tuesday (lowest level since May 2009). Close attention on further comments from government or BOJ officials after Nikkei newspaper and broadcaster NHK reported \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering taking additional easing steps; MOF may also consider unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on any further sharp single-day rise in yen, according to Nikkei. "While many see unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as ineffective, markets will see lack of inaction by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as acceptance of stronger yen," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Exporters likely to face selling pressure while flight to defensive shares expected to continue. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 8850 vs Osaka close at 8970.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall sharply again if BOJ fails to hold emergency meeting to take additional easing steps today, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior dealer Kenichi Nishii. "It's not a matter of whether the BOJ wants to do it or does not want to do it. They should, otherwise markets will get disappointed," he says. Adds USD/JPY fell to 83.58 overnight but has since backed up, last at 84.13. Nishii says that's due to expectations that BOJ will do something later in global day. Note, national broadcaster NHK reported Wednesday BOJ considering taking monetary easing measures after yen's overnight rise, Nikkei's fall below 9,000 mark. Tips 83.50-84.50 range. EUR/JPY may fall as well; tipped in 105.00-107.00 range vs last 106.32. EUR/USD may trade in 1.2550-1.2700 range vs last 1.2630. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures trade a few cents lower as market awaits weekly U.S. oil inventory data from DOE, and after industry group API releases own data showing larger-than-expected drop in crude stockpiles, but unexpected builds in fuel inventories; API says U.S. crude inventories fell by 1.8 million bbls last week vs analysts' expectations of 200,000-bbl drop; API says gasoline inventories rose by 692,000 bbls vs expectations for 500,000-bbl drop; API says distillate stockpiles rose by 1.9 million bbls, double expected level. API inventory report is "supportive for crude, but bearish for products," says Citi analyst Tim Evans. October crude recently 8 cents off at $71.55/bbl. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher on report by national broadcaster NHK that BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy amid rapidly surging yen; 10-year cash JGB yield expected to breach below psychologically important 0.90% mark, seek room for further downside, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. Adds, however, some profit-taking selling may also be possible on rally. Lead futures tipped in 143.05-143.25 band vs 142.92 last close, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.880%-0.900% vs 0.910% yesterday. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1944ET ]\par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely hit by Wall Street's decline after news of plunge in U.S. home sales in July fuels worries about recovery. Japanese exporters likely to be sold off with USD/JPY now trading well below the 85 handle. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, risk aversion picks up again; EUR/CHF hit record low of 1.3023 early Asian trade as S&P downgrades Ireland's long-term sovereign credit rating to AA-minus from AA because of high cost to prop country's financial sector, EUR/CHF last 1.3031. EUR/USD at 1.2633 vs 1.2674 in late NY trade Tuesday. USD/JPY at 84.23 vs 84.15 late NY, after hitting 15-year low of 83.58 overnight on the housing data. Chatter Japanese officials will do something about JPY strength picking up with Nikkei reporting BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy. On data slate, there's Japan corporate services price index, provisional trade at 2350 GMT, Philippines trade at 0100 GMT, Australia construction work done at 0130 GMT, \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  monetary policy committee meeting at 0700 GMT. Later there's German Ifo index, U.S. mortgage applications, durable goods orders, new residential sales, EIA oil report. (colin.ng@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1944ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100824e68o000fl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
