ata from DOE, and after industry group API releases own data showing larger-than-expected drop in crude stockpiles, but unexpected builds in fuel inventories; API says U.S. crude inventories fell by 1.8 million bbls last week vs analysts' expectations of 200,000-bbl drop; API says gasoline inventories rose by 692,000 bbls vs expectations for 500,000-bbl drop; API says distillate stockpiles rose by 1.9 million bbls, double expected level. API inventory report is "supportive for crude, but bearish for products," says Citi analyst Tim Evans. October crude recently 8 cents off at $71.55/bbl. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher on report by national broadcaster NHK that BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy amid rapidly surging yen; 10-year cash JGB yield expected to breach below psychologically important 0.90% mark, seek room for further downside, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. Adds, however, some profit-taking selling may also be possible on rally. Lead futures tipped in 143.05-143.25 band vs 142.92 last close, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.880%-0.900% vs 0.910% yesterday. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely hit by Wall Street's decline after news of plunge in U.S. home sales in July fuels worries about recovery. Japanese exporters likely to be sold off with USD/JPY now trading well below the 85 handle. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, risk aversion picks up again; EUR/CHF hit record low of 1.3023 early Asian trade as S&P downgrades Ireland's long-term sovereign credit rating to AA-minus from AA because of high cost to prop country's financial sector, EUR/CHF last 1.3031. EUR/USD at 1.2633 vs 1.2674 in late NY trade Tuesday. USD/JPY at 84.23 vs 84.15 late NY, after hitting 15-year low of 83.58 overnight on the housing data. Chatter Japanese officials will do something about JPY strength picking up with Nikkei reporting BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy. On data slate, there's Japan corporate services price index, provisional trade at 2350 GMT, Philippines trade at 0100 GMT, Australia construction work done at 0130 GMT, \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  monetary policy committee meeting at 0700 GMT. Later there's German Ifo index, U.S. mortgage applications, durable goods orders, new residential sales, EIA oil report. (colin.ng@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1939ET ]\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee expected to raise benchmark rate 25 bps to 1.75% at next meeting as robust exports continue to give economy a boost; inflationary pressures are expected to soon appear, with solid economic momentum from 1H extending into 3Q. BOT has already signaled price pressures are likely to be higher in 2011. Further fueling market expectations are repeated BOT comments on need to return rate to more normal level. Nine of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires predict BOT will raise policy rate by 25 bps, while one forecasts it will hold steady. Most economists expect key one-day repurchase rate to touch 2.0% by year-end. "The BOT wants to...bring the policy rate to a more normalized level, that's a clear message," said Pornthep Jubandhu, manager of macroeconomics analysis at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 's Economics Intelligence Center. Rate decision due to be released around 0730 GMT. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1939ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100824e68o000fk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
