 will see lack of inaction by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as acceptance of stronger yen," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Exporters likely to face selling pressure while flight to defensive shares expected to continue. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 8850 vs Osaka close at 8970.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall sharply again if BOJ fails to hold emergency meeting to take additional easing steps today, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior dealer Kenichi Nishii. "It's not a matter of whether the BOJ wants to do it or does not want to do it. They should, otherwise markets will get disappointed," he says. Adds USD/JPY fell to 83.58 overnight but has since backed up, last at 84.13. Nishii says that's due to expectations that BOJ will do something later in global day. Note, national broadcaster NHK reported Wednesday BOJ considering taking monetary easing measures after yen's overnight rise, Nikkei's fall below 9,000 mark. Tips 83.50-84.50 range. EUR/JPY may fall as well; tipped in 105.00-107.00 range vs last 106.32. EUR/USD may trade in 1.2550-1.2700 range vs last 1.2630. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures trade a few cents lower as market awaits weekly U.S. oil inventory data from DOE, and after industry group API releases own data showing larger-than-expected drop in crude stockpiles, but unexpected builds in fuel inventories; API says U.S. crude inventories fell by 1.8 million bbls last week vs analysts' expectations of 200,000-bbl drop; API says gasoline inventories rose by 692,000 bbls vs expectations for 500,000-bbl drop; API says distillate stockpiles rose by 1.9 million bbls, double expected level. API inventory report is "supportive for crude, but bearish for products," says Citi analyst Tim Evans. October crude recently 8 cents off at $71.55/bbl. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher on report by national broadcaster NHK that BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy amid rapidly surging yen; 10-year cash JGB yield expected to breach below psychologically important 0.90% mark, seek room for further downside, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. Adds, however, some profit-taking selling may also be possible on rally. Lead futures tipped in 143.05-143.25 band vs 142.92 last close, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.880%-0.900% vs 0.910% yesterday. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely hit by Wall Street's decline after news of plunge in U.S. home sales in July fuels worries about recovery. Japanese exporters likely to be sold off with USD/JPY now trading well below the 85 handle. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, risk aversion picks up again; EUR/CHF hit record low of 1.3023 early Asian trade as S&P downgrades Ireland's long-term sovereign credit rating to AA-minus from AA because of high cost to prop country's financial sector, EUR/CHF last 1.3031. EUR/USD at 1.2633 vs 1.2674 in late NY trade Tuesday. USD/JPY at 84.23 vs 84.15 late NY, after hitting 15-year low of 83.58 overnight on the housing data. Chatter Japanese officials will do something about JPY strength picking up with Nikkei reporting BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy. On data slate, there's Japan corporate services price index, provisional trade at 2350 GMT, Philippines trade at 0100 GMT, Australia construction work done at 0130 GMT, \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  monetary policy committee meeting at 0700 GMT. Later there's German Ifo index, U.S. mortgage applications, durable goods orders, new residential sales, EIA oil report. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee expected to raise benchmark rate 25 bps to 1.75% at next meeting as robust exports continue to give economy a boost; inflationary pressures are expected to soon appear, with solid economic momentum from 1H extending into 3Q. BOT has already signaled price pressures are likely to be higher in 2011. Further fueling market expectations are repeated BOT comments on need to return rate to more normal level. Nine of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires predict BOT will raise policy rate by 25 bps, while one forecasts it will hold steady. Most economists expect key one-day repurchase rate to touch 2.0% by year-end. "The BOT wants to...bring the policy rate to a more normalized level, that's a clear message," said Pornthep Jubandhu, manager of macroeconomics analysis at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 's Economics Intelligence Center. Rate decision due to be released around 0730 GMT. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1939ET ]\par \par \uc2 2247 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bearish with overnight SPI 200 futures down 57 points, or 1.3%, at 4312.0 after offshore equities slumped on weak economic data. DJIA down 1.3% at 10040.45 (4th straight fall), S&P 500 down 1.5% at 1051.87, Nasdaq down 1.7% at 2123.76. U.S. July existing home sales fell 27% to annual rate of 3.83 million (15-year low) vs 4.6 million expected. Chicago Fed's Evans said U.S. economic recovery is "extremely modest" and pace of recovery "slower than we had hoped for" but he said double dip recession unlikely. Industrials, financials and materials suffered steep declines. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$37.08 (based on AUD/USD at 0.8832) vs local close at A$37.54. In commodity markets, Nymex crude fell 2.0% to US$71.63, spot gold at US$1,230.80 vs US$1,224.96, LME copper down 1.6%, zinc down 2.6%, aluminum down 0.6%, nickel down 2.8%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.2635 vs 1.2653. AUD/USD 0.8832 vs 0.8864. S&P/ASX 200 last 4381.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1939ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100824e68o000fj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
