ing into 3Q. BOT has already signaled price pressures are likely to be higher in 2011. Further fueling market expectations are repeated BOT comments on need to return rate to more normal level. Nine of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires predict BOT will raise policy rate by 25 bps, while one forecasts it will hold steady. Most economists expect key one-day repurchase rate to touch 2.0% by year-end. "The BOT wants to...bring the policy rate to a more normalized level, that's a clear message," said Pornthep Jubandhu, manager of macroeconomics analysis at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 's Economics Intelligence Center. Rate decision due to be released around 0730 GMT. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2247 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bearish with overnight SPI 200 futures down 57 points, or 1.3%, at 4312.0 after offshore equities slumped on weak economic data. DJIA down 1.3% at 10040.45 (4th straight fall), S&P 500 down 1.5% at 1051.87, Nasdaq down 1.7% at 2123.76. U.S. July existing home sales fell 27% to annual rate of 3.83 million (15-year low) vs 4.6 million expected. Chicago Fed's Evans said U.S. economic recovery is "extremely modest" and pace of recovery "slower than we had hoped for" but he said double dip recession unlikely. Industrials, financials and materials suffered steep declines. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$37.08 (based on AUD/USD at 0.8832) vs local close at A$37.54. In commodity markets, Nymex crude fell 2.0% to US$71.63, spot gold at US$1,230.80 vs US$1,224.96, LME copper down 1.6%, zinc down 2.6%, aluminum down 0.6%, nickel down 2.8%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.2635 vs 1.2653. AUD/USD 0.8832 vs 0.8864. S&P/ASX 200 last 4381.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD at risk for further drops after weak U.S. home sales data, S&P's cut of Ireland's sovereign credit rating weighed on cross overnight, says John Kyriakopoulos, \b 