uc2 2247 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bearish with overnight SPI 200 futures down 57 points, or 1.3%, at 4312.0 after offshore equities slumped on weak economic data. DJIA down 1.3% at 10040.45 (4th straight fall), S&P 500 down 1.5% at 1051.87, Nasdaq down 1.7% at 2123.76. U.S. July existing home sales fell 27% to annual rate of 3.83 million (15-year low) vs 4.6 million expected. Chicago Fed's Evans said U.S. economic recovery is "extremely modest" and pace of recovery "slower than we had hoped for" but he said double dip recession unlikely. Industrials, financials and materials suffered steep declines. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$37.08 (based on AUD/USD at 0.8832) vs local close at A$37.54. In commodity markets, Nymex crude fell 2.0% to US$71.63, spot gold at US$1,230.80 vs US$1,224.96, LME copper down 1.6%, zinc down 2.6%, aluminum down 0.6%, nickel down 2.8%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.2635 vs 1.2653. AUD/USD 0.8832 vs 0.8864. S&P/ASX 200 last 4381.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD at risk for further drops after weak U.S. home sales data, S&P's cut of Ireland's sovereign credit rating weighed on cross overnight, says John Kyriakopoulos, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 . "We continue to look for a further modest decline in AUD/USD to around 0.8600 over the next month or so, reflecting the RBA being on hold and uncertainty over the global economy's health. Key support is at 0.8780," writes strategist in morning note. AUD/USD recently at 0.8837, up from morning low of 0.8811. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Stocks fell as record drop in U.S. existing-home sales added to investors' worries about health of economy. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  declined 3.7%, \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  fell 3%, Caterpillar slipped 2.7%. Declines followed report of 27.2% plunge in U.S. existing-home sales in July, latest in string of disappointing economic reports despite recent uptick in M&A activity, which would typically be seen as sign of improving confidence; Amid conflicting indicators, "people would rather be hoarding cash," says Seth Setrakian, partner and co-head of equity trading for \uc2 First New York Securities\uc2 , a proprietary trading firm. "It's the uncertainty of whether a double-dip is coming that's causing people to be like this. We're consistently being bombarded with negative economic data, which is highlighted by the housing data we saw today. Yet on the other hand, you're seeing these significant premiums paid in M&A deals." \uc2 Medtronic\uc2  fell 11% as fiscal 1Q earnings jumped 87%, but medical-device giant lowered earnings forecast for year. \uc2 Medtronic\uc2 's report weighed on competitors. \uc2 Boston Scientific\uc2  dropped 2.5%, \uc2 St. Jude Medical\uc2  declined 3.4%, Stryker slid 6.4%, \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  shed 1.5%. Dow dropped 1.3%, Nasdaq shed 1.7%, Philly Semicons fell 1.7%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1935ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100824e68o000fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
