\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Nikkei May Fall; Fresh Cues From BOJ Awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 2,009 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 August 2010\par 01:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to fall after USD/JPY (now 84.20) slid to 15-year low in New York trading following sharply disappointing existing U.S. homes sales data for July. Analysts put Nikkei range for today at 8750-9000 vs 8995.14 close Tuesday (lowest level since May 2009). Close attention on further comments from government or BOJ officials after Nikkei newspaper and broadcaster NHK reported \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is considering taking additional easing steps; MOF may also consider unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on any further sharp single-day rise in yen, according to Nikkei. "While many see unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as ineffective, markets will see lack of inaction by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as acceptance of stronger yen," says Hiroichi Nishi, general manager at Nikko Cordial Securities. Exporters likely to face selling pressure while flight to defensive shares expected to continue. Nikkei 225 September futures ended Chicago trading at 8850 vs Osaka close at 8970.(kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2316 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall sharply again if BOJ fails to hold emergency meeting to take additional easing steps today, says \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior dealer Kenichi Nishii. "It's not a matter of whether the BOJ wants to do it or does not want to do it. They should, otherwise markets will get disappointed," he says. Adds USD/JPY fell to 83.58 overnight but has since backed up, last at 84.13. Nishii says that's due to expectations that BOJ will do something later in global day. Note, national broadcaster NHK reported Wednesday BOJ considering taking monetary easing measures after yen's overnight rise, Nikkei's fall below 9,000 mark. Tips 83.50-84.50 range. EUR/JPY may fall as well; tipped in 105.00-107.00 range vs last 106.32. EUR/USD may trade in 1.2550-1.2700 range vs last 1.2630. (takashi.mochizuki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude oil futures trade a few cents lower as market awaits weekly U.S. oil inventory data from DOE, and after industry group API releases own data showing larger-than-expected drop in crude stockpiles, but unexpected builds in fuel inventories; API says U.S. crude inventories fell by 1.8 million bbls last week vs analysts' expectations of 200,000-bbl drop; API says gasoline inventories rose by 692,000 bbls vs expectations for 500,000-bbl drop; API says distillate stockpiles rose by 1.9 million bbls, double expected level. API inventory report is "supportive for crude, but bearish for products," says Citi analyst Tim Evans. October crude recently 8 cents off at $71.55/bbl. (cassandra.sweet@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2309 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs likely to open higher on report by national broadcaster NHK that BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy amid rapidly surging yen; 10-year cash JGB yield expected to breach below psychologically important 0.90% mark, seek room for further downside, says Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities chief strategist Jun Ishii. Adds, however, some profit-taking selling may also be possible on rally. Lead futures tipped in 143.05-143.25 band vs 142.92 last close, 10-year cash JGB yield in 0.880%-0.900% vs 0.910% yesterday. (megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2301 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely hit by Wall Street's decline after news of plunge in U.S. home sales in July fuels worries about recovery. Japanese exporters likely to be sold off with USD/JPY now trading well below the 85 handle. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, risk aversion picks up again; EUR/CHF hit record low of 1.3023 early Asian trade as S&P downgrades Ireland's long-term sovereign credit rating to AA-minus from AA because of high cost to prop country's financial sector, EUR/CHF last 1.3031. EUR/USD at 1.2633 vs 1.2674 in late NY trade Tuesday. USD/JPY at 84.23 vs 84.15 late NY, after hitting 15-year low of 83.58 overnight on the housing data. Chatter Japanese officials will do something about JPY strength picking up with Nikkei reporting BOJ considering additional steps to loosen monetary policy. On data slate, there's Japan corporate services price index, provisional trade at 2350 GMT, Philippines trade at 0100 GMT, Australia construction work done at 0130 GMT, \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  monetary policy committee meeting at 0700 GMT. Later there's German Ifo index, U.S. mortgage applications, durable goods orders, new residential sales, EIA oil report. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2 's Monetary Policy Committee expected to raise benchmark rate 25 bps to 1.75% at next meeting as robust exports continue to give economy a boost; inflationary pressures are expected to soon appear, with solid economic momentum from 1H extending into 3Q. BOT has already signaled price pressures are likely to be higher in 2011. Further fueling market expectations are repeated BOT comments on need to return rate to more normal level. Nine of 10 economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires predict BOT will raise policy rate by 25 bps, while one forecasts it will hold steady. Most economists expect key one-day repurchase rate to touch 2.0% by year-end. "The BOT wants to...bring the policy rate to a more normalized level, that's a clear message," said Pornthep Jubandhu, manager of macroeconomics analysis at \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2 's Economics Intelligence Center. Rate decision due to be released around 0730 GMT. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2247 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads bearish with overnight SPI 200 futures down 57 points, or 1.3%, at 4312.0 after offshore equities slumped on weak economic data. DJIA down 1.3% at 10040.45 (4th straight fall), S&P 500 down 1.5% at 1051.87, Nasdaq down 1.7% at 2123.76. U.S. July existing home sales fell 27% to annual rate of 3.83 million (15-year low) vs 4.6 million expected. Chicago Fed's Evans said U.S. economic recovery is "extremely modest" and pace of recovery "slower than we had hoped for" but he said double dip recession unlikely. Industrials, financials and materials suffered steep declines. BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) ADRs at equivalent A$37.08 (based on AUD/USD at 0.8832) vs local close at A$37.54. In commodity markets, Nymex crude fell 2.0% to US$71.63, spot gold at US$1,230.80 vs US$1,224.96, LME copper down 1.6%, zinc down 2.6%, aluminum down 0.6%, nickel down 2.8%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.2635 vs 1.2653. AUD/USD 0.8832 vs 0.8864. S&P/ASX 200 last 4381.3. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD at risk for further drops after weak U.S. home sales data, S&P's cut of Ireland's sovereign credit rating weighed on cross overnight, says John Kyriakopoulos, \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2 . "We continue to look for a further modest decline in AUD/USD to around 0.8600 over the next month or so, reflecting the RBA being on hold and uncertainty over the global economy's health. Key support is at 0.8780," writes strategist in morning note. AUD/USD recently at 0.8837, up from morning low of 0.8811. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2244 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Stocks fell as record drop in U.S. existing-home sales added to investors' worries about health of economy. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  declined 3.7%, \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  fell 3%, Caterpillar slipped 2.7%. Declines followed report of 27.2% plunge in U.S. existing-home sales in July, latest in string of disappointing economic reports despite recent uptick in M&A activity, which would typically be seen as sign of improving confidence; Amid conflicting indicators, "people would rather be hoarding cash," says Seth Setrakian, partner and co-head of equity trading for \uc2 First New York Securities\uc2 , a proprietary trading firm. "It's the uncertainty of whether a double-dip is coming that's causing people to be like this. We're consistently being bombarded with negative economic data, which is highlighted by the housing data we saw today. Yet on the other hand, you're seeing these significant premiums paid in M&A deals." \uc2 Medtronic\uc2  fell 11% as fiscal 1Q earnings jumped 87%, but medical-device giant lowered earnings forecast for year. \uc2 Medtronic\uc2 's report weighed on competitors. \uc2 Boston Scientific\uc2  dropped 2.5%, \uc2 St. Jude Medical\uc2  declined 3.4%, Stryker slid 6.4%, \uc2 Johnson & Johnson\uc2  shed 1.5%. Dow dropped 1.3%, Nasdaq shed 1.7%, Philly Semicons fell 1.7%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2236 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices to fall today after U.S. wheat prices tumbled overnight on profit-taking amid hopes that wetter weather will promote planting in former Soviet states hit hard by drought. Market participants concerned about reduced production from crop now being cut but think rain may improve outlook for the next crop. Soft red winter wheat for September delivery on Tuesday fell 17.50 cents, or 2.5%, to US$6.7475/bushel on the Chicago Board of Trade. Weaker AUD will moderate wheat's decline, AUD/USD last 0.8832, down from 0.8980 early yesterday. Yesterday, most active ASX milling wheat deliverable January in NSW settled unchanged at A$291.00; ASX milling wheat deliverable January in WA rose A$3.50 to A$313.50. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1916ET ]\par \par \uc2 2229 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: LME base metals fell yesterday after disappointing U.S. housing data sparked a selloff across many commodity and equity markets, while U.S. Treasurys rallied as investors sought safe havens from an increasingly gloomy economic outlook for the U.S. "The U.S. data made a lot of people panic and people sold on the numbers," said a senior metals trader in London, after existing home sales in U.S. in July plunged 27% to lowest level in 15 years. LME 3-month copper slumped $119 vs Monday PM kerb to $7,136/metric ton, zinc slumped $53 at $1,990, lead lost $36 at $2,012, nickel tumbled $600 to $20,600, tin slipped $50 at $20,400, while aluminum eased $12 at $2,028. Meanwhile, investors bid up gold prices yesterday as they fled to its perceived safety after news of U.S. housing sales slump emerged. "We definitely had the flight to quality today," said Sterling Smith, market analyst at \uc2 Country Hedging\uc2  in Inver Grove Heights, Minn. "Gold is working as a proxy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as people are looking to diversify." Spot gold early in Sydney bid $1,231.30/oz, up 80 cents from late NY, up about $6 from this time yesterday, bouncing of a low around $1,210. Of other precious metals, silver loses 2 cent from late NY at $18.36/oz, platinum adds $1 at $1,512, palladium unchanged at $483. Nymex October crude futures lost $1.47 overnight at $71.46/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2224 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY crosses against host of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be volatile in early morning trade, says Richard Franulovich, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Westpac\uc2 . Early Wednesday, national broadcaster NHK reported the Bank of Japan is considering taking additional easing steps to pre-empt adverse effects from recent yen strengthening. "Volume plunged (overnight) and then had a pretty decent prop on heightened fears of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . On the whole, yen crosses" are in focus, says Franulovich. AUD/JPY recently at 74.335, down from 75.40 late yesterday, pushing through solid support at 75. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com) [ 08-24-10 1916ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100824e68o000fe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
