\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Fall As Worries On Economy Outweigh Deal Activity\b0\par\par\uc2 2,537 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 August 2010\par 00:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA       10174.41 -39.21  -0.38% \par Nasdaq     2159.63  -20.13  -0.92% \par S&P 500    1067.36   -4.33  -0.40% \par FTSE 100   5234.84  +39.56  +0.76% \par Xetra DAX  6010.91   +5.75  +0.10% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 8/32  flat  0.49% -0.4BP \par 5Y Tsy 101 18/32  +4/32 1.42% -2.8BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 5/32  +2/32 2.61% -0.7BP \par  \par USD/JPY       85.20-22     -0.41% \par Range         85.68-85.09 \par EUR/USD       1.2658-60    -0.35% \par Range         1.2731-1.2647 \par AUD/USD       0.8911-14    +0.46% \par Range         0.8982-0.8865 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   10.60/11.00% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   12.04/12.46% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   13.30/13.90% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,228.50 -30 cents \par Comex Gold Range    $1,223.50-$1,234.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $73.10   -$0.72 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stocks fell Monday despite acquisition offers from companies including \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  as investors remained concerned about broad economic weakness.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average declined 39.21 points, or 0.38%, to 10174.41, its third straight session in the red. Caterpillar was the measure's worst performer, off 2.02, or 2.9%, to 66.84. Among the Dow's other weak performers, \uc2 Cisco\uc2  declined 55 cents, or 2.5%, to 21.68, and H-P slipped 81 cents, or 2%, to 39.04.\par \par \uc2 H-P's decline came as the technology giant launched a bidding war for small-capitalization stock \uc2 3PAR\uc2 , making a $1.6 billion bid for the data-storage company that tops an earlier offer from Dell. Dell, which isn't a Dow component, declined 13 cents, or 1.1%, to 11.94.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite dropped 20.13, or 0.92%, to 2159.63, and the Standard & Poor's 500 index fell 4.33, or 0.40%, to 1067.36.\par \par \uc2 Stocks had gained earlier in the session as the market initially took increased deal activity as a sign that companies are getting more confident to use their cash. However, as they mulled it over, investors reached the conclusion an increase in mergers and acquisitions won't necessarily alleviate the concerns of high unemployment and sluggish consumer spending. The deal activity could even add to the economic weakness as companies tend to follow mergers with layoffs.\par \par \uc2 "It's good to see deals," but significant concerns remain about "the more macro things in terms of the employment picture and the slowdown in growth," said \uc2 Thomas Nyheim\uc2 , portfolio manager at \uc2 Christiana Bank & Trust\uc2 . He said those worries are likely to keep weighing on the market regardless of deal activity.\par \par \uc2 China's shares ended slightly lower Monday as real estate developers fell on Beijing's reiteration Saturday of the need to curb property speculation, while some banks weakened amid continued concerns about exposure to high-risk loans to local governments.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely continue to consolidate in a range of 2600-2700 this week in the absence of fresh market-moving factors.\par \par \uc2 "Property shares cannot muster the momentum to keep up their rebound when Beijing keeps up its tough stance on tightening the real estate market," said Zhuo Xiangyu, an analyst at China Securities Information.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Beijing has repeatedly called recently on various local governments to clean up their own financing platforms set up to fund initiatives ranging from real estate development to infrastructure projects plagued by overcapacity.\par \par \uc2 "The local government financing vehicles are a significant source of business for banks, so there's not much reason for banks to rise unless there's a policy change," said Zhou Xu, an analyst at Nanjing Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks posted moderate declines Monday, with the Nikkei Stock Average closing at its lowest level for the year, after Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa appeared to make little progress on tackling the weak economy in what had been an eagerly anticipated discussion.\par \par \uc2 Market analysts saw the low-key discussion as a way to bide more time.\par \par \uc2 "Their decision not to address anything today was wise," said Norihiro Fujito, senior investment strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. "The BOJ may want to wait until the next policy meeting (on Sept. 6 and 7) to use the trump card to actually discuss the yen."\par \par \uc2 Fujito said that even if the two had signaled some action to curb the yen, upcoming U.S. jobs data, due Sept. 3, could swamp any reassuring comments from Japan. Fujito said that in such a situation "the trump card would be a waste."\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, market observers said that the Nikkei may now be supported above the 9000 level until the BOJ meeting--and if the dollar holds above the key level of Y85.00.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures settled lower in U.S. trading Monday as concerns about demand coupled with rising inventories, particularly of gasoline, pushed prices near $73 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures were steady Monday near seven-week highs as participants remained on edge about the sustainability of the economic recovery but didn't want to purchase new positions ahead of economic data later in the week.\par \par \uc2 Base metals ended mostly lower on the London Metal Exchange Monday in a choppy session with little clear direction. Worries about slowing growth and a sluggish performance in equity markets kept trading activity to a minimum. A lack of significant economic data also dulled trading interest, traders said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro fell against the dollar and yen Monday after concerns about the pace of global growth, exacerbated by disappointing euro-zone data, boosted the traditional safe harbors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen was also stronger against the dollar, extending earlier gains made as investors speculated that Japanese officials may be further away from taking action to slow the yen's appreciation than previously thought.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With trading flows thin due to late-summer vacations, investors are guarded, said Ron Leven, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. The cautious mood weighed most heavily on the euro, which hit a near two-month low against the yen and a five-week low against the dollar, Leven said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The euro is the most vulnerable to a slowdown," Leven said. "The southern European countries that are going through all these somersaults trying to get their deficits down...won't get the tax revenues they were expecting."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 After paring their anti-euro bets for six straight weeks, speculators increased their bets against the euro in the week ended Aug. 17, suggesting investors are becoming less supportive of the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . Net speculative bets against the euro, called shorts, rose to 14,600 contracts with a value of $2.3 billion, from 3,700 contracts with a value of $595 million in the prior week, according to data Friday from the Commodity Futures Trading Commission.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Monday in New York, the euro was at $1.2665, down from $1.2706 late Friday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y85.25, below Y85.75. The euro weakened to Y107.95 from Y108.71, after falling as low as Y107.70. The U.K. pound was at $1.5520, up from $1.5490. The U.S. dollar advanced to CHF1.0405 from CHF1.0375.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro's performance was further hampered by data showing the region's private sector expanded at a slower pace in August, weighed down by both the manufacturing and services sectors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The disappointing data came after comments Friday by European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  official Axel Weber--usually seen to be one of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's most conservative members--who said the ECB should offer unlimited liquidity for Europe's banks into 2011.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro's struggles are "a lingering legacy" of the euro-zone sovereign debt crisis, said Daragh Maher, deputy head of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategy at Credit Agricole CIB in London. Fears are increasing that the severe austerity measures put in place to combat soaring deficits in some euro-zone countries will negatively affect growth in the region.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite the generally downbeat tone in markets, the Swiss franc, which also often functions as a safe-haven \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was down against both the dollar and the euro. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  President \f28 \uc2 \f2 Philipp Hildebrand\f28 \uc2 \f2  reiterated in a newspaper interview Saturday that preserving price stability remains the bank's main goal. Hildebrand noted that should deflation again become a threat, the bank could certainly act to prevent a further appreciation of the franc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The SNB began \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets in March 2009 to hinder the franc's strengthening against the euro, spending billions of francs to buy the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The SNB is believed to have halted its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in June. The euro's all-time low is at CHF1.3072 and it traded at CHF1.3182 in New York Monday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors also questioned the likelihood of market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese officials after a Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  representative said that Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa didn't discuss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  measures during a much-anticipated phone conversation Monday. Many analysts and investors viewed the dialogue as indicating a lower degree of government pressure on the BOJ than had been expected.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 News that Australia's general election over the weekend failed to deliver a clear winner sent the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  plummeting, but the Australian dollar had pared its losses by the New York session.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The uncertainty following the Australian election is likely to cause some near-term volatility," said analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding a coalition government involving the Green party would heighten fears of a mining tax and put the Australian dollar under pressure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. government bond market put in a somnolent performance Monday in a session without major economic data, although there was good demand for a sale of long-dated inflation-indexed securities. [ 23-08-10 2200GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market drifted to the finish line with very small gains for short-dated securities, while the 30-year bond lost a minimal amount of ground. Markets had little to set the tone for the day, given that there were no major economic data scheduled for release.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Treasury sold $7 billion in 30-year inflation-indexed bonds to decent demand. The reopened securities, commonly referred to as TIPS, came at a yield of 1.768%. The security was first sold on Feb. 26. Its bid-to-cover ratio, a measure of investor interest, was 2.78, a record high. The indirect bid, a proxy for large buyer interest, was 39%, down from 42.4% in the winter sale.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is a good showing for the reopening," said David Ader of CRT Capital Group. But he added a note of caution, saying, "the history of 30-year TIPS is limited even if you extend history to the first issuance cycle that ended in 2001." He added, though, the yield on the issue "speaks to demand."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Given the lack of major data and the lack of action in the stock market, the TIPS auction pretty much was the show, with the market trading calmly throughout the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At 1916 GMT, the 10-year Treasury note stood at 100 5/32, up 2/32 to yield 2.61%. The 30-year Treasury was down 3/32 at 103 25/32, yielding 3.67%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The five-year price rose by 4/32 and yielded 1.42%, while the two-year was flat for a yield of 0.49%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Monday, market participants mostly overlooked the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Federal Reserve Bank of Chicago\f28 \uc2 \f2 's July National Activity Index, which showed the U.S. economic recovery regained some ground.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The index rose to zero from a revised -0.7 in June, a level that had been statistically consistent with the onset of a double-dip recession. The weak prior month pulled the three-month moving average down to -0.17 from -0.12.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also earlier, Federal Reserve Bank of Kansas City President Thomas Hoenig said the too-big-to-fail policies that have propped up the nation's largest banks are putting community banks, which are depended upon for credit in smaller communities, at a significant disadvantage.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hoenig, who has consistently opposed the ultra-loose monetary policy implemented by the Federal Reserve as it looks to stimulate economic growth, made his comments to a U.S. House panel.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Monday amid easing concerns over policy tightening after the China Securities Journal cited former PBOC adviser Li Yang as saying the country's macro policies will likely focus on preventing a further slowdown in economic growth given the unpromising global economic environment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Li's comment supported views that the government will allow the money market liquidity to remain in excess to bolster economic activity," a Shanghai-based trader with a local fund said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index may test 126.8 in coming sessions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds ended slightly lower Monday due to hedging ahead of Tuesday's 20-year auction and caution against surging JGB prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But "even if results of tomorrow's (Tuesday's) auction aren't so good, selling is unlikely to accelerate so much. Even if prices continue downward, the JGB market will likely manage to hold on" because of dip-buying, said Akito Fukunaga, chief rates strategist at RBS Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fukunaga tipped the benchmark 10-year cash JGB yield to trade in a range of 0.90%-0.98% this week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though recent sharp declines in superlong yields raise concern over demand at Tuesday's auction of Y1.1 trillion worth of 20-year JGBs, analysts think the tender will go generally well thanks to a broadening investor base and worries over slowing economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par H-P Tops Dell With 3Par Bid \par Mistake To View Housing As Investment, Hoenig Says \par Consortium Led By China's Hopu Studying Potash Bid \par BP Says $399M 